window.__ARXIV_DATA__ = {"papers":[{"id":"2610.28632v1","title":"Joints, Camera, Action! Co-Generating 3D Motion and Video for Arbitrary Rigs","abstract":"Video generation models can now depict remarkably realistic motion. Yet it is unclear how to use this motion beyond pixels: to animate a specific 3D asset and replay the animation in its 3D scene. For instance, a video model can show a monkey jumping over a crossbar, but it cannot return that jump as replayable 3D motion placed in the 3D scene. Here we present a model that directly co-generates the video and 3D motion for any supplied rig, along with a camera that registers this motion to the scene. Prior video-motion models either handle only human skeletons, often adding billions of parameters, or triangulate 3D motion from generated 2D pose maps. We instead keep 3D motion in a separate model that supports arbitrary skeletons, and connect it to a pretrained video model through a small set of cross-attention layers. This separation lets the motion model first learn how skeletons move from motion-only data, which is easier to obtain and augment than paired video. Additionally, our model learns to locate each joint in the video frames, which lets us recover the camera and place the 3D motion in the scene. To train across many skeletons, we use a generative video editor to place animated 3D assets in scenes suited to their actions, curating the edits so their known motion remains valid. Together with human and robot video, this yields nearly 200K training pairs. Our evaluations demonstrate that our model animates diverse rigs from text more faithfully than video-first pipelines, and its motion responds to the surrounding scene. It also turns video into rig motion, rig motion into video, and enables keyframe-based control of the 3D motion. Videos and additional results are available on our project page: https://research.nvidia.com/labs/par/projects/jca/.","authors":["Noam Rotstein","Yoad Tewel","Chen Tessler","Haggai Maron","Gal Chechik","Or Litany","Yoni Kasten","Yuval Atzmon"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:59:56Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:59:56Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28632v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28632v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28632v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":93581,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Video generation models can now depict remarkably realistic motion. Yet it is unclear how to use this motion beyond pixels: to animate a specific 3D asset and replay the animation in its 3D scene. For instance, a video model can show a monkey jumping over a c...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28631v1","title":"AEON: Unifying Video and 3D World Models","abstract":"Current world models for explicit world simulation typically follow two distinct pathways: (1) video generative models (e.g., Sora), which simulate realistic appearances but fail to preserve 3D consistency; and (2) 3D generative models (e.g., Marble), which generate geometrically consistent scenes but often yield static, less realistic observations. We present AEON, a unified framework that bridges video-based and 3D-based world models and combines the benefits of both. The key insight of AEON lies in a generative model that operates within the text- or image-conditioned video latent space while producing renderable 4D graphics primitives---specifically, 4D Gaussian Splatting (4DGS)---as the explicit world representation. We train a 4D reconstruction model to predict both 4DGS and camera poses from monocular videos, serving as our initialized 4D decoder. Furthermore, we design a domain adapter that translates generative video latents into reconstructive 4D latents through a distillation process on the video VAE. AEON naturally supports video-based world modeling by rendering 4DGS at predicted camera trajectories, and constructs a persistent 4D environment with scene dynamics for realistic world simulation. Consequently, AEON empowers a diverse range of world modeling applications and achieves state-of-the-art performance on standard benchmarks. Project Page: https://weiqi-zhang.github.io/AEON","authors":["Weiqi Zhang","Wenyuan Zhang","Yu-Shen Liu","Junsheng Zhou"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:59:51Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:59:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28631v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28631v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28631v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":70472,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Current world models for explicit world simulation typically follow two distinct pathways: (1) video generative models (e.g., Sora), which simulate realistic appearances but fail to preserve 3D consistency; and (2) 3D generative models (e.g., Marble), which g...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28630v1","title":"SolarRenderer: Few-Step Generative Reference Rendering","abstract":"Game engines and coding agents can specify the layout, motion, and camera of a scene, yet turning such coarse structure into realistic video requires a renderer that follows the structure and completes the appearance it leaves open. Existing renderers are tied to the representation of one engine, need tens of denoising steps or a costly distillation of their own, and learn from conditions that are not aligned with their target videos. We present SolarRenderer, a structure-conditioned video renderer that adapts a frozen MiniMax-H3 33B backbone with two lightweight LoRAs. Structural videos enter the native reference pathway of the backbone at the positions of the frames they describe, without a type identifier and described only in plain text, so that a single control LoRA follows arbitrary structural representations. Starting from a pretrained four-step LoRA, structure-guided distribution matching renders in four steps, 12.3 times faster than 50-step sampling, while preserving the learned control. A block-causal variant extends the same design to streaming and renders 768p video in real time. To train the model, we build a frame-aligned corpus of 136K clips from game engines, simulators, and YouTube videos. Experiments on an in-distribution test set and a generalization benchmark of 16 structural representations show that SolarRenderer achieves state-of-the-art results across both, and generalizes to 11 unseen representations, including pose skeletons, drawings, and point clouds.","authors":["Guian Fang","Shengju Qian","Dongyue Lu","Junchao Huang","Lutao Jiang","Xin Wang","Yiren Song","Li Jiang","Mike Zheng Shou"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:59:48Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:59:48Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28630v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28630v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28630v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":117035,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, agent, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Game engines and coding agents can specify the layout, motion, and camera of a scene, yet turning such coarse structure into realistic video requires a renderer that follows the structure and completes the appearance it leaves open. Existing renderers are tie...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28628v1","title":"Learning Adaptable Dexterous Skills via Hybrid Dexterous Mimicking","abstract":"Dexterous multi-finger hands hold promise to unlock diverse skills like functional grasps, in-hand reorientation, and dynamic handover that the human-designed world assumes, yet existing learning recipes force a trade-off. Goal-reaching reinforcement learning generalizes to arbitrary object poses but, lacking any human prior, converges to unnatural and unreliable finger strategies; motion tracking reproduces human-like behavior from a single clip but cannot deviate from it. We present AdaDex, a goal-conditioned RL framework that turns a single hand-object trajectory of a few seconds---from mocap data or HOI reconstruction from in-the-wild RGB(D) video---into an adaptable dexterous skill. At the core of AdaDex is Hybrid Dexterous Mimicking: a shared policy is trained on two populations of massively parallel environments: motion tracking environments trained with dense motion tracking objective, which instill the human prior from human motion; and generalization environments with noised initial states and goals and a sparse object-success reward, which force the skill to work far off the reference. From seconds of human motion per skill, the resulting policies can learn diverse adaptable dexterous skills, such as functional grasping, object reposing, in-hand reorientation with finger gaiting, and dynamic behaviors like throw-and-catch. Our method outperforms baselines in terms of generalization and functionality, and we demonstrate transfer across simulators and to the real world. Project page: https://yuxuank.com/AdaDex/.","authors":["Yuxuan Kuang","Jiashun Wang","Yixuan Sun","Yufei Ye","Sungjae Park","Umar Iqbal","Katerina Fragkiadaki","Shubham Tulsiani"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:59:32Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:59:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28628v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28628v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28628v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":76699,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Dexterous multi-finger hands hold promise to unlock diverse skills like functional grasps, in-hand reorientation, and dynamic handover that the human-designed world assumes, yet existing learning recipes force a trade-off. Goal-reaching reinforcement learning...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28626v1","title":"UniWALM: Unified World Action Language Model for Embodied Robot Control","abstract":"Motivated by the human ability to integrate experience across language, perception, and action, we introduce UniWALM, a unified world-action-language model that learns video, action, and language in a single shared representation, where each modality is both a conditioning signal and a supervision target. We ask: can jointly generating language enrich this shared representation, improving manipulation and supporting memory-dependent behavior? Unlike prior approaches that attach a reasoning or vision-language model to a video-action backbone and run them as distinct stages, UniWALM unifies all three modalities inside a single mixture-of-transformers backbone whose per-modality diffusion heads co-denoise synchronously, decoding subtask and textual memory in the same pass as action with no separate planning stage; the generated language is re-consumed on the next step, closing a language-as-memory loop without an external planner or memory bank. To keep the action stream reliable under this richer objective, we further introduce Flow Matching with Correction (FMC), a training objective that teaches recovery from perturbed action trajectories at no inference-time cost. UniWALM consistently improves over same-backbone baselines (e.g., FastWAM) on compositional pick-and-place (MolmoSpaces) and across the memory-dependent subset of RMBench, where it reaches 34.5% without any pretrained language or reasoning model and exceeds Mem-0's keyframe-equipped setup (28.5%) using only a single anchor frame. On a real-world Find-the-Item task, generating textual memory lifts success from 11.1% to 61.1%, while state-of-the-art VLAs stay at or below chance (<=33.3%).","authors":["Rahul Sajnani","Diego Romeres","Mingyuan Zhang","Kai Gao","Carl Qi","Baichuan Huang","Srinath Sridhar","Dylan Randle","Lillian J. Ratliff","Jose Barreiros","Manikantan Nambi","Jeroen Vanbaar"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:59:04Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:59:04Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28626v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28626v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28626v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":101637,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, representation, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Motivated by the human ability to integrate experience across language, perception, and action, we introduce UniWALM, a unified world-action-language model that learns video, action, and language in a single shared representation, where each modality is both...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28625v1","title":"PsycheMME: A Psychology-Informed Benchmark for Social Understanding and Interaction in Videos","abstract":"Understanding human needs and selecting responses require distinct judgments that aggregate accuracy can obscure. We introduce PsycheMME, a video benchmark informed by psychology, with eight capabilities spanning grounding, states, needs, interaction decisions, memory, and risk. Drawing on Social Information Processing and the Perception-Action Cycle, this organization separates human understanding from response selection. We construct 1,000 questions from 630 episodes through independent answering with proposed references withheld and expert review. Our Reasoning-Verified protocol weights answer credit by the support and logical consistency of explicit rationales against annotated cues. Across 17 models, Timing has the lowest Verified scores and credit retention, with weaker support judgments for correct Timing answers. Models with similar Need scores still differ in answer completeness on open questions. Input controls and SFT/GRPO examine context dependence and adaptation. Evaluating answers alongside rationales locates where models fail across understanding, response selection, and justification.","authors":["Heran Hu","Yuhang Dong","Zhucun Xue","Kaiwei Chen","Yong Liu","Jiangning Zhang"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:59:00Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:59:00Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28625v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28625v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28625v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":110610,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Understanding human needs and selecting responses require distinct judgments that aggregate accuracy can obscure. We introduce PsycheMME, a video benchmark informed by psychology, with eight capabilities spanning grounding, states, needs, interaction decision...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28615v1","title":"KoalaGS: Complexity-Aware Continuous Level of Detail for Gaussian Splatting","abstract":"Deploying a trained 3D Gaussian Splatting scene under shrinking compute budgets requires one primitive set to remain effective when rendered at smaller prefixes. In this continuous level of detail (LoD) setting, the challenge is to decide which Gaussians should retain capacity as the budget decreases. A pilot study on 3DGS-MCMC reconstructions shows that image complexity, computed from the input views before optimization, predicts where reconstruction error concentrates in image and 3D space. It also reveals that simple regions contain too many Gaussians and degrade as much as complex ones at lower budgets, despite being inherently cheaper to represent. Subsequently, in KoalaGS, during training we use this complexity prior to bias relocation toward poorly reconstructed areas while ensuring smooth degradation across LoDs. A primitive importance score then enables inference at low-capacity, while preserving the scene structure. Tested across three datasets, KoalaGS maintains competitive full-capacity quality while significantly improving quality across the budget spectrum, yielding smoother transitions across LoDs, and enabling faster progressive streaming.","authors":["Radu Beche","Raoul de Charette"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:55:06Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:55:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28615v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28615v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28615v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":82161,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Deploying a trained 3D Gaussian Splatting scene under shrinking compute budgets requires one primitive set to remain effective when rendered at smaller prefixes. In this continuous level of detail (LoD) setting, the challenge is to decide which Gaussians shou...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28598v1","title":"Neural Implicit Energy Optimization for LiDAR Mapping","abstract":"Neural implicit representations provide memory-efficient signed distance field (SDF) parameterizations for large-scale LiDAR scenes, but the geometry recovered from sparse and often noisy inputs carries jitter and artifacts. Classical geometric optimization techniques resolve such artifacts with surface energy functionals, whose minimizers correspond to smooth surfaces. Motivated by this principle, we formulate neural LiDAR mapping as the optimization of energy functionals over the parameter space of neural implicit representations. Specifically, we regularize the signed distance field with an energy functional defined on the shape operator of its level set, which represents the curvature of the surface, thus encouraging smoothness along the surface. This adapts classical geometric priors to the scale of million-point LiDAR maps. Experiments on large-scale datasets show reconstructions with improved smoothness at preserved geometric fidelity, and no additional inference-time cost. The code is publicly available at \\href{https://github.com/HrishikeshVish/nieo}{https://github.com/HrishikeshVish/nieo}","authors":["Hrishikesh Viswanath","Md Ashiqur Rahman","Chi Lin","Damon Conover","Aniket Bera"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:47:07Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:47:07Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28598v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28598v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28598v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":77635,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Neural implicit representations provide memory-efficient signed distance field (SDF) parameterizations for large-scale LiDAR scenes, but the geometry recovered from sparse and often noisy inputs carries jitter and artifacts. Classical geometric optimization t...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28594v1","title":"Learning the 4D World through Point Trajectory Forecasting","abstract":"Reliable multi-view forecasting requires videos from different cameras to describe the same evolving 3D scene. To this end, we introduce Video-Point Model (VPM), a world model that conditions on multi-view video histories and a language instruction to jointly forecast future videos from arbitrary viewpoints and the 3D trajectories of persistent scene points. We extend a pretrained world model with camera-conditioned multi-view processing that integrates complementary observations across cameras, and add a trajectory head that predicts how physical points move through time. Each trajectory tracks a single point across all views, and supervising these trajectories alongside video generation therefore anchors appearance changes in every camera to a shared description of 3D motion. To support training and evaluation, we build VPM-Data, a simulation corpus pairing synchronized multi-view videos with persistent point trajectories, and VP-WorldBench, a forecasting benchmark spanning simulated and real environments. We further extend VPM to robot control by adding robot actions as a token modality in the generator. Action tokens share the point-supervised backbone and are denoised jointly with future videos, thereby grounding the policy in the same 3D-aware representation. On VP-WorldBench and existing 4D generation benchmarks, point supervision improves both video fidelity and 3D motion accuracy over a video-only counterpart, and in simulated and real-robot experiments, it also raises the manipulation success of the action-enabled model.","authors":["Byungwoo Jeon","Sunghwan Hong","Dohyeon Kim","Jonghoon Lee","Jungwoo Park","Inhee Lee","Doosan Baek","Jinil Kim","Taehyun Lim","Hanbyul Joo","Marc Pollefeys","Saining Xie","Jinwoo Shin"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:45:45Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:45:45Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28594v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28594v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28594v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":142071,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Reliable multi-view forecasting requires videos from different cameras to describe the same evolving 3D scene. To this end, we introduce Video-Point Model (VPM), a world model that conditions on multi-view video histories and a language instruction to jointly...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28585v1","title":"SceneRig: An Agentic System for Simulation-Ready 3D Scene Reconstruction from Single Images","abstract":"Simulation is a powerful tool for robotics, but building diverse, simulation-ready scenes at scale remains difficult. Reconstructing scenes from single images is a promising alternative, but visual similarity alone is insufficient: objects must also be recovered, accurately placed, and physically grounded. Existing methods that rely on post-hoc physics settling often misplace objects, producing scenes that look correct but behave poorly under manipulation. We present SceneRig, an agentic system that turns a single image into an editable, simulation-ready scene. SceneRig combines geometric, visual, and physical tools to reconstruct a faithful 3D scene. An agent then performs edit-and-validate loops inside a physics simulator to achieve physical stability. Across diverse inputs, SceneRig improves instance segmentation, object-centric appearance, and 3D placement while keeping the reconstructed scenes physically stable. Furthermore, SceneRig is effective for downstream robotics: its scenes predict real-robot policy outcomes more accurately than the baseline method and more reliably support replay of recorded manipulations without trajectory adaptation.","authors":["Rundong Luo","Yunong Liu","Di Cao","Matthew Tancik","Shyamal Buch","Colton Stearns","Wenqi Xian"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:39:42Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:39:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28585v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28585v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28585v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent","instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":138992,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d, agent, instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Simulation is a powerful tool for robotics, but building diverse, simulation-ready scenes at scale remains difficult. Reconstructing scenes from single images is a promising alternative, but visual similarity alone is insufficient: objects must also be recove...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28567v1","title":"Continual Concept Personalization in Diffusion Models via Residual-Space Adaptation","abstract":"Text-to-image personalization aims to equip pretrained diffusion models with the ability to learn user-specific concepts from only a few reference images. Existing methods, however, typically train each concept independently or assume that all concepts are available simultaneously. In practical applications, concepts often arrive sequentially, requiring models to continually learn new subjects, objects, or styles while minimizing interference with previously acquired concepts. We address this continual concept personalization setting by extending residual-space adaptation to the stochastic adaptation process of pretrained diffusion models. Our work achieves the highest concept fidelity among competing continual personalization methods while maintaining near-zero forgetting. Beyond these empirical gains, the learned residual-space dynamics reveal where adaptation capacity is concentrated within the diffusion model. Restricting adaptation to the identified layers by our method preserves nearly the same performance while reducing the number of trainable parameters by up to 72\\%, demonstrating that residual-space dynamics can identify the layers that contribute most to continual concept personalization and directly guide more parameter-efficient adaptation.","authors":["Yuzhi Liu","Enis Simsar","Giulia Lanzillotta","Thomas Hofmann"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:26:18Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:26:18Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28567v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28567v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28567v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":64881,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Text-to-image personalization aims to equip pretrained diffusion models with the ability to learn user-specific concepts from only a few reference images. Existing methods, however, typically train each concept independently or assume that all concepts are av...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28554v1","title":"OverSeer: Multi-Agent World Models Beyond What Agents See","abstract":"Despite the success of video world models, extending them to multiple agents is difficult because it requires generating several agent-specific video streams that are consistent in time and between agents. To address this gap, we introduce OverSeer, a multi-agent world model that augments egocentric agent views with auxiliary camera views. We suggest that, by fixing the auxiliary views in space, they act as a proxy for a world-centric representation: Unlike the agents' views, which can change entirely due to a simple change in agent position, changes in the auxiliary views directly map to localized changes in the world state. We show that these fixed views substantially help the model to maintain consistency. We also show that, due to their redundant and semi-static nature, the auxiliary views can be efficiently compressed with a delta-VAE that encodes only the changes relative to a static reference image, reducing the number of tokens required to process them. Experiments in Minecraft and Doom demonstrate improved world and agent consistency, with qualitative results on real-world multi-camera captures indicating that the approach is not tied to game engines. Our compression analysis further shows that delta-VAE substantially reduces the computational overhead of auxiliary-view generation. We conclude that these fixed views provide a simple and efficient way to improve multi-agent world models.","authors":["Runjia Li","Kam Woh Ng","Minghao Chen","Junlin Han","Jianyuan Wang","Zhaochong An","Juan-Manuel Perez-Rua","Andrea Vedaldi","Shikun Liu"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:17:59Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:17:59Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28554v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28554v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28554v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":72846,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, agent, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Despite the success of video world models, extending them to multiple agents is difficult because it requires generating several agent-specific video streams that are consistent in time and between agents. To address this gap, we introduce OverSeer, a multi-a...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28530v1","title":"CoGaussian: Open-Vocabulary Gaussian-based Collaborative 3D Scene Understanding","abstract":"Collaborative perception enables agents to retrieve the scene semantics and geometry beyond the field of view of any individual agent. This broader scene understanding enhances transportation safety and improves efficiency in multi-robot systems. However, existing collaborative semantic occupancy methods rely on dense 3D voxel annotations and are limited to recognizing objects within a predefined set of semantic classes. To overcome these limitations, we present CoGaussian, an open-vocabulary Gaussian-based collaborative semantic occupancy prediction framework. CoGaussian represents a scene as a set of foundation model-aligned sparse 3D Gaussians, which serve as a compact representation for communicating and fusing geometric and semantic information across multiple agents. On the Semantic-OPV2V benchmark, our method outperforms existing open-vocabulary single-agent approaches, highlighting the effectiveness of open-vocabulary collaborative scene understanding.","authors":["Om Shivam Verma","Shaya Farahmand","Lingjun Zhao","Lu Gan"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:04:38Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:04:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28530v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28530v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28530v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":26494,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d, agent, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Collaborative perception enables agents to retrieve the scene semantics and geometry beyond the field of view of any individual agent. This broader scene understanding enhances transportation safety and improves efficiency in multi-robot systems. However, exi...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:23+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28524v1","title":"Copyright Leakage Detection in AI Generated Videos","abstract":"Video generation models can create realistic-looking video footage from images and text prompts. Some of the recent advances have raised concerns about the reproduction of copyrighted visual content. However, copyright leakage detection in generated videos remains largely unexplored. In this paper, we introduce a new benchmark data set for copyright leakage detection, comprising 50 copyrighted concepts, each with 5 images. For each concept, we have 5 videos created using the image+prompt video generation model and the prompt-only video generation model. Following that, we develop an AI toolset based on a contrastive retrieval framework that formulates copyright leakage detection as a video-to-image retrieval problem by matching aggregated video frame embeddings against a database of copyrighted image embeddings. Our method uses a single similarity threshold and retrieves the most similar copyrighted reference as supporting evidence. We conducted experiments showing strong retrieval and detection performance, achieving 99.6\\% detection accuracy with only a single false negative. These results show the effectiveness of the retrieval-based method for scalable and explainable copyright leakage detection in AI-generated videos. Data can be accessed publicly from https://zenodo.org/records/21386117 and the source code for this project is publicly available at https://github.com/Shaif95/video-cp-Leakage.","authors":["Shaif Chowdhury","Moshiur Rahman Tonmoy"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:59:22Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:59:22Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28524v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28524v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28524v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","retrieval"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":42557,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, retrieval 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Video generation models can create realistic-looking video footage from images and text prompts. Some of the recent advances have raised concerns about the reproduction of copyrighted visual content. However, copyright leakage detection in generated videos re...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:23+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28521v1","title":"Latent Bridge: From Video Latents to 3D Geometry via Feature Alignment","abstract":"Video generation and feed-forward 3D reconstruction typically use separate representations, requiring RGB decoding and visual re-encoding to recover geometry from generated latents. We propose Latent Bridge, which connects video generative latent spaces with geometric feature spaces to directly predict 3D geometry from video latents. We develop representation-specific bridges for Wan2.1 and V-RAE, enabling feed-forward prediction of cameras, depth, and 3D point clouds without the intermediate RGB pathway. Each bridge is trained on RGB videos through feature distillation, while the video encoder and geometry model remain frozen. Training requires neither ground-truth geometry nor execution of the downstream geometric aggregator or prediction heads. At inference, the same video latents support both RGB decoding and geometry prediction. The same trained bridge can be reused for image-to-video and text-to-video generation within the same video latent space, without bridge retraining. Experiments on Co3Dv2 and WildRGB-D demonstrate competitive geometric reconstruction quality, efficient end-to-end generation and reconstruction, and faster latent-to-geometry decoding, highlighting latent alignment as a practical and reusable interface between video generation and 3D reconstruction. Our code is available at: https://github.com/hustvl/Latent-Bridge.","authors":["Shuai Zhang","Zhanpeng Ouyang","Zhengqing Chen","Ruohong Mei","Shaoyu Chen","Weiqiang Ren","Hui Sun","Qian Zhang","Wenyu Liu","Xinggang Wang"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:57:49Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:57:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28521v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28521v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28521v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":60080,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Video generation and feed-forward 3D reconstruction typically use separate representations, requiring RGB decoding and visual re-encoding to recover geometry from generated latents. We propose Latent Bridge, which connects video generative latent spaces with...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:23+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28519v1","title":"Touch-JEPA: Tactile Representation Learning from Hand-Object Video with Limited Tactile Supervision","abstract":"Understanding contact and tactile magnitude in hand-object interaction is a key step toward physically intelligent systems, yet learning such representations is limited by the cost and restricted coverage of tactile measurements. We present Touch-JEPA, a framework for learning tactile representations from hand-object videos with limited tactile supervision. We first introduce a self-supervised pretraining objective that learns from video alone without tactile supervision. Using video-derived hand geometry, Touch-JEPA predicts masked visual features at corresponding hand-surface locations from spatial and temporal context, encouraging the representation to capture latent contact dynamics. We then extend this formulation to joint representation learning, where masked prediction is applied to all videos while tactile supervision is available only for a labeled subset, grounding the shared representation in measured contact and magnitude. With self-supervised pretraining alone, simple supervised readouts on frozen Touch-JEPA features outperform supervised baselines in contact discrimination and tactile magnitude estimation across three human-hand benchmarks, and transfer to robotic hands without robot tactile training data. Joint learning with limited tactile supervision further improves contact and magnitude estimation on the human-hand benchmarks. Together, these results show that hand-object video can provide scalable self-supervision for tactile representation learning, while limited tactile measurements provide complementary physical grounding.","authors":["Minseong Kweon","Qiao Feng","Yiming Huang","Lingjie Liu"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:56:11Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:56:11Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28519v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28519v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28519v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":100625,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Understanding contact and tactile magnitude in hand-object interaction is a key step toward physically intelligent systems, yet learning such representations is limited by the cost and restricted coverage of tactile measurements. We present Touch-JEPA, a fram...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:23+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28508v1","title":"Deep Learning-Based Road Lane Quality Assessment from Egocentric Dashcam Video","abstract":"Reliable lane markings are important for both human drivers and automated driving systems, but their condition is still commonly assessed through manual inspection. Existing autonomous-driving datasets primarily support lane detection and scene understanding and usually lack maintenance-oriented labels for lane-marking visibility, continuity, or degradation. In this study, we present a geo-referenced smartphone dashcam dataset and baseline framework for lane-marking quality assessment. The dataset contains 63,839 road-facing frames collected across South Texas using a GPS-enabled dashcam application. A subset of 3,339 frames is matched with OpenStreetMap-derived lane-count labels, which are used as weak supervision for lane-count regression and map-consistency evaluation. We propose three lane-quality measures: Contrast Stability Index (CSI), which captures lane-background contrast using Laplacian responses; Line Continuity Ratio (LCR), which estimates structural continuity using edge and Hough-line evidence; and Regression Lane Confidence Score (RLCS), which measures agreement between predicted and map-derived lane counts on matched frames. The baseline prediction models attained strong performance, with CSI (MAE $0.039$, $R^2=0.97$) and LCR (MAE $0.093$, $R^2=0.84$). Notably, CSI and LCR are no-reference, segmentation-derived quality metrics that can be computed without dense human labels. In a pilot validation using 50 human-ranked images, these proposed lane-specific metrics show stronger rank agreement with human lane-quality ordering (e.g., Spearman's $ρ=0.7316$ and Kendall's $τ=0.5472$ for LCR) than general image-quality measures such as PSNR, SSIM, BRISQUE, NIQE, and LPIPS. We believe the dataset, metrics, and baseline provide an initial step toward scalable, low-cost, location-aware lane-marking quality assessment. Data and code: https://github.com/Shaif95/Road_Quality","authors":["Shaif Chowdhury","Akhila Yeruvasu","Moshiur Rahman Tonmoy","Ayush Goyal","Dunren Che"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:46:32Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:46:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28508v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28508v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28508v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":42556,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Reliable lane markings are important for both human drivers and automated driving systems, but their condition is still commonly assessed through manual inspection. Existing autonomous-driving datasets primarily support lane detection and scene understanding...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:23+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28502v1","title":"GazeLens for Efficient Egocentric Video Question Answering with Simple Gaze-Driven Adaptive Token Reduction","abstract":"Smart glasses can help users understand their activities and surroundings, but small vision-language models must preserve useful evidence within limited computation. This is difficult because redundant frames consume the visual budget, while aggressive spatial compression can discard relevant details. Although gaze identifies the wearer's focus, the question may require evidence from the surrounding scene. To address these constraints, we propose GazeLens, which combines frame selection with gaze- and question-guided token reduction. First, HOG transitions and image sharpness select frames before visual encoding. For each selected frame, Gaussian pooling then forms gaze-focused and complementary summaries. Because their relevance depends on the question, a lightweight gate blends them into a primary token. Together with a global scene token, this retains both focused and contextual summaries within two tokens per frame. On StreamGaze, GazeLens achieves higher macro and micro accuracy than the evaluated full-grid and token-reduction baselines, with lower input-forward FLOPs than the shared-backbone token-reduction baselines.","authors":["Trong-Thang Pham","Anh Nguyen","Hien Van Nguyen","Ngan Le"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:43:06Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:43:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28502v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28502v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28502v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":57216,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Smart glasses can help users understand their activities and surroundings, but small vision-language models must preserve useful evidence within limited computation. This is difficult because redundant frames consume the visual budget, while aggressive spatia...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:23+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28490v1","title":"Learning from Automatic 3D Gaussian Splatting Edits for Online Bayesian Scene Change Detection","abstract":"Online scene change detection requires integrating incoming observations to determine which regions have changed relative to a reference scene. We update change beliefs as new images arrive using generalised Bayesian evidence fusion. However, the prior probability of change and evidence models are unknown beforehand, and numerical Bayesian inference can be costly. We address these challenges by learning offline from automatic edits to a reference 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) model. These edits generate labelled training views without manual annotations. Together with unchanged reference observations, they provide supervision for learning scene-specific evidence models and estimating the effective pixel prior and response statistics. Online prediction combines current evidence with historical support, while closed-form local Bayes factors, parameterized by the learned response statistics, update an accumulated 3D Gaussian field. Object-guided refinement produces the final change masks. Our method achieves state-of-the-art accuracy on PASLCD and CCS3D, with $67.64\\%$ and $75.82\\%$ mIoU, respectively. On PASLCD, the complete pipeline runs at $17.2$\\,FPS over complete query sequences, the fastest among the evaluated online methods.","authors":["Changrui Zhu","Simon Julier"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:36:39Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:36:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28490v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28490v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28490v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":130477,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Online scene change detection requires integrating incoming observations to determine which regions have changed relative to a reference scene. We update change beliefs as new images arrive using generalised Bayesian evidence fusion. However, the prior probab...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:23+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28476v1","title":"CLOSER: Evidence-Aware Active Visual Verification for Embodied Object-Goal Navigation","abstract":"Object-goal navigation (ObjectNav) requires an embodied agent to find an instance of a specified category in an unseen environment and stop near it. The agent typically relies on an object detector but must judge online from a limited egocentric video stream, sometimes a single frame, whether a detected candidate is the target. Detectors often confidently mistake similar objects, such as a sofa for a bed, and stopping at a false positive fails the episode. Prevailing systems also approach candidates greedily, leaving them occluded or partially observed. We present CLOSER, an evidence-aware active verification framework that treats stopping as closed-loop evidence acquisition, coupling vision-language model (VLM) verification with active perception. CLOSER fine-tunes an open-source VLM to label each view of a candidate tracked across frames as target, non-target, or uncertain, selects complementary views with an embedding-based diversity gate, and fuses their per-view probabilities with a lightweight network. If the fused judgment remains uncertain, a diffusion policy drives the agent toward the candidate using only the tracked mask and depth until the decision becomes decisive. CLOSER runs locally and plugs into existing ObjectNav systems without retraining them. On Gibson and Habitat-Matterport 3D, it raises average success rate by 3.6 points and success weighted by path length by 2.0 points, showing that verifying evidence before committing improves embodied navigation.","authors":["Pengying Wu","Mingye Jiang","Yunze Hu","Zhejia Wen","Kangjie Zhou","Zongru Yang","Zhe Peng","Chang Liu"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:27:33Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:27:33Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28476v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28476v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28476v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","agent","instance","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":76123,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, agent, instance, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Object-goal navigation (ObjectNav) requires an embodied agent to find an instance of a specified category in an unseen environment and stop near it. The agent typically relies on an object detector but must judge online from a limited egocentric video stream,...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:23+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28458v1","title":"LocaLeJEPA: Maximum Entropy Representations under Geometric Priors","abstract":"Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPA) offer an appealing approach to self-supervised learning, but are typically prone to representation collapse. LeJEPA resolves this by introducing a theoretically grounded distribution-matching objective that drives the embedding towards an isotropic Gaussian. However, this approach is fundamentally tied to Euclidean assumptions, and its global target does not reflect domain-specific geometric priors, such as the use of non-Euclidean embedding geometries, e.g., equivariant CNNs or hyperbolic ViTs. We thus introduce LocaLeJEPA, a framework motivated by a manifold-theoretic reformulation of LeJEPA's entropy-maximization property. The accompanying LocaLeReg, a novel locally Euclidean distribution-matching regularizer, enables a stable and scalable implementation of the LocaLeJEPA framework. We then perform extensive experiments in the vision setting on models trained on ImageNet-1K. LocaLeJEPA significantly outperforms LeJEPA when paired with hyperbolic ViT-S/8 and equivariant ResNet-50 backbones, and also with ordinary Euclidean ViT-S/8 and ViT-L/14 backbones. This holds both in terms of linear and k-NN classification performance on ImageNet and on a series of downstream tasks tested. We view the LocaLeJEPA formalism as a principled approach to the JEPA paradigm that better respects intrinsic data geometry while also accommodating generalizations to exotic embedding geometries. Code available at https://github.com/alessioborgi/LocaLeJEPA.","authors":["Kartik Tandon","Alessio Borgi","Pavel Snopov","Inés García-Redondo","Davide D'Ascenzo","Sebastiano Cultrera di Montesano","Fabrizio Silvestri","Pietro Liò","Francesca Dominici","Randall Balestriero","Claudio Battiloro"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:15:06Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:15:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28458v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28458v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28458v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28444v1","title":"EvaOmni: An Evidence-Grounded Benchmark for Audio-Visual Reasoning","abstract":"Omni LLMs can naturally locate key audio and visual evidence and integrate information across modalities for audiovisual reasoning. Existing audio-visual reasoning benchmarks that mainly rely on multiple-choice questions usually score models solely based on answer correctness, making it difficult to assess whether a model truly forms a reasoning chain from evidence to the correct answer. In addition, textual or unimodal shortcuts may allow models to answer correctly without analyzing the necessary audio-visual evidence, further weakening the reliability of evaluation benchmarks. To address these issues, we introduce EvaOmni, an evidence-grounded benchmark containing 392 videos and 1,027 purely human-annotated question-answer pairs, covering 15 capabilities and three levels: retrieval and localization, basic understanding, and complex reasoning. We design a series of rigorous annotation procedures in which experts annotate audio and visual evidence and construct video-based distractors; humans review whether questions meet strict inspection criteria, and finally models filter textual and unimodal shortcuts. Beyond high-quality question-answer pairs, we require models to simultaneously return the answer and supporting evidence intervals in order to evaluate the complete reasoning chain. We propose the Coverage Grounded Score (C.G.) to measure how much annotated evidence a model identifies, and the Bottleneck Grounded Score (B.G.) to penalize poor temporal localization in either modality; both are incorporated into answer correctness. Across 7 open-weight and 17 proprietary Omni LLMs, Gemini3.7-Flash achieved the highest overall accuracy (79.4%), C.G. (70.8%), and B.G. (35.1%). At Level 3, it reached 79.1% accuracy, but its B.G. was only 12.3%. These results show that even the most advanced models still have room for development in grounded audio-visual reasoning.","authors":["Xiaoxing Hu","Wenxi Yao","Chaoyuan Shi","Ziqi Ye","Xiaorong Guo","Peixuan Li","Zhuoran Sun","Ziyang Gong","Peiyuan Zhang","Mingxin Liu","Shuran Ma","Hongbo Liu","Xiaomin Yu","Yu Tian","Wentao Wang","Zhenjie Yang","Jian Hu","Yunhang Shen","Haoyu Cao","Xue Yang"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:03:30Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:03:30Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28444v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28444v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28444v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","retrieval"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28423v1","title":"WorldSR: Harnessing World Knowledge Search for Grounded Image Super-Resolution","abstract":"Generative-prior-based super-resolution (SR) methods leverage implicit knowledge learned from large-scale models to enhance low-resolution inputs. However, such priors are inherently stochastic and optimized for diverse generation, conflicting with the deterministic, high-fidelity reconstruction required for SR. It is also tied to the base model, costly to update, and prone to hallucinations as real-world data evolves. To address these limitations, we introduce WorldSR, a novel framework that augments SR with explicit, entity-aligned visual world knowledge. WorldSR automatically decomposes the input into semantic entities and performs entity-wise retrieval of structurally and semantically aligned images from a large-scale external corpus. For efficiency and reliability, we incorporate a memory mechanism to reuse previously retrieved results and a multi-step filtering process to refine candidate references. The selected references as world knowledge are then integrated through a novel multi-reference super-resolution model, which provides fine-grained constraints for detail synthesis and structural recovery. Experiments on real-world SR benchmarks show that WorldSR consistently improves structural fidelity and visual quality, outperforming existing generative super-resolution methods. Our results suggest that entity-level grounding in external visual world knowledge provides a simple and effective complement to model-bound generative priors.","authors":["Junyuan Deng","Xinyi Wu","Zhenyao Wu","Song Wang","Yu Wang","Congchao Zhu"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:54:25Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:54:25Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28423v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28423v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28423v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28419v1","title":"DISTRACT: Degradation-Indexed Study of Textual Reference Across Competing Targets","abstract":"Vision-language trackers (VLTs) accept descriptions intended to assist tracking, but accepting text does not establish that its content changes tracking decisions, particularly as visual evidence deteriorates. We introduce DISTRACT, a matched-intervention benchmark that holds video, target, and distractor fixed while crossing four text priors with four image degradations along a controlled severity axis. We evaluate five VLTs on four datasets using behavioral contrasts, layerwise representation probes, and activation patching. Molmo2 and JointNLT exhibit directed text use. Some effects contract under degradation, whereas others show no significant endpoint change after multiplicity correction. UVLTrack, MMTrack, and DUTrack respond only weakly to changes in text semantics, with no directed behavioral effect surviving FDR correction in the tested settings. Model-internal analyses reveal that at decision-near states, correct-versus-conflicting text is decodable in Molmo2 and JointNLT but not in the other three systems. In the responsive models, decodability peaks at late states where replacement produces little behavioral change, whereas replacing selected earlier states redirects conflict-associated trajectories toward the target. Because matched-correct donors do not significantly outperform unrelated donors, these interventions identify earlier intervention-sensitive states without establishing target-specific semantic rescue. DISTRACT thus exposes when and where text affects tracking under degraded visual evidence.","authors":["Dan Zlotnikov","Ohad Ben-Shahar"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:53:23Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:53:23Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28419v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28419v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28419v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28416v1","title":"Code2Video-Compass: A Comprehensive Benchmark for General-Purpose Code-Based Video Generation across T2V, I2V, and V2V","abstract":"Code-based video generation offers fine-grained control, targeted editing, and component reuse compared with direct video generation. This approach requires multimodal large language models (MLLMs) to translate visual and temporal requirements into executable programs that produce complete videos. However, existing code-based benchmarks primarily target specialized tasks, leaving complete video creation across diverse creative categories and input modalities insufficiently evaluated. We introduce Code2Video-Compass, a general-purpose benchmark for code-based video generation across text-to-video (T2V), image-to-video (I2V), and video-to-video (V2V) settings. Code2Video-Compass comprises 2,104 tasks spanning 10 categories, 24 subtypes, and three complexity dimensions covering construction, dynamic animation, and state dependency. It combines tasks curated from textual instructions and visual references with corresponding T2V, I2V, and V2V tasks derived from complete professional and user-created videos. We propose a dual-level evaluation framework combining 12 objective metrics and five subjective metrics. Objective evaluation covers operational performance, video quality, text-video alignment, and reference fidelity, while subjective evaluation uses an MLLM judge to evaluate instruction following and holistic video quality. Extensive evaluation of 15 recent proprietary and open-weight MLLMs from nine families reveals that even leading models face challenges in reliably generating high-quality complete videos aligned with textual and visual requirements, with performance generally declining as construction, dynamic animation, and state-dependency complexity increases. We anticipate that Code2Video-Compass will advance reliable, coherent, and controllable code-based video generation and inspire broader progress in programmatic visual creation.","authors":["Zhenghan Chen","Zekai Shao","Xin Lin","Leting Cheng","Siming Chen"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:51:41Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:51:41Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28416v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28416v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28416v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28411v1","title":"Brain2Vec: A Self-Supervised 3D Foundation Model for Structural Brain MRI","abstract":"Deep learning models have become essential tools in medical imaging, yet training them end-to-end for diverse tasks remains challenging due to limited labeled data and computational constraints. To address this, we introduce Brain2Vec, a foundation model pre-trained on 74,425 T1-weighted Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) scans pooled from multiple studies. Brain2Vec can serve both as a weight initialization backbone for fine-tuning downstream tasks and as a standalone feature extractor. We evaluated Brain2Vec in three tasks: downstream predictive performance, convergence speed, and embedding quality. Using Brain2Vec's pretrained weights as initialization and fine-tuning on labeled data for a brain age prediction task, results demonstrate improved final accuracy and faster convergence compared to random initialization. Additionally, its frozen embeddings outperform anatomically defined volumetric features on four of five downstream classification tasks and perform competitively with a state-of-the-art foundation model pre-trained on substantially more data. Lastly, we show that Brain2Vec encodes information primarily from a spatially localized and neuroanatomically meaningful set of brain regions, and that manipulating a subset of latent features produces monotonic, region-specific effects on the reconstructed anatomy. Brain2Vec code and trained models are available as an open-source package on https://github.com/CBICA/Brain2Vec. Users can apply Brain2Vec locally by installing the package, or directly through the NiChart cloud platform https://cloud.neuroimagingchart.com, where Brain2Vec features can be derived without requiring local installation or specialized infrastructure.","authors":["Spiros Maggioros","Guray Erus","Gareth Harman","George Aidinis","Alexander Getka","Aristeidis Sotiras","Christos Davatzikos"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:50:35Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:50:35Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28411v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28411v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28411v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28410v1","title":"A Multi-Agent Framework for Automated Machine Learning with Evidence-based Planning for Medical Image Analysis","abstract":"Deep learning has become the default tool for medical image analysis, driven by the open-source movement. However, achieving the best performance on a given task remains difficult, with architecture selection and hyperparameter optimization (HPO) still considered \"more of an art than a science\". Automated machine learning (AutoML) automates parts of model training, yet helps little in medical imaging: general-purpose approaches lack medical architectures and preprocessing, and medical-focused ones demand engineering expertise and remain confined to narrow tasks such as 3D segmentation. We introduce MedicalMLOmni, a multi-agent AutoML framework that automates model training for diverse single- and multi-modal medical tasks through a natural language interface. Specifically, MedicalMLOmni performs evidence-based planning by retrieving published results to guide model selection and configuration, and employs parallel HPO where multiple agents explore adjustment strategies concurrently. MedicalMLOmni achieves the best score on 19 of 20 benchmarks; PMC-VQA is the exception, where a vision-language model with broader pre-training leads. Backbone-matched controls attribute the gains to the framework, not the backbone. By removing coding requirements, MedicalMLOmni lowers the barrier for domain experts with rich datasets but limited engineering expertise.","authors":["Yiqing Shen","Mathias Unberath"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:49:38Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:49:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28410v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28410v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28410v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28402v1","title":"StreamEval: Evaluating Interactive Streaming Video Generation Models","abstract":"As streaming video generation advances, enabling real-time interaction with generated video is becoming increasingly important. We introduce StreamEval, a unified benchmark for interactive streaming video generation spanning five tasks: avatar dialogue, avatar performance, world generation, instruction editing, and reference editing. StreamEval comprises 300 scripted test cases lasting 15-300s, each specifying initial assets, incrementally arriving multimodal requests, and explicit requirements for state changes and content preservation. Performance is assessed along four evaluation dimensions: task execution, state consistency, visual fidelity, and audiovisual coordination, instantiated through ten task-specific metrics on a 0-100 scale. Evaluating 37 distinct models across 300 test cases, we find that high visual quality and identity retention do not necessarily coincide with reliable request execution or sustained state consistency. In particular, request execution remains challenging: the highest task-specific scores are 36.90 for Following among seven avatar-performance models, 25.93 for Following in text-conditioned world generation, and 15.00 for Edit Success among three reported reference-editing models. These findings identify reliable request execution under timing and preservation constraints as a central challenge for advancing omni-modal real-time interactive systems. The leaderboard is available at https://streameval.ai","authors":["StreamEval Team"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:47:43Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:47:43Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28402v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28402v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28402v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28382v1","title":"RACE: Learning Multimodal Search through Referential Abstraction and Compositional Evidence","abstract":"Training multimodal search agents requires tasks that make visually grounded search necessary for determining the answer. Textual identity clues and unused target evidence allow shortcuts in synthetic questions, weakening supervision for visual search and evidence integration. We propose RACE, an image-conditioned task synthesis framework that constructs dependencies from images to targets and from target-specific evidence to answers. Referential Abstraction (RA) replaces identity-revealing text with specific visual references or ambiguous relational references resolved by searching among depicted candidates. Compositional Evidence (CE) links retrieved facts about multiple entities in one image or entities from different images into joint questions whose answers require every designated contribution. Both operations also apply to images found during search, allowing new visual observations to determine subsequent search targets or supply answer evidence. Fine-tuning Qwen3.5-27B on only 12K teacher-generated trajectories from RACE raises average accuracy across ten multimodal benchmarks from 53.5 to 56.0, a relative improvement of approximately 4.7% over the matched base agent. Our code, data, and model will be publicly available.","authors":["Eric Tao","Minhao Sun","Liyan Liu","Zhijing Wu","Xiaohan Yu","Lang Mei","Li Huang","Chao Feng","Jinchao Ma","Sichen Kang","Siyu Mo","Chong Chen"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:33:24Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:33:24Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28382v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28382v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28382v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28356v1","title":"CountVideoBench: A Benchmark to Evaluate Prediction Biases on the Object Counting task by Video-Language Models","abstract":"Object counting in videos remains a poorly characterized capability of multimodal large language models. Counting questions are included in many existing VideoQA benchmarks, but the ground-truth count distributions in these benchmarks are heavily skewed toward small values. Across the nine benchmarks we survey, the median ground-truth count is at most five, and the three most frequent values together account for between 45% and 93% of the videos. The resulting mean accuracy is then dominated by the most frequent counts; a value-dependent bias at a rare count contributes negligibly to the average and remains invisible. We introduce CountVideoBench, a benchmark of 2,600 synthetic videos covering 26 cardinalities in the interval [5, 50] (100 videos each), grouped into six clusters of consecutive counts that span the range while enabling near-neighbor comparisons. Each video contains a single class of static objects under a controlled camera motion, removing object motion and reducing recognition as confounds. We evaluate sixteen video-language models from nine architecture families in a zero-shot setting. No model exceeds 27.6% Exact Match. The dependence of accuracy on cardinality varies across models, ranging from monotonic decline to a non-monotonic peak-and-collapse pattern, which rules out increasing difficulty with cardinality as the dominant explanation of counting failure. Every model further concentrates its predictions on its own set of preferred integers, with accuracy peaks coinciding with these values and collapsing at adjacent counts. These findings highlight the need for balanced, diagnostic counting benchmarks to reliably assess this fundamental visual capability.","authors":["Marc Chapus","Carlos Crispim-Junior","Véronique Eglin","Atilla Baskurt"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:20:28Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:20:28Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28356v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28356v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28356v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28354v1","title":"S-Agent 2.0: Spatial Reasoning with a General Coding Agent","abstract":"Spatial reasoning asks a model to work out where objects are in 3D space, how they relate to one another, and how they move. Agentic VLMs have recently brought large improvements on such tasks by letting the model actively gather evidence with 3D perception tools instead of answering in a single pass. Even so, on many questions the tools return the right evidence and the model still does not arrive at the correct answer: the gap between model and tools, where raw readings become usable conclusions, is left unfilled. We present S-Agent 2.0, a spatial reasoning agent that equips an off-the-shelf general coding agent with a command-line perception toolkit, and we design the interface around one principle: help the VLM close the evidence loop: acquire scene evidence, remember it in a persistent scene memory, and interpret each reading in plain spatial language. This design keeps the full flexibility of a coding agent, and every reading it acts on stays grounded in the tools' geometry. On MMSI-Bench and ReVSI, the toolkit improves a strong open-source model, Qwen3.8-27B, by +13.9 and +20.5 points over the same agent without tools, and a strong closed-source model, Gemini-3.8-Flash, by +11.2 and +9.6 points. With the open backbone S-Agent 2.0 reaches 66.5 on MMSI-Bench and 82.3 on ReVSI, ahead of the specialized system SpatialClaw on the same backbone (62.6 and 74.5), and with Gemini-3.8-Flash it reaches 74.1 and 90.5 against its 67.0 and 79.7. Both tool gains shrink as the backbone grows more capable, but SpatialClaw's shrinks far more on ReVSI, from +13.2 to +2.7, while ours stays at +9.6. S-Agent 2.0 also works across different general coding agent harnesses, such as Codex and Claude Code.","authors":["Rui Gong","Ruoyu Wang","Yalun Dai","Hao Li","Sidi Yang","Qihao Zhao","Hongyu Li","Zeyang Guan","Fangzhou Hong","Zhaoxi Chen","Kim-Hui Yap","Ziwei Liu"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:20:00Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:20:00Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28354v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28354v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28354v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28351v1","title":"RayCo4D: Ray-Conditioned Continuous Motion Model for Monocular 4D Dynamic Reconstruction","abstract":"Reconstructing dynamic 3D scenes from monocular video requires inferring motion from noisy and intermittently visible observations. Long-range tracks provide valuable geometric constraints, but integrating their fragmented temporal coverage into shared, continuous motion remains challenging. We introduce \\textbf{RayCo4D}, a ray-conditioned continuous motion model for monocular 4D reconstruction that explicitly separates visual measurements from latent 3D motion states. Each track is associated with a reference-time 3D query constrained to its camera ray, and its image and depth observations constrain shared latent motion nodes through local many-to-many associations. Each node maintains an $\\mathrm{SE}(3)$ pose and body-frame velocity at sparse support times. A white-noise-on-acceleration process prior couples neighboring states and defines continuous-time interpolation, allowing observations across visibility intervals to jointly constrain the same latent state sequence.The inferred shared motion is transferred to Gaussian primitives through dual-quaternion blending, while reference-anchored local $\\mathrm{SE}(3)$ residuals capture fine-scale non-rigid deformation beyond the shared scaffold. Experiments on diverse monocular dynamic scenes show that RayCo4D achieves competitive novel-view synthesis quality, improves structural and temporal consistency under rapid motion and incomplete tracks, and supports continuous-time rendering.","authors":["Yuanxiang Ni","Xianliang Huang","Ruihan Hou","Feng Xie","Hao Zhang"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:17:29Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:17:29Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28351v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28351v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28351v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28346v1","title":"Auto-Director: Leveraging Multi-Agent Collaboration for Autonomous Video Editing","abstract":"Autonomous video editing from open-world resources remains a challenging problem in multimedia content creation. Retrieval-based methods are constrained by fixed source material and lack creative flexibility, while generative models struggle with long-horizon coherence and often diverge from user intent. Both paradigms fail to replicate the structured, multi-stage reasoning that underlies professional post-production. To address these limitations, we present Auto-Director, a tool-augmented multi-agent framework that mirrors professional editorial workflows: a Planning Agent decomposes abstract topics into fact-grounded scripts via retrieval-augmented planning; a Material Agent indexes open-world footage through shot segmentation and visual tagging; an Editor Agent assembles semantically aligned clips with MLLM-driven spatial adaptation; and a Critic Agent governs an iterative Reflect-and-Revise loop that checks scene-level alignment and pacing. To rigorously evaluate this new paradigm, we introduce VEdit-Bench, a benchmark of 100 expert-curated topics with human-annotated Golden Scripts and a five-dimensional metric suite covering Narrative Logic, Factual Accuracy, Semantic Alignment, Compositional Quality, and Stylistic Cohesion. Extensive experiments demonstrate that Auto-Director significantly outperforms baseline methods in both automated metrics and human evaluation, effectively bridging the gap between amateurs and professional-grade video production.","authors":["Yuxia Chen","Wenbo Zhu","Yangguang Ji","Zhenxiang Jiang","Teng Zhong","Yu Zhang"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:11:55Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:11:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28346v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28346v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28346v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","retrieval","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28323v1","title":"PACE: Plan, Attend, Check, and Emit for Training-Free Response Timing in Streaming Video Understanding","abstract":"Streaming video understanding requires a model to decide not only what to answer but also when to answer. Evidence arrives progressively, and recent benchmarks identify premature responses, issued before the queried event has concluded, as a recurring failure mode. Existing approaches often learn response timing through additional training, while training-free alternatives typically rely on scalar gating signals that do not explicitly represent when an answer becomes final. We organize chunk-wise streaming errors into three patterns: sampling variation can make an answer unstable, a stable answer may lack support in the observed video, or an answer may be supported by the current evidence while the event remains unfinished. We propose PACE (Plan, Attend, Check, and Emit), a training-free framework designed around these three failure modes. Given a question, PACE drafts a plan specifying what to observe and what will signal completion. As the stream unfolds, it invokes an off-the-shelf video LLM at informative moments, evaluates each draft for stability, grounding in its cited frames, and completion, and records verified observations in an event ledger. Experiments on ProactiveVideoQA and OVO-Bench Forward show that PACE improves over the official protocols by up to 5.9 average PAUC points and 6.2 average accuracy points across proprietary and open-source backbones, yielding state-of-the-art performance.","authors":["Siqi Guo","Yuxia Chen","Wenbo Zhu","Yangguang Ji","Zhenxiang Jiang","Teng Zhong","Yu Zhang"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:58:18Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:58:18Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28323v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28323v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28323v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28316v1","title":"ABACUS: A Benchmark for Image Editing Models to Assess Object Category and Counting Under Scene Conservation","abstract":"Instruction-based image editing models can now add, remove a specified number of objects or change their category while preserving the scene. Yet these models often fail to produce the correct number, and these failures grow sharply as scenes become more complex and the requested count increases. Current benchmarks largely miss this problem: they score edits with holistic similarity metrics or with vision-language model (VLM) judges that are themselves poor at counting, so numerical errors go unnoticed or are graded incorrectly. As a result, the true numerical ability of today's editing models has never been accurately measured. We address this issue with ABACUS, a new benchmark, evaluation protocol and dedicated scoring metric for numerical image editing. Our protocol is judge-free: no VLM or MLLM scores an edit or counts objects (a VLM only confirms the category of rare ambiguous crops). Instead, it verifies each edit at the instance level against panoptic ground truth confirmed by detector consensus, checking both that the exact requested count was achieved and that the rest of the scene was preserved, across the add, remove and change operations. By evaluating leading image editors, our method reveals substantial numerical editing failures that worsen with increasing scene complexity and object count; failures that prior benchmarks often overlooked or misjudged.","authors":["Aymen Lassoued","Angelique Loesch","Romaric Audigier"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:53:22Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:53:22Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28316v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28316v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28316v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28313v1","title":"Towards Consistent Geospatial Representations: Aligning One Encoder to Many Foundation Models","abstract":"Geospatial foundation models (GFMs) differ widely in pretraining objective, modality and resolution, and none leads across benchmarks. We ask whether their representations are complementary, and whether a single encoder can inherit that complementarity. We introduce Pangaea-Latents, an extension of the Pangaea benchmark providing frozen embeddings of $5$ GFMs over its $11$ datasets, along with reproduced baselines, and find GFM embeddings to be neither redundant nor disjoint. We then propose GeoMixture, a preliminary framework to align a ViT to several GFMs per dataset through a multi-representation alignment (mREPA) objective. Our encoder ties the best GFM in average rank across nine Pangaea datasets, trading peak performance for cross-task consistency.","authors":["Arthur Ouaknine","David Rolnick"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:52:21Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:52:21Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28313v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28313v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28313v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28284v1","title":"HRoPE: Homography Relative Positional Encoding","abstract":"Geometric Relative Positional Encoding has become the primary mechanism for injecting spatial inductive biases into multi-view transformers. Existing designs condition attention on camera-level frustums or ray-marched depth, associating cross-view tokens through the sensor geometry. Such sensor-centric formulations, however, describe only where a token is observed from, leaving the rich surface geometry of the scene region depicted by the token. In this paper, we advocate a shift toward surface-aware positional encoding that explicitly grounds patch tokens in the geometry of physical surfaces. We introduce HRoPE, which represents the local geometry of each patch as a slanted 3D plane, following the slanted support-window assumption of classical multi-view stereo. We inject token-level, plane-induced homographies into attention as relative transformations. This equips the transformer with a geometric prior for rectifying perspective distortions and aligning patch features across viewpoints. Our encoding requires no ground-truth geometry: the plane parameters can be predicted end-to-end under a view-synthesis objective alone, or derived from foundation models. Extensive experiments on RealEstate10K, CO3Dv2, and DL3DV show that our best variant of HRoPE consistently outperforms the evaluated geometric positional encodings for novel view synthesis. Extensive ablations and analysis show the effectiveness of the proposed encoding. Please refer to our project page at: https://gaohchen.github.io/HRoPE","authors":["Huachen Gao","Yang Yang","Xiaoqian Liang","Xingyu Chen","Youjia Zhang","Qi Zhang","Baorui Ma","Anpei Chen","Wei Yang"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:36:51Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:36:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28284v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28284v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28284v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28282v1","title":"ChartEvo: Self-Evolving Agent Harnesses for Long-Tail Chart Reasoning","abstract":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have substantially advanced chart understanding and reasoning, yet their performance remains highly uneven across chart types. While modern MLLMs perform well on common charts, their accuracy drops sharply on long-tail charts with diverse visual structures and reasoning requirements. Tool-augmented agents can improve complex chart reasoning by introducing specialized visual capabilities, but manually engineering strategies and tools for the heterogeneous and evolving long tail is difficult to scale. We introduce \\textbf{ChartEvo}, a recursive self-improvement framework that enables chart agents to adapt to underperforming chart types through experience-driven harness-level self-improvement. ChartEvo jointly evolves reusable chart-specific Skills and executable Tools, while learning reusable local tool-call chains to compose evolved capabilities and control the inference overhead of tool-augmented reasoning. On long-tail charts from ChartBench and ChartX, ChartEvo improves accuracy over its base MLLM, Qwen3.7-Plus, by \\textbf{45.6\\%} and \\textbf{32.5\\%}, respectively, demonstrating the effectiveness of recursive self-improvement for long-tail chart adaptation.","authors":["Qi Wang","Peijie Wang","Jun Cao","Haoyang Tong","Xiao-Hui Li","Fei Yin","Cheng-Lin Liu"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:34:54Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:34:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28282v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28282v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28282v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28280v1","title":"GALA: Geometry-Aware Language Model for Controllable Object Arrangement","abstract":"Reasoning about fine-grained placement constraints, such as support and containment, is essential for realistic 3D scene creation. It remains a significant challenge for existing methods that rely on simplified object representations like oriented bounding box (OBB), which are blind to the detailed geometry. To address this limitation, we introduce GALA, a geometry-aware language model with explicit point cloud perception for controllable object placement. Our model performs autoregressively by predicting the 6D pose of one target object at a time conditioned on the scene context and placement instruction. We utilize a two-stage training strategy: the first stage aligns geometric and textual modalities through pretraining tasks, and the second stage fine-tunes the model for placement. To facilitate this task, we also curate a multi-source scene dataset covering diverse room types and fine-grained furniture arrangements, which will be open-sourced for community benefit. Experiments on our benchmark show our model significantly outperforms baselines without explicit geometric reasoning. We further develop a multi-stage plan-place-verify agent pipeline that uses the model for text-driven scene generation. Project Page: https://xccelephant.github.io/paper/gala/","authors":["Chucheng Xiang","Runze Wang","Zhi Deng","Ruchao Bao","Yuanwei Zhang","Yuxuan Xie","Hanliu Wang","Liangzhen Fei","Wenzheng Wu","Cheng Wan","Peifeng Li","Zhongyuan Liu","Ligang Liu"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:32:52Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:32:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28280v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28280v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28280v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28266v1","title":"VGL-stGen: Learning a shared multimodal model for lung histology and spatial transcriptomics","abstract":"VGL-stGen is a multimodal large language model adapted from a pretrained medical vision-language model using paired lung H&E and spatial transcriptomics with single-cell references. Training integrates image-expression and expression-cell-type pairs, and gene-conditioned pseudo-labels transfer transcriptomic cell-type supervision to histology images. One deployed checkpoint supports thirteen evaluated tasks spanning tissue and cell-type classification, gene prediction and conditional expression completion from combinations of histology, cell identity and observed gene expression. Task-specific decoding produces categorical labels, gene lists and structured expression levels. For conditional completion, fixing queried gene identities and predicting their levels autoregressively improves structured prediction. Controlled adaptation shows that restricting supervision to requested expression-level tokens can further improve image-conditioned completion. Image-permutation experiments indicate that correctly paired histology adds information beyond observed expression. External classification relies partly on computational references with shared annotation lineage, and some H&E sources were exposed during cancer training or model development. Within these conditions, VGL-stGen demonstrates how a medical vision-language backbone can learn from spatial transcriptomics and histology to connect tissue morphology and molecular states through shared generative modeling.","authors":["Haenara Shin","Dongjoo Lee","Sungwoo Bae","Kwon Joong Na","Daeseung Lee","Hongyoon Choi"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:22:47Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:22:47Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28266v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28266v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28266v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28264v1","title":"PI-MLLM: Psychophysics-Inspired Multimodal Large Language Model for Oddly Satisfying Video Emotion Analysis","abstract":"Oddly Satisfying Videos (OSVs) can evoke strong viewer responses through repetitive motion, visual tactility, and ASMR-like sounds despite containing few explicit semantic emotion cues. This makes OSVs a challenging setting for Video Emotion Analysis (VEA), since most existing methods focus on semantic video content and under-model the physical sensory dynamics that shape viewer responses. To support Oddly Satisfying Video Emotion Analysis (OSVEA), we construct the Oddly Satisfying Video Dataset (OSVD), comprising 12,000 annotated 5-second clips. Each clip provides viewer-emotion labels under video-only, audio-only, and audio-visual conditions, together with explanations grounded in visual and acoustic sensory evidence. Building on OSVD, we propose PI-MLLM, a psychophysics-inspired multimodal large language model that integrates Modality-Specific Dynamics Alignment (MSDA) and Coalition-Based Audio-Visual Attribution (CAVA). MSDA uses auxiliary prediction objectives to align modality representations with visual motion and acoustic energy-onset dynamics. CAVA evaluates emotion scores across modality coalitions and applies Shapley attribution to identify the dominant modality and determine whether visual and auditory evidence is synergistic or conflicting. Experiments show that PI-MLLM improves both viewer-side emotion prediction and sensory-grounded explanation quality, suggesting the value of physical sensory cues to video emotion analysis.","authors":["Yuheng Zhang","Jie Zhang","Yuecong Min","Yunwei Zhao","Meichen Liu","Qiankun Li","Shiguang Shan"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:20:39Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:20:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28264v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28264v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28264v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28257v1","title":"SplatFlux: Feed-Forward 4D Gaussian Reconstruction and Generation from Video Latents","abstract":"This work presents SplatFlux, a feed-forward latent-to-4DGS framework for both video-based 4D reconstruction and conditional 4D generation. Our key observation is that observed videos and conditionally generated content can be represented in the same pretrained video latent space, allowing both tasks to be formulated as latent-to-4DGS decoding. To construct compact and temporally coherent 4D scenes, SplatFlux introduces a static-dynamic decoupled 4D Gaussian representation that explicitly predicts dynamic probabilities for Gaussian primitives. This representation separates static scene structures from moving regions, enabling complete 4D scene construction in a feed-forward manner through static aggregation and dynamic motion interpolation. To learn static-dynamic decomposition and dynamic Gaussian motion without costly annotations, we further develop an auxiliary distillation strategy that provides pseudo dynamic masks and optical-flow cues. Extensive experiments across 3D and 4D reconstruction and generation demonstrate that SplatFlux achieves state-of-the-art performance.","authors":["Runmin Zhang","Binglin Ren","Zhu Yu","Fudong Wang","Jiangke Lin","Yichun Shentu","Si-Yuan Cao","Yi Yuan","Hui-Liang Shen"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:12:53Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:12:53Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28257v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28257v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28257v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28255v1","title":"Convergent Performance, Divergent Mechanisms in LoRA-Adapted Vision Foundation Models for Brain MRI","abstract":"Adapting Vision Foundation Models (VFMs) to medical imaging via LoRA has become standard practice, yet model selection is guided almost entirely by downstream task metrics, treating backbones with similar performance as interchangeable. We investigate whether equal performance implies equal internal representational shift. We analyse four ViT-B/16 backbones, DINOv3, SigLIP2, MerMED-FM, and BiomedCLIP, pre-trained with different objectives (self-distillation vs. contrastive vision-language) and on different data domains (natural vs. medical images). We adapt each to brain tumour segmentation and evaluate on six datasets covering both segmentation and Alzheimer's disease classification. Using attention divergence, weight-change analysis, and activation patching, we find that the four models converge to similar segmentation performance (mean Dice 78-81%) through markedly different internal dynamics, yet diverge in cross-task transfer: segmentation adaptation improves Alzheimer's disease classification for DINOv3 but degrades it for the other three backbones. We reveal a dissociation between modification magnitude and causal importance: the most heavily modified layers are not the causally relevant ones, with early-to-mid layers identified as sufficient for segmentation recovery. Restricting LoRA to these layers matches or approaches full-layer segmentation while reducing classification degradation in the three affected backbones, providing a mechanistically grounded strategy that is more parameter-efficient and more robust than standard full-layer adaptation.","authors":["Cecilia Diana-Albelda","Fiona Kekwick","Raghav Mehta","Aleksandra Kulbaka","Paul M. Matthews","Jesus Bescos","Álvaro García-Martín","Wenjia Bai","Bernhard Kainz"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:12:44Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:12:44Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28255v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28255v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28255v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28210v1","title":"Learn to Modify: Self-evolving Multi-agent Refinement Framework for Conversational Text-to-Motion Generation","abstract":"Despite rapid progress in text-to-motion generation, existing methods are commonly designed for single-shot generation. When users are dissatisfied with specific motion details, they are often forced to regenerate the entire sequence from scratch, which is computationally inefficient and potentially destroys previously established kinematic context. A practical generation system should instead support iterative user feedback and refine only the affected spatio-temporal regions while preserving the remaining motion. Therefore, we introduce a conversational motion generation paradigm that requires the system to coordinate instruction understanding, motion generation, localized editing, and quality assessment. Recent advances in LLM-driven multi-agent systems provide a promising foundation for such role-specialized coordination. To tackle these challenges, a Self-evolving Multi-Agent RefinemenT Framework (SMART) is proposed for conversational motion generation. Within SMART, to preserve semantic consistency, we design a semantic-guided masked motion editing mechanism, which precisely grounds natural language feedback into spatiotemporal motion regions to enable localized refinements without altering unedited regions. To reduce redundant exploration, we propose a memory-based self-evolving strategy that dynamically accumulates motion editing knowledge from interaction history and reuses past successful experience. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SMART effectively supports multi-turn conversational motion generation and enables efficient motion refinement.","authors":["Jianan Li","Jiawei Zhao","Yangtao Zhou","Zhifu Zhao","Shaoxin Wu","Yutao Wang","Dezhen Wang","Hua Chu","Fu Li"],"published":"2026-10-20T13:47:11Z","updated":"2026-10-20T13:47:11Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28210v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28210v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28210v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28192v1","title":"Offline Makes Online Better: Calibrated Cache Compression for Video Generation","abstract":"Token merging is an effective approach to reducing KV-cache memory consumption in autoregressive video generation by clustering temporally redundant tokens and retaining only a small set of representatives. Existing methods, however, typically apply a uniform merging ratio across all attention layers and heads. We observe that different layers and heads exhibit substantially different yet jointly correlated sensitivities to token merging, and that these sensitivity patterns remain consistent across prompts. Based on this observation, we introduce CALM, a Calibrated Allocation for Layer-Head Merging that uses offline calibration to improve online cache compression. Exhaustively evaluating all possible layer-head allocations is prohibitively expensive. To enable efficient calibration, we show that attention-output error strongly correlates with end-to-end video quality and can therefore serve as a lightweight proxy for allocation risk assessment. We evaluate different allocation strategies using this proxy and construct a fixed layer-head risk map offline. Given a cache budget at inference time, CALM uses dynamic programming to determine the best allocation of merging ratios. Experiments across three different autoregressive video models demonstrate that CALM consistently outperforms uniform token merging under the same memory budget, by reducing the LPIPS to original videos by around 21% with higher video-quality scores.","authors":["Zhen Wang","Ning Lai","Qingtao Yu"],"published":"2026-10-20T13:33:04Z","updated":"2026-10-20T13:33:04Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28192v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28192v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28192v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28190v1","title":"CrossFit-GS: Out-of-Fold Pose Estimation for COLMAP-Free 3D Gaussian Splatting","abstract":"Pose-free 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) aims to bypass cumbersome Structure-from-Motion (SfM) by directly reconstructing scenes from unposed image sequences. In this work, we present CrossFit-GS, a deferred-commitment progressive framework that mitigates cumulative trajectory drift and breaks the self-reinforcing loop of distorted-map error propagation. Specifically, we partition the input sequence into interleaved subsets and maintain two complementary submaps. Each candidate pose is cross-refined against a frozen opposite submap that has never been optimized using the target frame, thereby providing an independent geometric reference for pose estimation. Checkerboard validation and spatial coverage safeguards further reject unreliable updates, and only verified poses are committed to their corresponding owner submap. Once the trajectory has been refined, the intermediate submaps are discarded and the final global representation is trained from scratch using the fixed refined poses. Extensive evaluations across multiple benchmarks demonstrate that CrossFit-GS substantially reduces cumulative trajectory drift and consistently improves both camera pose estimation and novel-view synthesis over existing methods.","authors":["Zijian Wu","Jinliang Wang","Mingfeng Jiang","Zidian Lin","Ying Song","Ziqian Lu"],"published":"2026-10-20T13:29:26Z","updated":"2026-10-20T13:29:26Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28190v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28190v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28190v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28131v1","title":"OV-Foam: How 3D Representations Shape Semantic Feature Lifting","abstract":"Existing feature lifting approaches for open-vocabulary segmentation rely on simple backprojection to assign features to primitives. In the case of 3D Gaussian splatting reconstruction, the primitives overlap heavily, leading to suboptimal reconstructions, governed by many primitives per ray. Backprojection, however, reduces to a least-squares solvable problem, exactly when no two primitives share a ray. Motivated by this insight, we propose OV-Foam, which uses PowerFoam as the underlying representation. This representation uses a power-diagram to partition the scene, resulting in zero overlap between primitives and a substantial reduction in primitives per ray, which ultimately simplifies the lifting problem and allows for closer-to-optimal feature assignment. We show that the space partitioning primitives of OV-Foam reach near-optimal assignment, and our experiments demonstrate improved open-vocabulary segmentation over Gaussian baselines.","authors":["Yousef Rajeh","Alejandra Esparza Pelcastre","Ivan Viola","Dominik Engel"],"published":"2026-10-20T12:38:14Z","updated":"2026-10-20T12:38:14Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28131v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28131v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28131v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28129v1","title":"GAMF-Net: Grouped Availability-Aware Multimodal Fusion for Smartphone Skin Lesion Classification","abstract":"Smartphone clinical images provide a low-cost data source for computer-aided skin lesion classification in resource-constrained settings, but images alone cannot fully capture patients' medical histories and lesion symptoms. To address the lack of structured organization among heterogeneous clinical variables, the ambiguity between observed and imputed values, and the risk that clinical information may interfere with visual evidence, we propose GAMF-Net, a grouped availability-aware multimodal fusion network. Ten clinical variables are divided into three semantic groups---demographics, history-related risk, and lesion presentation---and each group jointly encodes field values and field-level availability masks. The image branch produces the base classification logits, while each clinical group generates image-conditioned class-level residual evidence. Only available groups participate in availability-normalized equal-weight fusion; when all clinical information is unavailable, the model reduces exactly to the image-only pathway. We evaluate the method on PAD-UFES-20 under a patient-disjoint five-fold protocol with separate training, validation, and test partitions. GAMF-Net achieves an Accuracy of $74.17\\pm2.85\\%$, a balanced accuracy (BACC) of $70.11\\pm2.11\\%$, a Macro-F1 of $68.85\\pm0.85\\%$, and a Macro-AUROC of $93.42\\pm1.20\\%$, outperforming all image-only and multimodal baselines reimplemented under a unified protocol. Compared with the strongest baseline, MetaBlock, the corresponding improvements are 2.37, 2.59, 4.14, and 1.02 percentage points, respectively. Ablation results show that clinical information substantially improves class-balanced performance, constrained equal-weight fusion is more effective than dynamic fusion, and the lesion-presentation group provides the most informative clinical complement.","authors":["Yue Zhuo","Jingjia Yan","Fangyu Liu","Lingjun Liu","Zongjun Chen","Zhisheng Luo","Zhonghua Xie"],"published":"2026-10-20T12:35:39Z","updated":"2026-10-20T12:35:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28129v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28129v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28129v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28120v1","title":"PointForward V2: Point-Aligned Interaction for Feedforward Driving Reconstruction","abstract":"Feedforward reconstruction of dynamic driving scenes requires integrating observations across multiple views and time steps into a coherent 3D representation. PointForward established state-of-the-art feedforward driving reconstruction by lifting image observations into sparse 3D queries, demonstrating the effectiveness of point-aligned scene representations. However, these queries are largely processed independently, limiting information exchange across space and time, while direct global attention among tens of thousands of point queries is computationally prohibitive. In this work, we present \\textbf{PointForward V2}, which advances point-aligned representation to point-aligned interaction. Our key idea is to mediate query interaction through compact, scene-structured registers. For the static background, frame-wise Static Scene Registers compress point information, communicate across frames, and propagate the aggregated context back to the queries. For each dynamic instance, queries from different frames are aligned in an object-centric canonical space and interact through instance-specific Dynamic Instance Registers. We further employ 4D positional encoding that combines aligned spatial coordinates with original timestamps, enabling spatially aligned yet temporally distinguishable interactions. In this way, PointForward V2 enables scalable spatio-temporal reasoning directly among lifted 3D queries without dense global attention. Experiments on Waymo, nuScenes, and Argoverse 2 demonstrate state-of-the-art performance across large-scale driving benchmarks.","authors":["Xianqi Wang","Cheng Chi","Yuechuan Pu","Hongcheng Luo","Junda Cheng","Guang Chen","Hangjun Ye","Bing Wang","Xin Yang"],"published":"2026-10-20T12:30:21Z","updated":"2026-10-20T12:30:21Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28120v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28120v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28120v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation","instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28118v1","title":"Reliability-Guided Vision-Language Decoding with Text-Aware Skip Refinement for Medical Image Segmentation","abstract":"Medical image segmentation remains challenging when targets exhibit limited visual distinction, irregular structure, or substantial variation across imaging modalities. Medical text can reduce this ambiguity, but indiscriminate fusion may introduce unreliable guidance. We present RELiF, a vision--language framework that integrates text through reliability-guided decoding. Its Reliability-guided Fusion Gate (RFG) adaptively controls cross-modal updates, while the Text-guided Skip Refinement Adapter (TSRA) refines high-resolution features using text guidance. RELiF is evaluated on radiographic, CT, and endoscopic segmentation tasks, where it shows competitive performance across diverse imaging characteristics and target appearances. Additional analyses examine efficiency and stability. The complementary uncertainty representation derived from the reliability response also shows an association with prediction difficulty, with higher uncertainty values occurring more frequently in pixels associated with segmentation errors. Code: https://github.com/SeivanTalaie/RELiF.","authors":["Sivan Talaei","Fatemeh Daneshfar","Mustaqeem Khan","Abdulhady Abas Abdullah","Mourad Oussalah"],"published":"2026-10-20T12:29:44Z","updated":"2026-10-20T12:29:44Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28118v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28118v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28118v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28091v1","title":"LatticeRank: Spacing-Ranked Consistency for Native-Grid 3D Medical Image Segmentation","abstract":"Variable voxel spacing changes more than image resolution: it alters the discrete sampling lattice, tensor geometry, and anatomical evidence available to a 3D segmentation network. While conventional pipelines restore images to a canonical grid, direct-grid deployment requires the model itself to tolerate these changes. We observe that candidate grids are not exchangeable views -- a coarse grid removes more structural information than a mildly resampled one -- yet standard consistency objectives assign them equal influence. We therefore propose LatticeRank, a training-only framework that aligns candidate posteriors in physical space and allocates consistency supervision by voxel-spacing order: among endpoint-matched candidates, the auxiliary loss concentrates on the coarsest requested grid, while inference retains a single network forward with no candidate branch or ensemble. On MSD Task04, LatticeRank improves coarse-grid Extra Dice over mean consistency by 0.03436 across five seeds while meeting the prespecified reference-grid noninferiority criterion. A controlled ladder shows that grid exposure provides the largest recovery, posterior consistency adds a second gain, and spacing-ranked allocation yields the strongest direct-grid result; requested spacing thus provides a simple, model-independent ordering signal for direct-grid consistency learning.","authors":["Jingjia Yan","Yue Zhuo","Zhonghua Xie"],"published":"2026-10-20T12:08:51Z","updated":"2026-10-20T12:08:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28091v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28091v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28091v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28087v1","title":"WildAIGC-Bench: Can AI Image Detectors Handle the Wild? A Benchmark of Generated and Edited Images in Online Circulation","abstract":"AI-generated images linked to news events and online claims can mislead audiences, enable fraud, or cause public controversy. We introduce WildAIGC-Bench, built through recurring collection across news websites, fact-checking sources, and related social media content. The pipeline combines broad coverage, timely updates, and human-verified labels through scheduled discovery, automatic screening and preliminary annotation, and human review of image-specific evidence. The benchmark contains 407 verified AI-generated or edited images, each accompanied by a source webpage link and an evidence summary. Images retain the conditions encountered online, including compression, screenshots, and recapture. Evaluation of dedicated visual detectors, multimodal large language models, and a simple Perception Encoder baseline reveals substantial recall gaps relative to existing benchmarks. WildAIGC-Bench provides both a practical detection challenge and a basis for continued data expansion and source-based verification. The dataset is released at https://github.com/YuZijian/WildAIGC-Bench.","authors":["Zijian Yu","Hangwei Zhang","Jun Lan","Huijia Zhu","Weiqiang Wang","Jianfu Zhang"],"published":"2026-10-20T12:07:16Z","updated":"2026-10-20T12:07:16Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28087v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28087v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28087v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28086v1","title":"MoDDiT: Simple Token Routing in Diffusion Transformers","abstract":"Real-time video generation, interactive world models, and on-device image synthesis are almost a reality. Diffusion Transformers (DiT) achieve excellent sample quality, shifting the bottleneck to the throughput of the models themselves. Natural images are inherently sparse, as large portions of an image are often background, contribute little to the scene content, and are easy to denoise. DiTs fail to exploit this sparsity, as they process image tokens statically and uniformly. We propose MoDDiT, a simple architecture modification that learns to route tokens through both MLP and self-attention operations. By routing tokens through both blocks independently, MoDDiT allows for dynamic compute allocation in MLPs that varies across tokens, images, and denoising timesteps. Using MoDDiT to train image generation models from scratch significantly reduces computational costs of training and inference, processing as few as 12.5% of the tokens. By increasing sparsity and model depth, MoDDiT reduces costs and improves generation performance over unrouted baselines. The learned allocation is interpretable, revealing how computation varies with visual content and noise level. We also use MoDDiT to train video generation models, where allocation correlates with motion, and to distill a dense bidirectional video model into a sparse autoregressive student.","authors":["Jarek Liesen","Matthew T. Jackson","Benjamin Ellis","Shimon Whiteson","Jakob N. Foerster"],"published":"2026-10-20T12:05:49Z","updated":"2026-10-20T12:05:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28086v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28086v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28086v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28074v1","title":"Slot-JEPA: Object-Structured Prediction Interfaces for Latent World Models","abstract":"As a prototypical latent world model, Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPAs) provide strong semantic representations. However, dense patch representations expose a redundant interface to downstream predictors, which must implicitly infer spatial organization and temporal correspondence, presenting three challenges. First, object information is distributed across patches, forcing each downstream predictor to redundantly recover object structure that could otherwise be reused across tasks before modeling interactions. Second, the lack of explicit entity correspondence across time makes historical transitions expensive, restricting the predictor's flexibility in utilizing context. Third, without explicit transport structure, existing JEPA models typically use pointwise l1/l2 distances, which are insensitive to spatial displacement when predicted and goal supports are largely disjoint. We propose the Slot-based Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture (Slot-JEPA), which decomposes dense representations into stable semantic slots and time-varying spatial masks. Pretrained once and frozen downstream, this decomposition exposes a reusable object-structured interface. Building on it, our Transition Memory Predictor (TMP) stores visual transitions as compact masks and retrieves useful context using statistics of corresponding slots. Its two branches separate slot-specific dynamics from shared cross-slot dynamics. We further use Unbalanced Optimal Transport (UOT) to supervise mask prediction and evaluate candidate plans by transporting mass between aligned predicted and goal masks, providing a signal better aligned with spatial progress. Across seven visual-understanding benchmarks, the slot-reconstructed features remain transferable. Slot-JEPA also improves goal-conditioned planning across six planar environments and four robotic arm planning tasks on the REALM-JEPA benchmark.","authors":["Peisong Wen","Qianqian Xu","Minye Lei","Siran Dai","Yang Liu","Qingming Huang"],"published":"2026-10-20T11:53:42Z","updated":"2026-10-20T11:53:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28074v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28074v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28074v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28059v1","title":"Auditing Where Concept Bottleneck Accuracy Comes From on CUB","abstract":"Concept Bottleneck Models (CBMs) promise predictions that humans can inspect and correct through concepts such as \"curved beak\". Yet on CUB-200-2011, their standard benchmark, progress is measured like that of ordinary classifiers: by task and concept accuracy. Neither reveals whether the label is predicted from the exposed concept scores, or whether a concept is supported by visual evidence of that concept rather than by co-occurring features such as cues to the species as a whole. We audit where the accuracy of CBMs and Concept Embedding Models (CEMs) on CUB comes from. The CUB annotations are too noisy to explain it: class-level training labels agree with per-image annotations on only ~82% of image--concept pairs, and classifiers trained on per-image annotations reach only ~37% species accuracy. Leakage probes show that CEM predictions cannot be reconstructed from their concept scores; the missing class information sits in internal embeddings. We identify four design axes through which class information can enter concept scores---receptive field, shared versus concept-specific representations, norm-dependent scoring, and task supervision. An ablation shows that most of it enters through non-local features: restricting a ResNet50 backbone to local BagNet patches lowers the accuracy recoverable from concept scores from 72% to 43%, close to what ground-truth annotations support, while joint training raises it again at the cost of concept accuracy. Localization and occlusion show that locality makes grounding measurable but does not guarantee it: under class-level labels, local concept scores use the head as a proxy for body, wing, and tail concepts, and CEM concept scores barely respond to occluding their supporting part. Task and concept accuracy should thus support, not drive, the evaluation of CBMs, and given the noisy annotations, CUB accuracy gaps should not be overinterpreted.","authors":["Sascha Saralajew","Eleonora Poeta","Eliana Pastor","Ammar Shaker"],"published":"2026-10-20T11:47:49Z","updated":"2026-10-20T11:47:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28059v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28059v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28059v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28051v1","title":"Assessing Representation Semantics Under Language Pretraining in Fine-Grained Classification","abstract":"Understanding how supervision signals shape semantic visual representations remains challenging, particularly in fine-grained, low-data settings. Standard evaluations of vision models rely on downstream accuracy, which often fails to capture structural differences in learned representations. We propose a compact explainability-driven framework for comparing encoders trained with classification-only and caption-generation supervision on CUB-200. Using a controlled two-stage protocol in which both encoders are ultimately optimized for the same classification task, we enable a direct comparison of the resulting representations. Saliency maps are generated using attention rollout and gradient-weighted rollout and evaluated with five metrics covering faithfulness (positive and negative perturbation) and localization (keypoint emphasis, background attribution, and Earth Mover's Distance). Across random seeds and explanation methods, caption supervision yields representations that are more sensitive to the removal of salient regions, better aligned with annotated object parts, and less reliant on background regions, whereas classification supervision produces representations that are slightly more robust to the removal of low-saliency regions. An ablation study on the Oxford-IIIT Pet dataset shows consistent trends across key metrics. These results demonstrate that the proposed framework makes supervision-induced representational differences observable and quantifiable beyond downstream accuracy alone. More broadly, it provides a systematic basis for assessing learned representations and comparing alternative training and transfer-learning strategies.","authors":["Md Raisul Kibria","Sébastien Lafond"],"published":"2026-10-20T11:39:21Z","updated":"2026-10-20T11:39:21Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28051v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28051v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28051v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28018v1","title":"P$^3$-VLM: A Point-based Alternative for Grounded 3D Vision-Language Models","abstract":"Examining existing benchmarks for grounding in 3D VLMs reveals a pervasive \"semantic leakage\" in the evaluation and prompting protocols: a heavy reliance on bounding boxes as spatial prompts or representation primitives. Because bounding boxes are highly informative of object categories, they introduce a shortcut for the models to neglect visual information during 3D VLM training. We demonstrate this with a \"blind\" LLM that, prompted only with the box coordinates, can perform surprisingly well. To further analyze this shortcut, we propose point-based grounding prompting - querying models with a single 3D point instead of a box. Under this protocol, the accuracy of the blind LLM drops from 36.4% to 18.0% on ScanNet's Nr3D, and similarly degrades for 3D VLMs (e.g. LL3DA). Building on this insight, we introduce P$^3$-VLM, a novel tri-pathway 3D VLM that supports point-based prompts and achieves 52.1% state-of-the-art accuracy on location-grounded captioning under this setting. To achieve this, we redesign the architecture while keeping it detector-free, scene-centric, and suitable for 3D GS inputs. P$^3$-VLM utilizes three complementary pathways (location-, task-, and context-aware), enabling strong reasoning about scenes without any bounding box dependencies. The key benefit of point-based learning is a consistent improvement across settings: P$^3$-VLM achieves state-of-the-art performance under point-based grounding while also improving robustness, OOD generalization, and performance on ungrounded scene-centric 3D VQA benchmarks, reflecting its reduced reliance on bounding-box shortcuts and resulting stronger visual-spatial reasoning.","authors":["Anna-Maria Halacheva","Jan-Nico Zaech","Sombit Dey","Luc Van Gool","Danda Pani Paudel"],"published":"2026-10-20T11:08:07Z","updated":"2026-10-20T11:08:07Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28018v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28018v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28018v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28007v1","title":"AdaptMIL: Domain Adaptation for Multiple Instance Learning in Digital Pathology","abstract":"Pathology foundation models (PFMs) support a wide range of histopathology tasks on whole-slide images through multiple instance learning (MIL). Despite recent progress, these models remain sensitive to cross-hospital distribution shifts: performance often degrades when a task head (e.g., classification) is trained on data from one hospital and then used on data from another hospital. To address this challenge, we propose AdaptMIL, a training strategy for integrating domain adaptation into MIL training. AdaptMIL is motivated by our finding that directly applying domain adaptation objectives during MIL training can interfere with MIL aggregation learning, often resulting in performance degradation over standard training. AdaptMIL resolves this by decoupling representation-level domain alignment learning from MIL aggregation learning through a two-stage training procedure. We show that AdaptMIL improves cross-hospital performance and outperforms alternative adaptation methods across experiments spanning multiple WSI datasets, tasks, PFMs, MIL architectures, and domain adaptation objectives. AdaptMIL's code will be released upon publication.","authors":["Ben Vardi","Dana Schonberger","Yuval Friedmann","Zohar Yakhini","Iris Barshack","Alexander Loebel","Ariel Shamir"],"published":"2026-10-20T10:52:55Z","updated":"2026-10-20T10:52:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28007v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28007v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28007v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28004v1","title":"Differentiable Task-Driven Coded Aperture Camera Design with 3D Gaussian Scene Representations","abstract":"Camera design heavily influences perception task performance, yet cameras are typically designed by domain experts through costly experimentation, in isolation from the tasks they serve. Existing joint-design methods either rely on differentiable simulation limited to pre-captured 2D images, restricting them to optics or image-processing pipeline design, or use non-differentiable camera simulators optimised with derivative-free or reinforcement-learning methods that scale poorly with the number of design parameters. We present a differentiable framework for jointly designing complex camera systems and downstream perception tasks. We use it to design two coded-aperture depth-from-defocus cameras, a monocular and a stereo configuration, by jointly optimising the coded aperture mask, camera parameters, and a depth estimation network end-to-end. Our framework represents scenes with 3D Gaussian Splatting reconstructed from real-world captures, providing a differentiable image formation process for gradient-based optimisation over design spaces intractable for prior differentiable approaches. Results demonstrate that our differentiable method improves over existing coded aperture design and non-differentiable camera design baselines, offering a step forward in automated and principled camera design for autonomous systems.","authors":["Chengyang Yan","Donald G. Dansereau"],"published":"2026-10-20T10:48:08Z","updated":"2026-10-20T10:48:08Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28004v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28004v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28004v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27999v1","title":"Matrix-Game 4.0: Harnessing Video Models for Real-Time Playable Game Worlds","abstract":"Moving from controllable videos to genuinely playable worlds requires persistent world state, executable game rules, and low-latency interaction. We outline a roadmap for the next generation of Matrix-Game that separates game execution from visual generation through an executable game harness, and introduce Matrix-Game 4.0 as the first system built on this design. A lightweight game engine maintains world state and executes interaction rules, a multimodal coding agent translates player ideas into game code and dynamic scene captions, and a renderer based on autoregressive video model transforms engine-rendered low-poly proxies into detailed visuals, conditioned on dynamic captions and memory references. This framework provides continuous guidance for visual generation and enables natural-language creation and modification of scenes and gameplay. Our renderer is trained on aligned low-poly and high-fidelity video pairs and distilled into a causal generator that generates in four-frame chunks with one or two denoising steps. Together with targeted inference optimizations, Matrix-Game 4.0 enables 480p, 60 FPS gameplay on a single NVIDIA RTX5090 with a key-press-to-display latency of 112ms. Single-step generation achieves 83FPS steady-state throughput with 18.2GiB of peak GPU memory usage. With fixed prompts, steady-state throughput also reaches 67FPS on an RTX5080 and 58FPS on an RTX5070Ti. We demonstrate customizable shooter and driving games with engine-enforced mechanics, and release code, model weights, and low-poly game assets to support research and creation of playable game worlds.","authors":["Jihai Zhang","Runjia Qian","Kaiqin Kong","Biao Jiang","Puyi Wang","Siru Chen","Tianle Li","Guanjie Chen","Junyi Wu","Chubin Chen","Yuanhang Li","Kaichen Huang","Haobo Zhang","Yangguang Li","Wei Yu","Guibin Chen","Yu Cheng","Yahui Zhou"],"published":"2026-10-20T10:41:38Z","updated":"2026-10-20T10:41:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27999v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27999v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27999v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27986v1","title":"Recast the Past: Stable Long Video Generation with Full Context Memory","abstract":"Long-horizon video generation requires retaining an ever-expanding history, but conditioning on its growing token sequence is costly. Existing methods restrict or compress historical context, potentially losing information needed for future generation. This work proposes ReCam, which groups local spatiotemporal blocks of historical latents into vectors and projects them directly into the high-dimensional hidden space of a Diffusion Transformer. The model learns to use the resulting memory tokens through native self-attention, reducing token count while retaining block-level access to the full history. To mitigate temporal drift during autoregressive rollout, we further introduce dual-pointer denoising with separate noise schedules for recent context and target frames. Among the evaluated methods, ReCam achieves the highest VBench-Long overall score of 80.1 and competitive static-scene revisitation performance on R2M-Bench. It also leads on all four consistency metrics for off-screen subject reappearance, including clothing consistency of 4.09 versus 3.03 for the best baseline.","authors":["Yukuo Ma","Cong Liu","Junke Wang","Junqi Liu","Haibin Huang","Zuxuan Wu","Xuelong Li"],"published":"2026-10-20T10:27:07Z","updated":"2026-10-20T10:27:07Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27986v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27986v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27986v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27982v1","title":"Beyond the Frame: Redundancy, Not Trajectory, Carries the Privacy Leak in Video Unlearning","abstract":"Class unlearning for video segmentation aims to ensure that a removed category cannot be reconstructed from a deployed model's outputs. Current leakage evaluation algorithms inspect one frame at a time, and we show that this understates leakage by a wide margin: an attacker that averages a model's output field over a two-second clip recovers the forgotten class from every method we evaluate on VSPW and most on Virtual KITTI~2, including models whose per-frame predictions are visually indistinguishable from a retrain oracle (a model trained from scratch without the forgotten class). We attribute the recoverable signal to cross-frame redundancy rather than object trajectory, establish this with a time-shuffled control that leaves leakage intact, and introduce PACE, an unlearning method that suppresses redundancy directly using an exactly permutation-invariant pooled adversarial discriminator and a two-sided temporal consistency objective. Across VSPW and Virtual KITTI~2, PACE lowers pooled attacker AP to $18.79$ (\\texttt{person}) and $12.10$ (\\texttt{car}) on VSPW and $9.11$ (\\texttt{car}) on Virtual KITTI~2, achieving on the latter the strongest suppression among verdict-safe methods retaining usable utility, while retaining $19.8$ mIoU on VSPW \\texttt{car} against at most $8.3$ for any baseline achieving comparable erasure.","authors":["Kavyansh Tyagi","Paolo Russo"],"published":"2026-10-20T10:24:33Z","updated":"2026-10-20T10:24:33Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27982v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27982v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27982v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27974v1","title":"Media Intelligence Platform: Multimodal Detection of Synthetic and Manipulated Content","abstract":"The rapid progress of generative artificial intelligence has led to a significant increase in manipulated multimedia content, including synthetic faces, forged videos, AI-generated images, fake audio, and locally edited images. Existing detection approaches are typically designed for a single manipulation type, limiting their applicability in real-world scenarios involving heterogeneous media sources. In this work, we present the Media Intelligence Platform (MIP), a unified web-based framework for multimodal media forensics integrating specialized modules for fake video detection, face morphing detection, AI-generated image detection, audio deepfake detection, localized image manipulation detection, and text analysis. Its modular architecture enables flexible integration of heterogeneous models and supports future extensions for emerging manipulation techniques. Experimental results on representative benchmark datasets demonstrate reliable detection performance across multiple media modalities while providing a practical and user-friendly interface for forensic investigations. The platform highlights the potential of unified multimodal systems for comprehensive deepfake detection in real-world environments, and is accompanied by a public demonstrator released with this workshop submission to support reproducibility and community evaluation.","authors":["David Fischinger","Marcel Hasenbalg","Silvia Poletti","Davide Antonutti","Mina Schütz","Lam Pham","Justin Ilyes","Péter Rjabcsenkó","Simon Freitter","Martin Boyer"],"published":"2026-10-20T10:18:49Z","updated":"2026-10-20T10:18:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27974v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27974v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27974v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27960v1","title":"Efficient SAM3 Adaptation for Multi-Class Semantic Segmentation via Dense Competitive Representations","abstract":"The Segment Anything Model 3 (SAM3) has achieved significant progress in Promptable Concept Segmentation (PCS) by processing short noun phrases to generate segmentation masks with unique instance identifiers. While SAM3 excels at single-concept segmentation, it falls short in complex, real-world environments where multiple concepts must be segmented simultaneously. In particular, SAM3 typically fails to simultaneously process concepts with semantic overlap, leading to semantic misclassifications and redundant mask predictions. Moreover, as the number of concepts increases, the independent inference paradigm introduces prohibitive inference latency. To address these limitations, we propose the Dense Competitive Representations SAM (DCR-SAM) framework. By decoupling cross-modal interactions, the proposed method alleviates the inference latency. To resolve semantic misclassifications, DCR-SAM dynamically aligns textual concepts with visual representations and incorporates a dense competition head to enforce explicit inter-class competition. Furthermore, a learnable background token is applied to absorb non-target objects dynamically. These mechanisms effectively suppress overlapping predictions and generate high-fidelity masks. Extensive experimental evaluations across multiple benchmarks demonstrate that the proposed framework: (1) achieves a 2.3% $\\sim$ 10.7% performance improvement over state-of-the-art methods, (2) delivers a 33$\\times$ inference speedup compared to the native SAM3, and (3) exhibits strong robustness across diverse scenarios.","authors":["Wenbin Liao","Hao Zhu","Yike Ma","Hao Jiang","Feng Dai"],"published":"2026-10-20T10:06:19Z","updated":"2026-10-20T10:06:19Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27960v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27960v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27960v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27958v1","title":"From Proper Scoring Rules and Edge Biases to Mumford-Shah Models: Robust Unsupervised Hyperspectral Image Segmentation","abstract":"Hyperspectral images provide a rich representation of the underlying spectrum for each pixel, allowing for a pixel-wise classification/segmentation into different classes. As the availability of labeled hyperspectral images is limited and the acquisition of labeled training data is time-consuming, unsupervised segmentation methods are important in hyperspectral image segmentation. Spectral variability, mixed pixels, noise as well as high correlation between neighboring spectral bands make this task challenging. We present a feature-agnostic, unsupervised hyperspectral segmentation framework based on the piecewise-constant Mumford-Shah functional. Our framework turns a strictly proper scoring rule and a family of parametric probability distributions into an indicator function for the Mumford-Shah functional, and we naturally generalize some previous Mumford-Shah based models for image segmentation this way. Our focus will be on heavy-tailed elliptical distributions, the negative log-likelihood score and the score based on the beta-divergence to deal with the heavy outliers in hyperspectral imaging. Using the interplay between the calculus of variations and statistics, we also propose a robustness step to estimate the statistical parameters of these heavy-tailed elliptical distributions. In addition, an edge bias, which contains prior boundary information, is incorporated into both the data fidelity term and the perimeter term of the Mumford-Shah functional. Numerical experiments show that, to the best of our knowledge, our method achieves the highest overall accuracy among fully unsupervised methods on three of the four standard benchmarks (Indian Pines, Kennedy Space Center, Pavia University and Salinas) evaluated on the full scenes. It also improves upon our previous Mumford-Shah based model.","authors":["Fabian Conrad","Benjamin Berkels"],"published":"2026-10-20T10:04:28Z","updated":"2026-10-20T10:04:28Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27958v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27958v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27958v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27947v1","title":"Edit in Sync: Unified End-to-End Audio-Visual Editing for Human-Centric Videos","abstract":"Human-centric audio-visual editing requires precise alignment of lip movements, gestures, and expressions with speech. Existing methods process inputs through independent video and audio pipelines, preventing cross-modal interaction between visual editing and audio dubbing. Many of these pipelines further rely on mask-based inpainting. As a result, they suffer from poor lip-audio synchronization, cumulative pipeline errors, and an inherent mask-size dilemma. We present AVE, the first unified end-to-end framework that jointly performs reference-guided person swapping, insertion, and deletion across both audio and video modalities without requiring spatial masks. To overcome data scarcity, we design a synthesis pipeline and introduce AVE-Dataset, the first audio-visual editing dataset to provide 100K pairs with appearance and voice references. By combining Synthetic-to-Real reconstruction with preliminary audio warm-up, our framework transcends synthetic pipeline limits and mitigates audio-video token imbalance. AVE outperforms modality-separate audio-visual editing pipelines in editing realism, temporal consistency, and audio-visual alignment.","authors":["Ziye Yuan","Minghong Cai","Xu He","Ruchang Yao","Xiao Fu","Kaixin Ding","Ming Zhang","Xiangyu Du","Tianpeng Lan","Da Xie","Xiangyu Yue"],"published":"2026-10-20T09:53:32Z","updated":"2026-10-20T09:53:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27947v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27947v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27947v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27946v1","title":"Decoupling Action from Egocentric Observation for World Simulation","abstract":"Egocentric action transfer aims to decouple the semantic action from egocentric observations and reproduce them in new visual contexts, enabling scalable world simulation, embodied policy learning, and robotic data generation. Existing approaches to transferring actions rely on condition-guided video generation, which converts the observation video into explicit geometric controls (e.g., hand poses or meshes) to drive synthesis. However, these methods introduce geometric estimation errors, making it difficult to preserve physically plausible interactions when transferred to new scenes. Alternatively, motion transfer methods directly extract motion patterns from the observation video, yet motion-level features alone cannot encode rich contact dynamics, frequently leading to severe hand structural collapse and implausible contact layout. To address both limitations, we present EgoACT, a test-time framework for egocentric action transfer that operates directly on the denoising process of video diffusion models without requiring additional geometric estimators. EgoACT uses Velocity-guided Structure Anchoring to stabilize reference-consistent hand-object structure in the early denoising stage, and Sparse Correspondence Calibration to refine reliable local correspondences in the mid-to-late denoising stages. Together, these two components preserve transferable action semantics while improving temporal coherence and interaction realism. We further establish EgoActionBench, a benchmark for evaluating action preservation, visual quality, and hand-object plausibility across diverse egocentric manipulation scenarios. Experiments show that EgoACT generates more coherent and physically plausible interaction videos than strong baselines.","authors":["Yue Ma","Pengjie Song","Xinyu Wang","Yi He","Zeqian Long","Fangneng Zhan","Kaichen Zhou","Hongyu Liu","Hongfa Wang","Peihao Li","Haoyang Huang","Nan Duan","Qifeng Chen"],"published":"2026-10-20T09:52:46Z","updated":"2026-10-20T09:52:46Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27946v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27946v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27946v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27944v1","title":"In PRAISE of Attributes: Grounding Retrieval Geometry with Query-Conditioned Subspaces for Person Re-Identification","abstract":"Person re-identification (ReID) ranks an image gallery using either an image query or a textual query. In image-to-image (I2I) ReID, viewpoint, illumination, occlusion, and background changes can make distractors appear more similar than the true match. In text-to-image (T2I) ReID, incomplete or ambiguous descriptions may omit key visual evidence. We study this problem for vision-language model (VLM) based ReID, where attributes such as clothing color and carried objects provide useful fine-grained matching cues. However, directly using attribute-prompt similarities as retrieval scores is unreliable because templated prompts can be biased, correlated, and poorly calibrated. We therefore propose \\textbf{PR}ompt-guided \\textbf{A}ttribute-based \\textbf{I}nference-time \\textbf{S}imilarity \\textbf{E}nhancement (\\texttt{PRAISE}), a query-side test-time adaptation framework for VLM-based ReID. Instead of treating prompts as scores, \\texttt{PRAISE} uses a transferable attribute-prompt bank to construct an \\emph{Attribute-Conditioned Subspace} (ACS): a query-dependent low-rank subspace induced by the most compatible attribute prompts. ACS provides a controlled attribute-conditioned geometry for reshaping query--gallery affinities and guiding lightweight inference-time updates without identity or attribute labels. Experiments across multiple I2I- and T2I-ReID benchmarks show that \\texttt{PRAISE} consistently improves VLM retrieval backbones and outperforms both generic and ReID-specific TTA baselines.","authors":["Rajarshi Bhattacharya","Aryan Shukla","Shakeeb Murtaza","Christian Desrosiers","Jose Dolz","Maguelonne Heritier","Eric Granger"],"published":"2026-10-20T09:52:06Z","updated":"2026-10-20T09:52:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27944v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27944v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27944v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27914v1","title":"World Models with Configurable Perception","abstract":"Real-world observations are noisy and often contain excessive task-irrelevant information. Effective world modeling and planning need to abstract observations into world state representations while filtering out irrelevant information. However, encoders in existing world models are typically non-adaptive, leading to state representations that are easily distracted by task-irrelevant information. As a result, the model may not selectively filter observations to cater to different task demands. To address this problem, we introduce ConfigWM, which realizes configurable perception through task-conditioned state extraction. Given a language description of the task at hand, ConfigWM uses it as a configuration signal to steer how visual embeddings are mapped into a task-specific state space, selectively preserving information relevant to the current task while suppressing task-irrelevant variations. Action-conditioned dynamics are then learned in this task-specific state space for planning. We evaluate ConfigWM in environments with increasing levels of distraction. While existing methods exhibit substantial declines in planning success rate as the environments become increasingly noisy, ConfigWM remains comparatively stable and outperforms the strongest competing method by 22.3% on average across diverse environments. Consistent gains in physical-quantity probing (+0.365 Pearson r) and distracted-to-clean retrieval (+75.5%) further indicate that ConfigWM learns states that preserve task-relevant information while remaining robust to distractors, supporting reliable world modeling and planning in noisy worlds. Project Page: https://configwm.github.io","authors":["Yijun Shen","Delong Chen","Xianming Hu","Kai Zhang","Pascale Fung"],"published":"2026-10-20T09:26:32Z","updated":"2026-10-20T09:26:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27914v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27914v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27914v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27904v1","title":"Staged Continual Adaptation of Multimodal Foundation Models for Japanese Financial Documents","abstract":"Staged post-training of multimodal foundation models chains vision-language alignment, reasoning distillation, and domain tuning, yet how each phase trades capabilities against the others remains under-characterised. Tracking an 8.4B-parameter model from an un-fine-tuned baseline through three training phases on Japanese financial documents (evaluated on five text-input benchmarks), we find the phases interact non-additively: math-only distillation overwrites the financial knowledge acquired earlier even as procedural math skill survives, and the domain phase repairs more than it installs. A skip-phase ablation is best for domain knowledge but forfeits numerical-reasoning gains, so per-phase contributions are not decomposable and the best checkpoint is task-dependent, with implications for checkpoint retention and scheduling rather than added stages.","authors":["Genshin Kakimoto","Atsushi Yanagisawa"],"published":"2026-10-20T09:19:36Z","updated":"2026-10-20T09:19:36Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27904v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27904v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27904v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27894v1","title":"Testing Associative Creativity in Humans and Multimodal LLMs with Difficult-to-Name Visual Stimuli","abstract":"We investigate associative creativity in descriptions of abstract visual stimuli by humans and multimodal large language models (MLLMs). While conceptual association is central to human communication, it remains unclear how humans and models adapt when visual stimuli become increasingly difficult to map onto familiar semantic concepts. We evaluate several state-of-the-art MLLMs and human participants along two dimensions of creativity, originality and effectiveness, using two datasets with controlled manipulations of difficulty. SilCC varies naming difficulty through progressively more complex combinations of abstract shapes, and PixCC varies difficulty through increasing levels of random visual noise. Results show that humans produce more diverse associative descriptions than MLLMs, including greater semantic distance, higher entropy, and more novel expressions relative to reference lexica. As difficulty increases, effectiveness declines in SilCC, and humans increasingly shift from holistic associations to descriptions of low-level visual properties in PixCC.","authors":["Manar Ali","Lisa Gottschalk","Simeon Junker","Lisa Hudalla","Alon Fishman","Joana Cholin","Stefan Kopp","Hendrik Buschmeier","Sina Zarrieß"],"published":"2026-10-20T09:13:16Z","updated":"2026-10-20T09:13:16Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27894v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27894v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27894v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27890v1","title":"EndoWorld-JEPA: Motion-Guided Global-Local Latent World Modeling for Endoscopic Video Representation Learning","abstract":"Endoscopic video contains state transitions induced by camera displacement, deformable tissue, and instrument interaction, yet existing pre-training methods treat dynamics implicitly. Inspired by action-conditioned joint-embedding predictive world models and by the observation that endoscopic optical flow contains both shared image-plane displacement and localized residual motion, we reformulate passive endoscopic video pre-training as causal motion-guided latent-state prediction. We present EndoWorld-JEPA, to our knowledge the first latent world-model pre-training framework designed for general-purpose endoscopic video representation learning. In place of recorded actions, calibrated poses, or robot kinematics, the model conditions prediction of the next latent state on motion observed up to the current state. Its Optical Motion-guided module separates dominant displacement from residual motion and converts the resulting evidence into an ordered temporal motion condition and spatial local-motion weights. Complementary global and motion-local predictors use these outputs: the temporal condition modulates both latent transitions, whereas the local weights define an auxiliary motion-local state that extends prediction from scene-level evolution to spatially selective dynamics. Patch-wise motion weighting emphasizes localized change while retaining anatomical context, and past-motion-window dropout randomly withholds the complete motion condition to reduce dependence on unreliable motion. This global--local formulation learns predictable visual--motion dynamics in representation space without reconstructing stochastic appearance artifacts. Evaluation shows that a common pre-trained checkpoint achieves strong transfer across four downstream benchmarks, supporting motion-guided global--local latent prediction as an effective pre-training paradigm for endoscopic video.","authors":["Jie Wang","Shilong Yang","Tao Zhou","Juan Yu","Rong Qu","Jianfeng Ren","Zhicheng Zhang","Linlin Shen"],"published":"2026-10-20T09:11:49Z","updated":"2026-10-20T09:11:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27890v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27890v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27890v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27882v1","title":"S$^3$E-Det: A Strict Spatial-Spectral Equivariant Detector for Multispectral Object Detection","abstract":"Multispectral object detection requires detectors to exploit material-dependent spectral responses while maintaining consistent localization under geometric variations. Existing MOD detectors learn discriminative spectral representations, yet lack a unified mechanism that preserves spectral order, adapts spectral-context fusion across pyramid levels, and regulates geometric responses under rotation. To address these limitations, we propose S$^3$E-Det, a strict spatial-spectral equivariant detector that couples ordered spectral modeling with transformation-consistent prediction. Spectral Order-aware Sampling Calibration (SOSC) imposes local order constraints on input-derived spectral features through first-order variation, while Common-Differential Equivariant Fusion (CDEF) adapts spectral-context aggregation across pyramid levels. A compact Geometric Alignment Head (GAH) reconciles invariant and equivariant output properties within a unified prediction stage. Across MODA, HOD3K, and DroneVehicle, S$^3$E-Det combines high detection accuracy with a compact parameter footprint and reduced rotation-induced variation. Code is available at https://github.com/Riiluo/S3E-Det.","authors":["Peng Zhang","Tingfa Xu","Jinrui Sui","Yuyang Tian","Jianan Li"],"published":"2026-10-20T09:06:01Z","updated":"2026-10-20T09:06:01Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27882v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27882v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27882v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27877v1","title":"Gaze2HOI: Gaze-Aware Intention Disambiguation for Egocentric Hand-Object Interaction Forecasting","abstract":"Anticipating how users will interact with their surroundings is essential for proactive systems that provide timely assistance in AR, VR, and embodied applications. Prior work typically forecasts hand-object interactions directly from historical observations, leaving intention and contact geometry implicit. However, similar observation histories can precede semantically or geometrically distinct futures. Inspired by behavioral evidence that gaze can provide early cues for underlying intention, we propose Gaze2HOI, a gaze-aware framework that formulates hand-object interaction forecasting as a hierarchical problem comprising intention inference, contact grounding, and coupled hand-object motion generation. Specifically, Gaze2HOI first weights gaze cues by fixation strength and recency, and uses them to modulate the visual representations of a vision-language model (VLM) through Temporal Gaze Saliency Modulation (TGSM), thereby reducing semantic ambiguity in intention inference. Subsequently, an Anticipatory Contact Grounding (ACG) module maps these structured intentions into explicit contact, support, and placement fields, effectively mitigating geometric ambiguity. Driven by these explicit conditions, a diffusion-based model jointly generates the 6-DoF object trajectories and future articulated motion of both hands. To evaluate Gaze2HOI, we further introduce EgoAIM, a phase-aware benchmark that organizes egocentric hand-object interactions into four phases defined by evolving hand-object relations and provides fine-grained intention annotations. Extensive experiments demonstrate that Gaze2HOI achieves state-of-the-art performance in both intention inference and motion forecasting, even approaching oracle-level end-to-end performance in specific interaction phases. Project page: https://gaze2hoi.github.io/.","authors":["Chuanyang Ma","Chenye Wang","Yixuan Wang","Huiling Zhou","Xingqun Qi","Muyi Sun","Man Zhang","Caifeng Shan","Zhenan Sun"],"published":"2026-10-20T08:59:54Z","updated":"2026-10-20T08:59:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27877v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27877v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27877v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27851v1","title":"LaLa-World: Scaling Latent World Models with Natural Language Actions","abstract":"This paper shows that JEPA-style latent world models (LWMs) can learn across diverse domains and data sources by adopting the language-conditioned interface of video generation models. We establish this approach as an effective and efficient basis for planning with imagination and downstream policy fine-tuning. To this end, we introduce LaLa-World, a Language-conditioned Latent World model that learns from simulation and robot trajectories together with broader image and video data. Two designs make this work. First, we describe both numerical actions and visual changes in language, enabling joint training of a single future predictor across heterogeneous datasets. Second, we build the predictor on a language model, allowing it to interpret language conditions while predicting future latent states. The resulting predictor achieves higher average MPC planning performance than existing LWMs trained separately for each domain or task. Adding image and video data further improves average planning performance and robustness to action phrasing. In a matched comparison with an RAEv2-based video generation model baseline, LaLa-World reaches comparable planning performance with $6\\times$ less training cost and $13.9\\times$ faster planning. Beyond planning, the pretrained LaLa-World serves as an initialization for LaLa-Policy, which jointly predicts actions and future visual representations. LaLa-Policy performs competitively with Cosmos Policy on LIBERO and RoboCasa, with up to $5.5\\times$ faster inference.","authors":["Junha Song","Geonmo Gu","Jeongeun Park","Heejeong Nam","Lucas Maes","Byeongho Heo","Dongyoon Han","Sangdoo Yun"],"published":"2026-10-20T08:32:14Z","updated":"2026-10-20T08:32:14Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27851v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27851v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27851v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"}],"favorites":[],"latest":{"generated_at_utc":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","category":"cs.CV","keywords":["video","retrieval","3d","agent","representation","instance","multimodal","diffusion"],"fetch_window_hours":72,"daily_window_hours":30,"match_count":75,"new_or_recent_papers":[{"id":"2610.28632v1","title":"Joints, Camera, Action! Co-Generating 3D Motion and Video for Arbitrary Rigs","abstract":"Video generation models can now depict remarkably realistic motion. Yet it is unclear how to use this motion beyond pixels: to animate a specific 3D asset and replay the animation in its 3D scene. For instance, a video model can show a monkey jumping over a crossbar, but it cannot return that jump as replayable 3D motion placed in the 3D scene. Here we present a model that directly co-generates the video and 3D motion for any supplied rig, along with a camera that registers this motion to the scene. Prior video-motion models either handle only human skeletons, often adding billions of parameters, or triangulate 3D motion from generated 2D pose maps. We instead keep 3D motion in a separate model that supports arbitrary skeletons, and connect it to a pretrained video model through a small set of cross-attention layers. This separation lets the motion model first learn how skeletons move from motion-only data, which is easier to obtain and augment than paired video. Additionally, our model learns to locate each joint in the video frames, which lets us recover the camera and place the 3D motion in the scene. To train across many skeletons, we use a generative video editor to place animated 3D assets in scenes suited to their actions, curating the edits so their known motion remains valid. Together with human and robot video, this yields nearly 200K training pairs. Our evaluations demonstrate that our model animates diverse rigs from text more faithfully than video-first pipelines, and its motion responds to the surrounding scene. It also turns video into rig motion, rig motion into video, and enables keyframe-based control of the 3D motion. Videos and additional results are available on our project page: https://research.nvidia.com/labs/par/projects/jca/.","authors":["Noam Rotstein","Yoad Tewel","Chen Tessler","Haggai Maron","Gal Chechik","Or Litany","Yoni Kasten","Yuval Atzmon"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:59:56Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:59:56Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28632v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28632v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28632v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":93581,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Video generation models can now depict remarkably realistic motion. Yet it is unclear how to use this motion beyond pixels: to animate a specific 3D asset and replay the animation in its 3D scene. For instance, a video model can show a monkey jumping over a c...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28631v1","title":"AEON: Unifying Video and 3D World Models","abstract":"Current world models for explicit world simulation typically follow two distinct pathways: (1) video generative models (e.g., Sora), which simulate realistic appearances but fail to preserve 3D consistency; and (2) 3D generative models (e.g., Marble), which generate geometrically consistent scenes but often yield static, less realistic observations. We present AEON, a unified framework that bridges video-based and 3D-based world models and combines the benefits of both. The key insight of AEON lies in a generative model that operates within the text- or image-conditioned video latent space while producing renderable 4D graphics primitives---specifically, 4D Gaussian Splatting (4DGS)---as the explicit world representation. We train a 4D reconstruction model to predict both 4DGS and camera poses from monocular videos, serving as our initialized 4D decoder. Furthermore, we design a domain adapter that translates generative video latents into reconstructive 4D latents through a distillation process on the video VAE. AEON naturally supports video-based world modeling by rendering 4DGS at predicted camera trajectories, and constructs a persistent 4D environment with scene dynamics for realistic world simulation. Consequently, AEON empowers a diverse range of world modeling applications and achieves state-of-the-art performance on standard benchmarks. Project Page: https://weiqi-zhang.github.io/AEON","authors":["Weiqi Zhang","Wenyuan Zhang","Yu-Shen Liu","Junsheng Zhou"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:59:51Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:59:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28631v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28631v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28631v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":70472,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Current world models for explicit world simulation typically follow two distinct pathways: (1) video generative models (e.g., Sora), which simulate realistic appearances but fail to preserve 3D consistency; and (2) 3D generative models (e.g., Marble), which g...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28630v1","title":"SolarRenderer: Few-Step Generative Reference Rendering","abstract":"Game engines and coding agents can specify the layout, motion, and camera of a scene, yet turning such coarse structure into realistic video requires a renderer that follows the structure and completes the appearance it leaves open. Existing renderers are tied to the representation of one engine, need tens of denoising steps or a costly distillation of their own, and learn from conditions that are not aligned with their target videos. We present SolarRenderer, a structure-conditioned video renderer that adapts a frozen MiniMax-H3 33B backbone with two lightweight LoRAs. Structural videos enter the native reference pathway of the backbone at the positions of the frames they describe, without a type identifier and described only in plain text, so that a single control LoRA follows arbitrary structural representations. Starting from a pretrained four-step LoRA, structure-guided distribution matching renders in four steps, 12.3 times faster than 50-step sampling, while preserving the learned control. A block-causal variant extends the same design to streaming and renders 768p video in real time. To train the model, we build a frame-aligned corpus of 136K clips from game engines, simulators, and YouTube videos. Experiments on an in-distribution test set and a generalization benchmark of 16 structural representations show that SolarRenderer achieves state-of-the-art results across both, and generalizes to 11 unseen representations, including pose skeletons, drawings, and point clouds.","authors":["Guian Fang","Shengju Qian","Dongyue Lu","Junchao Huang","Lutao Jiang","Xin Wang","Yiren Song","Li Jiang","Mike Zheng Shou"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:59:48Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:59:48Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28630v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28630v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28630v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":117035,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, agent, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Game engines and coding agents can specify the layout, motion, and camera of a scene, yet turning such coarse structure into realistic video requires a renderer that follows the structure and completes the appearance it leaves open. Existing renderers are tie...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28628v1","title":"Learning Adaptable Dexterous Skills via Hybrid Dexterous Mimicking","abstract":"Dexterous multi-finger hands hold promise to unlock diverse skills like functional grasps, in-hand reorientation, and dynamic handover that the human-designed world assumes, yet existing learning recipes force a trade-off. Goal-reaching reinforcement learning generalizes to arbitrary object poses but, lacking any human prior, converges to unnatural and unreliable finger strategies; motion tracking reproduces human-like behavior from a single clip but cannot deviate from it. We present AdaDex, a goal-conditioned RL framework that turns a single hand-object trajectory of a few seconds---from mocap data or HOI reconstruction from in-the-wild RGB(D) video---into an adaptable dexterous skill. At the core of AdaDex is Hybrid Dexterous Mimicking: a shared policy is trained on two populations of massively parallel environments: motion tracking environments trained with dense motion tracking objective, which instill the human prior from human motion; and generalization environments with noised initial states and goals and a sparse object-success reward, which force the skill to work far off the reference. From seconds of human motion per skill, the resulting policies can learn diverse adaptable dexterous skills, such as functional grasping, object reposing, in-hand reorientation with finger gaiting, and dynamic behaviors like throw-and-catch. Our method outperforms baselines in terms of generalization and functionality, and we demonstrate transfer across simulators and to the real world. Project page: https://yuxuank.com/AdaDex/.","authors":["Yuxuan Kuang","Jiashun Wang","Yixuan Sun","Yufei Ye","Sungjae Park","Umar Iqbal","Katerina Fragkiadaki","Shubham Tulsiani"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:59:32Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:59:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28628v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28628v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28628v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":76699,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Dexterous multi-finger hands hold promise to unlock diverse skills like functional grasps, in-hand reorientation, and dynamic handover that the human-designed world assumes, yet existing learning recipes force a trade-off. Goal-reaching reinforcement learning...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28626v1","title":"UniWALM: Unified World Action Language Model for Embodied Robot Control","abstract":"Motivated by the human ability to integrate experience across language, perception, and action, we introduce UniWALM, a unified world-action-language model that learns video, action, and language in a single shared representation, where each modality is both a conditioning signal and a supervision target. We ask: can jointly generating language enrich this shared representation, improving manipulation and supporting memory-dependent behavior? Unlike prior approaches that attach a reasoning or vision-language model to a video-action backbone and run them as distinct stages, UniWALM unifies all three modalities inside a single mixture-of-transformers backbone whose per-modality diffusion heads co-denoise synchronously, decoding subtask and textual memory in the same pass as action with no separate planning stage; the generated language is re-consumed on the next step, closing a language-as-memory loop without an external planner or memory bank. To keep the action stream reliable under this richer objective, we further introduce Flow Matching with Correction (FMC), a training objective that teaches recovery from perturbed action trajectories at no inference-time cost. UniWALM consistently improves over same-backbone baselines (e.g., FastWAM) on compositional pick-and-place (MolmoSpaces) and across the memory-dependent subset of RMBench, where it reaches 34.5% without any pretrained language or reasoning model and exceeds Mem-0's keyframe-equipped setup (28.5%) using only a single anchor frame. On a real-world Find-the-Item task, generating textual memory lifts success from 11.1% to 61.1%, while state-of-the-art VLAs stay at or below chance (<=33.3%).","authors":["Rahul Sajnani","Diego Romeres","Mingyuan Zhang","Kai Gao","Carl Qi","Baichuan Huang","Srinath Sridhar","Dylan Randle","Lillian J. Ratliff","Jose Barreiros","Manikantan Nambi","Jeroen Vanbaar"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:59:04Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:59:04Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28626v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28626v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28626v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":101637,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, representation, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Motivated by the human ability to integrate experience across language, perception, and action, we introduce UniWALM, a unified world-action-language model that learns video, action, and language in a single shared representation, where each modality is both...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28625v1","title":"PsycheMME: A Psychology-Informed Benchmark for Social Understanding and Interaction in Videos","abstract":"Understanding human needs and selecting responses require distinct judgments that aggregate accuracy can obscure. We introduce PsycheMME, a video benchmark informed by psychology, with eight capabilities spanning grounding, states, needs, interaction decisions, memory, and risk. Drawing on Social Information Processing and the Perception-Action Cycle, this organization separates human understanding from response selection. We construct 1,000 questions from 630 episodes through independent answering with proposed references withheld and expert review. Our Reasoning-Verified protocol weights answer credit by the support and logical consistency of explicit rationales against annotated cues. Across 17 models, Timing has the lowest Verified scores and credit retention, with weaker support judgments for correct Timing answers. Models with similar Need scores still differ in answer completeness on open questions. Input controls and SFT/GRPO examine context dependence and adaptation. Evaluating answers alongside rationales locates where models fail across understanding, response selection, and justification.","authors":["Heran Hu","Yuhang Dong","Zhucun Xue","Kaiwei Chen","Yong Liu","Jiangning Zhang"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:59:00Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:59:00Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28625v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28625v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28625v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":110610,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Understanding human needs and selecting responses require distinct judgments that aggregate accuracy can obscure. We introduce PsycheMME, a video benchmark informed by psychology, with eight capabilities spanning grounding, states, needs, interaction decision...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28615v1","title":"KoalaGS: Complexity-Aware Continuous Level of Detail for Gaussian Splatting","abstract":"Deploying a trained 3D Gaussian Splatting scene under shrinking compute budgets requires one primitive set to remain effective when rendered at smaller prefixes. In this continuous level of detail (LoD) setting, the challenge is to decide which Gaussians should retain capacity as the budget decreases. A pilot study on 3DGS-MCMC reconstructions shows that image complexity, computed from the input views before optimization, predicts where reconstruction error concentrates in image and 3D space. It also reveals that simple regions contain too many Gaussians and degrade as much as complex ones at lower budgets, despite being inherently cheaper to represent. Subsequently, in KoalaGS, during training we use this complexity prior to bias relocation toward poorly reconstructed areas while ensuring smooth degradation across LoDs. A primitive importance score then enables inference at low-capacity, while preserving the scene structure. Tested across three datasets, KoalaGS maintains competitive full-capacity quality while significantly improving quality across the budget spectrum, yielding smoother transitions across LoDs, and enabling faster progressive streaming.","authors":["Radu Beche","Raoul de Charette"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:55:06Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:55:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28615v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28615v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28615v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":82161,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Deploying a trained 3D Gaussian Splatting scene under shrinking compute budgets requires one primitive set to remain effective when rendered at smaller prefixes. In this continuous level of detail (LoD) setting, the challenge is to decide which Gaussians shou...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28598v1","title":"Neural Implicit Energy Optimization for LiDAR Mapping","abstract":"Neural implicit representations provide memory-efficient signed distance field (SDF) parameterizations for large-scale LiDAR scenes, but the geometry recovered from sparse and often noisy inputs carries jitter and artifacts. Classical geometric optimization techniques resolve such artifacts with surface energy functionals, whose minimizers correspond to smooth surfaces. Motivated by this principle, we formulate neural LiDAR mapping as the optimization of energy functionals over the parameter space of neural implicit representations. Specifically, we regularize the signed distance field with an energy functional defined on the shape operator of its level set, which represents the curvature of the surface, thus encouraging smoothness along the surface. This adapts classical geometric priors to the scale of million-point LiDAR maps. Experiments on large-scale datasets show reconstructions with improved smoothness at preserved geometric fidelity, and no additional inference-time cost. The code is publicly available at \\href{https://github.com/HrishikeshVish/nieo}{https://github.com/HrishikeshVish/nieo}","authors":["Hrishikesh Viswanath","Md Ashiqur Rahman","Chi Lin","Damon Conover","Aniket Bera"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:47:07Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:47:07Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28598v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28598v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28598v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":77635,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Neural implicit representations provide memory-efficient signed distance field (SDF) parameterizations for large-scale LiDAR scenes, but the geometry recovered from sparse and often noisy inputs carries jitter and artifacts. Classical geometric optimization t...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28594v1","title":"Learning the 4D World through Point Trajectory Forecasting","abstract":"Reliable multi-view forecasting requires videos from different cameras to describe the same evolving 3D scene. To this end, we introduce Video-Point Model (VPM), a world model that conditions on multi-view video histories and a language instruction to jointly forecast future videos from arbitrary viewpoints and the 3D trajectories of persistent scene points. We extend a pretrained world model with camera-conditioned multi-view processing that integrates complementary observations across cameras, and add a trajectory head that predicts how physical points move through time. Each trajectory tracks a single point across all views, and supervising these trajectories alongside video generation therefore anchors appearance changes in every camera to a shared description of 3D motion. To support training and evaluation, we build VPM-Data, a simulation corpus pairing synchronized multi-view videos with persistent point trajectories, and VP-WorldBench, a forecasting benchmark spanning simulated and real environments. We further extend VPM to robot control by adding robot actions as a token modality in the generator. Action tokens share the point-supervised backbone and are denoised jointly with future videos, thereby grounding the policy in the same 3D-aware representation. On VP-WorldBench and existing 4D generation benchmarks, point supervision improves both video fidelity and 3D motion accuracy over a video-only counterpart, and in simulated and real-robot experiments, it also raises the manipulation success of the action-enabled model.","authors":["Byungwoo Jeon","Sunghwan Hong","Dohyeon Kim","Jonghoon Lee","Jungwoo Park","Inhee Lee","Doosan Baek","Jinil Kim","Taehyun Lim","Hanbyul Joo","Marc Pollefeys","Saining Xie","Jinwoo Shin"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:45:45Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:45:45Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28594v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28594v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28594v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":142071,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Reliable multi-view forecasting requires videos from different cameras to describe the same evolving 3D scene. To this end, we introduce Video-Point Model (VPM), a world model that conditions on multi-view video histories and a language instruction to jointly...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28585v1","title":"SceneRig: An Agentic System for Simulation-Ready 3D Scene Reconstruction from Single Images","abstract":"Simulation is a powerful tool for robotics, but building diverse, simulation-ready scenes at scale remains difficult. Reconstructing scenes from single images is a promising alternative, but visual similarity alone is insufficient: objects must also be recovered, accurately placed, and physically grounded. Existing methods that rely on post-hoc physics settling often misplace objects, producing scenes that look correct but behave poorly under manipulation. We present SceneRig, an agentic system that turns a single image into an editable, simulation-ready scene. SceneRig combines geometric, visual, and physical tools to reconstruct a faithful 3D scene. An agent then performs edit-and-validate loops inside a physics simulator to achieve physical stability. Across diverse inputs, SceneRig improves instance segmentation, object-centric appearance, and 3D placement while keeping the reconstructed scenes physically stable. Furthermore, SceneRig is effective for downstream robotics: its scenes predict real-robot policy outcomes more accurately than the baseline method and more reliably support replay of recorded manipulations without trajectory adaptation.","authors":["Rundong Luo","Yunong Liu","Di Cao","Matthew Tancik","Shyamal Buch","Colton Stearns","Wenqi Xian"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:39:42Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:39:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28585v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28585v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28585v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent","instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":138992,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d, agent, instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Simulation is a powerful tool for robotics, but building diverse, simulation-ready scenes at scale remains difficult. Reconstructing scenes from single images is a promising alternative, but visual similarity alone is insufficient: objects must also be recove...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28567v1","title":"Continual Concept Personalization in Diffusion Models via Residual-Space Adaptation","abstract":"Text-to-image personalization aims to equip pretrained diffusion models with the ability to learn user-specific concepts from only a few reference images. Existing methods, however, typically train each concept independently or assume that all concepts are available simultaneously. In practical applications, concepts often arrive sequentially, requiring models to continually learn new subjects, objects, or styles while minimizing interference with previously acquired concepts. We address this continual concept personalization setting by extending residual-space adaptation to the stochastic adaptation process of pretrained diffusion models. Our work achieves the highest concept fidelity among competing continual personalization methods while maintaining near-zero forgetting. Beyond these empirical gains, the learned residual-space dynamics reveal where adaptation capacity is concentrated within the diffusion model. Restricting adaptation to the identified layers by our method preserves nearly the same performance while reducing the number of trainable parameters by up to 72\\%, demonstrating that residual-space dynamics can identify the layers that contribute most to continual concept personalization and directly guide more parameter-efficient adaptation.","authors":["Yuzhi Liu","Enis Simsar","Giulia Lanzillotta","Thomas Hofmann"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:26:18Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:26:18Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28567v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28567v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28567v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":64881,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Text-to-image personalization aims to equip pretrained diffusion models with the ability to learn user-specific concepts from only a few reference images. Existing methods, however, typically train each concept independently or assume that all concepts are av...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28554v1","title":"OverSeer: Multi-Agent World Models Beyond What Agents See","abstract":"Despite the success of video world models, extending them to multiple agents is difficult because it requires generating several agent-specific video streams that are consistent in time and between agents. To address this gap, we introduce OverSeer, a multi-agent world model that augments egocentric agent views with auxiliary camera views. We suggest that, by fixing the auxiliary views in space, they act as a proxy for a world-centric representation: Unlike the agents' views, which can change entirely due to a simple change in agent position, changes in the auxiliary views directly map to localized changes in the world state. We show that these fixed views substantially help the model to maintain consistency. We also show that, due to their redundant and semi-static nature, the auxiliary views can be efficiently compressed with a delta-VAE that encodes only the changes relative to a static reference image, reducing the number of tokens required to process them. Experiments in Minecraft and Doom demonstrate improved world and agent consistency, with qualitative results on real-world multi-camera captures indicating that the approach is not tied to game engines. Our compression analysis further shows that delta-VAE substantially reduces the computational overhead of auxiliary-view generation. We conclude that these fixed views provide a simple and efficient way to improve multi-agent world models.","authors":["Runjia Li","Kam Woh Ng","Minghao Chen","Junlin Han","Jianyuan Wang","Zhaochong An","Juan-Manuel Perez-Rua","Andrea Vedaldi","Shikun Liu"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:17:59Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:17:59Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28554v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28554v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28554v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":72846,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, agent, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Despite the success of video world models, extending them to multiple agents is difficult because it requires generating several agent-specific video streams that are consistent in time and between agents. To address this gap, we introduce OverSeer, a multi-a...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:22+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28530v1","title":"CoGaussian: Open-Vocabulary Gaussian-based Collaborative 3D Scene Understanding","abstract":"Collaborative perception enables agents to retrieve the scene semantics and geometry beyond the field of view of any individual agent. This broader scene understanding enhances transportation safety and improves efficiency in multi-robot systems. However, existing collaborative semantic occupancy methods rely on dense 3D voxel annotations and are limited to recognizing objects within a predefined set of semantic classes. To overcome these limitations, we present CoGaussian, an open-vocabulary Gaussian-based collaborative semantic occupancy prediction framework. CoGaussian represents a scene as a set of foundation model-aligned sparse 3D Gaussians, which serve as a compact representation for communicating and fusing geometric and semantic information across multiple agents. On the Semantic-OPV2V benchmark, our method outperforms existing open-vocabulary single-agent approaches, highlighting the effectiveness of open-vocabulary collaborative scene understanding.","authors":["Om Shivam Verma","Shaya Farahmand","Lingjun Zhao","Lu Gan"],"published":"2026-10-20T17:04:38Z","updated":"2026-10-20T17:04:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28530v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28530v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28530v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":26494,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d, agent, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Collaborative perception enables agents to retrieve the scene semantics and geometry beyond the field of view of any individual agent. This broader scene understanding enhances transportation safety and improves efficiency in multi-robot systems. However, exi...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:23+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28524v1","title":"Copyright Leakage Detection in AI Generated Videos","abstract":"Video generation models can create realistic-looking video footage from images and text prompts. Some of the recent advances have raised concerns about the reproduction of copyrighted visual content. However, copyright leakage detection in generated videos remains largely unexplored. In this paper, we introduce a new benchmark data set for copyright leakage detection, comprising 50 copyrighted concepts, each with 5 images. For each concept, we have 5 videos created using the image+prompt video generation model and the prompt-only video generation model. Following that, we develop an AI toolset based on a contrastive retrieval framework that formulates copyright leakage detection as a video-to-image retrieval problem by matching aggregated video frame embeddings against a database of copyrighted image embeddings. Our method uses a single similarity threshold and retrieves the most similar copyrighted reference as supporting evidence. We conducted experiments showing strong retrieval and detection performance, achieving 99.6\\% detection accuracy with only a single false negative. These results show the effectiveness of the retrieval-based method for scalable and explainable copyright leakage detection in AI-generated videos. Data can be accessed publicly from https://zenodo.org/records/21386117 and the source code for this project is publicly available at https://github.com/Shaif95/video-cp-Leakage.","authors":["Shaif Chowdhury","Moshiur Rahman Tonmoy"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:59:22Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:59:22Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28524v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28524v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28524v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","retrieval"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":42557,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, retrieval 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Video generation models can create realistic-looking video footage from images and text prompts. Some of the recent advances have raised concerns about the reproduction of copyrighted visual content. However, copyright leakage detection in generated videos re...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:23+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28521v1","title":"Latent Bridge: From Video Latents to 3D Geometry via Feature Alignment","abstract":"Video generation and feed-forward 3D reconstruction typically use separate representations, requiring RGB decoding and visual re-encoding to recover geometry from generated latents. We propose Latent Bridge, which connects video generative latent spaces with geometric feature spaces to directly predict 3D geometry from video latents. We develop representation-specific bridges for Wan2.1 and V-RAE, enabling feed-forward prediction of cameras, depth, and 3D point clouds without the intermediate RGB pathway. Each bridge is trained on RGB videos through feature distillation, while the video encoder and geometry model remain frozen. Training requires neither ground-truth geometry nor execution of the downstream geometric aggregator or prediction heads. At inference, the same video latents support both RGB decoding and geometry prediction. The same trained bridge can be reused for image-to-video and text-to-video generation within the same video latent space, without bridge retraining. Experiments on Co3Dv2 and WildRGB-D demonstrate competitive geometric reconstruction quality, efficient end-to-end generation and reconstruction, and faster latent-to-geometry decoding, highlighting latent alignment as a practical and reusable interface between video generation and 3D reconstruction. Our code is available at: https://github.com/hustvl/Latent-Bridge.","authors":["Shuai Zhang","Zhanpeng Ouyang","Zhengqing Chen","Ruohong Mei","Shaoyu Chen","Weiqiang Ren","Hui Sun","Qian Zhang","Wenyu Liu","Xinggang Wang"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:57:49Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:57:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28521v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28521v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28521v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":60080,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Video generation and feed-forward 3D reconstruction typically use separate representations, requiring RGB decoding and visual re-encoding to recover geometry from generated latents. We propose Latent Bridge, which connects video generative latent spaces with...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:23+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28519v1","title":"Touch-JEPA: Tactile Representation Learning from Hand-Object Video with Limited Tactile Supervision","abstract":"Understanding contact and tactile magnitude in hand-object interaction is a key step toward physically intelligent systems, yet learning such representations is limited by the cost and restricted coverage of tactile measurements. We present Touch-JEPA, a framework for learning tactile representations from hand-object videos with limited tactile supervision. We first introduce a self-supervised pretraining objective that learns from video alone without tactile supervision. Using video-derived hand geometry, Touch-JEPA predicts masked visual features at corresponding hand-surface locations from spatial and temporal context, encouraging the representation to capture latent contact dynamics. We then extend this formulation to joint representation learning, where masked prediction is applied to all videos while tactile supervision is available only for a labeled subset, grounding the shared representation in measured contact and magnitude. With self-supervised pretraining alone, simple supervised readouts on frozen Touch-JEPA features outperform supervised baselines in contact discrimination and tactile magnitude estimation across three human-hand benchmarks, and transfer to robotic hands without robot tactile training data. Joint learning with limited tactile supervision further improves contact and magnitude estimation on the human-hand benchmarks. Together, these results show that hand-object video can provide scalable self-supervision for tactile representation learning, while limited tactile measurements provide complementary physical grounding.","authors":["Minseong Kweon","Qiao Feng","Yiming Huang","Lingjie Liu"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:56:11Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:56:11Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28519v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28519v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28519v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":100625,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Understanding contact and tactile magnitude in hand-object interaction is a key step toward physically intelligent systems, yet learning such representations is limited by the cost and restricted coverage of tactile measurements. We present Touch-JEPA, a fram...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:23+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28508v1","title":"Deep Learning-Based Road Lane Quality Assessment from Egocentric Dashcam Video","abstract":"Reliable lane markings are important for both human drivers and automated driving systems, but their condition is still commonly assessed through manual inspection. Existing autonomous-driving datasets primarily support lane detection and scene understanding and usually lack maintenance-oriented labels for lane-marking visibility, continuity, or degradation. In this study, we present a geo-referenced smartphone dashcam dataset and baseline framework for lane-marking quality assessment. The dataset contains 63,839 road-facing frames collected across South Texas using a GPS-enabled dashcam application. A subset of 3,339 frames is matched with OpenStreetMap-derived lane-count labels, which are used as weak supervision for lane-count regression and map-consistency evaluation. We propose three lane-quality measures: Contrast Stability Index (CSI), which captures lane-background contrast using Laplacian responses; Line Continuity Ratio (LCR), which estimates structural continuity using edge and Hough-line evidence; and Regression Lane Confidence Score (RLCS), which measures agreement between predicted and map-derived lane counts on matched frames. The baseline prediction models attained strong performance, with CSI (MAE $0.039$, $R^2=0.97$) and LCR (MAE $0.093$, $R^2=0.84$). Notably, CSI and LCR are no-reference, segmentation-derived quality metrics that can be computed without dense human labels. In a pilot validation using 50 human-ranked images, these proposed lane-specific metrics show stronger rank agreement with human lane-quality ordering (e.g., Spearman's $ρ=0.7316$ and Kendall's $τ=0.5472$ for LCR) than general image-quality measures such as PSNR, SSIM, BRISQUE, NIQE, and LPIPS. We believe the dataset, metrics, and baseline provide an initial step toward scalable, low-cost, location-aware lane-marking quality assessment. Data and code: https://github.com/Shaif95/Road_Quality","authors":["Shaif Chowdhury","Akhila Yeruvasu","Moshiur Rahman Tonmoy","Ayush Goyal","Dunren Che"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:46:32Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:46:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28508v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28508v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28508v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":42556,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Reliable lane markings are important for both human drivers and automated driving systems, but their condition is still commonly assessed through manual inspection. Existing autonomous-driving datasets primarily support lane detection and scene understanding...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:23+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28502v1","title":"GazeLens for Efficient Egocentric Video Question Answering with Simple Gaze-Driven Adaptive Token Reduction","abstract":"Smart glasses can help users understand their activities and surroundings, but small vision-language models must preserve useful evidence within limited computation. This is difficult because redundant frames consume the visual budget, while aggressive spatial compression can discard relevant details. Although gaze identifies the wearer's focus, the question may require evidence from the surrounding scene. To address these constraints, we propose GazeLens, which combines frame selection with gaze- and question-guided token reduction. First, HOG transitions and image sharpness select frames before visual encoding. For each selected frame, Gaussian pooling then forms gaze-focused and complementary summaries. Because their relevance depends on the question, a lightweight gate blends them into a primary token. Together with a global scene token, this retains both focused and contextual summaries within two tokens per frame. On StreamGaze, GazeLens achieves higher macro and micro accuracy than the evaluated full-grid and token-reduction baselines, with lower input-forward FLOPs than the shared-backbone token-reduction baselines.","authors":["Trong-Thang Pham","Anh Nguyen","Hien Van Nguyen","Ngan Le"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:43:06Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:43:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28502v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28502v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28502v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":57216,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Smart glasses can help users understand their activities and surroundings, but small vision-language models must preserve useful evidence within limited computation. This is difficult because redundant frames consume the visual budget, while aggressive spatia...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:23+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28490v1","title":"Learning from Automatic 3D Gaussian Splatting Edits for Online Bayesian Scene Change Detection","abstract":"Online scene change detection requires integrating incoming observations to determine which regions have changed relative to a reference scene. We update change beliefs as new images arrive using generalised Bayesian evidence fusion. However, the prior probability of change and evidence models are unknown beforehand, and numerical Bayesian inference can be costly. We address these challenges by learning offline from automatic edits to a reference 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) model. These edits generate labelled training views without manual annotations. Together with unchanged reference observations, they provide supervision for learning scene-specific evidence models and estimating the effective pixel prior and response statistics. Online prediction combines current evidence with historical support, while closed-form local Bayes factors, parameterized by the learned response statistics, update an accumulated 3D Gaussian field. Object-guided refinement produces the final change masks. Our method achieves state-of-the-art accuracy on PASLCD and CCS3D, with $67.64\\%$ and $75.82\\%$ mIoU, respectively. On PASLCD, the complete pipeline runs at $17.2$\\,FPS over complete query sequences, the fastest among the evaluated online methods.","authors":["Changrui Zhu","Simon Julier"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:36:39Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:36:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28490v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28490v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28490v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":130477,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Online scene change detection requires integrating incoming observations to determine which regions have changed relative to a reference scene. We update change beliefs as new images arrive using generalised Bayesian evidence fusion. However, the prior probab...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:23+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28476v1","title":"CLOSER: Evidence-Aware Active Visual Verification for Embodied Object-Goal Navigation","abstract":"Object-goal navigation (ObjectNav) requires an embodied agent to find an instance of a specified category in an unseen environment and stop near it. The agent typically relies on an object detector but must judge online from a limited egocentric video stream, sometimes a single frame, whether a detected candidate is the target. Detectors often confidently mistake similar objects, such as a sofa for a bed, and stopping at a false positive fails the episode. Prevailing systems also approach candidates greedily, leaving them occluded or partially observed. We present CLOSER, an evidence-aware active verification framework that treats stopping as closed-loop evidence acquisition, coupling vision-language model (VLM) verification with active perception. CLOSER fine-tunes an open-source VLM to label each view of a candidate tracked across frames as target, non-target, or uncertain, selects complementary views with an embedding-based diversity gate, and fuses their per-view probabilities with a lightweight network. If the fused judgment remains uncertain, a diffusion policy drives the agent toward the candidate using only the tracked mask and depth until the decision becomes decisive. CLOSER runs locally and plugs into existing ObjectNav systems without retraining them. On Gibson and Habitat-Matterport 3D, it raises average success rate by 3.6 points and success weighted by path length by 2.0 points, showing that verifying evidence before committing improves embodied navigation.","authors":["Pengying Wu","Mingye Jiang","Yunze Hu","Zhejia Wen","Kangjie Zhou","Zongru Yang","Zhe Peng","Chang Liu"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:27:33Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:27:33Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28476v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28476v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28476v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","agent","instance","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":76123,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, agent, instance, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Object-goal navigation (ObjectNav) requires an embodied agent to find an instance of a specified category in an unseen environment and stop near it. The agent typically relies on an object detector but must judge online from a limited egocentric video stream,...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:23+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28458v1","title":"LocaLeJEPA: Maximum Entropy Representations under Geometric Priors","abstract":"Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPA) offer an appealing approach to self-supervised learning, but are typically prone to representation collapse. LeJEPA resolves this by introducing a theoretically grounded distribution-matching objective that drives the embedding towards an isotropic Gaussian. However, this approach is fundamentally tied to Euclidean assumptions, and its global target does not reflect domain-specific geometric priors, such as the use of non-Euclidean embedding geometries, e.g., equivariant CNNs or hyperbolic ViTs. We thus introduce LocaLeJEPA, a framework motivated by a manifold-theoretic reformulation of LeJEPA's entropy-maximization property. The accompanying LocaLeReg, a novel locally Euclidean distribution-matching regularizer, enables a stable and scalable implementation of the LocaLeJEPA framework. We then perform extensive experiments in the vision setting on models trained on ImageNet-1K. LocaLeJEPA significantly outperforms LeJEPA when paired with hyperbolic ViT-S/8 and equivariant ResNet-50 backbones, and also with ordinary Euclidean ViT-S/8 and ViT-L/14 backbones. This holds both in terms of linear and k-NN classification performance on ImageNet and on a series of downstream tasks tested. We view the LocaLeJEPA formalism as a principled approach to the JEPA paradigm that better respects intrinsic data geometry while also accommodating generalizations to exotic embedding geometries. Code available at https://github.com/alessioborgi/LocaLeJEPA.","authors":["Kartik Tandon","Alessio Borgi","Pavel Snopov","Inés García-Redondo","Davide D'Ascenzo","Sebastiano Cultrera di Montesano","Fabrizio Silvestri","Pietro Liò","Francesca Dominici","Randall Balestriero","Claudio Battiloro"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:15:06Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:15:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28458v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28458v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28458v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28444v1","title":"EvaOmni: An Evidence-Grounded Benchmark for Audio-Visual Reasoning","abstract":"Omni LLMs can naturally locate key audio and visual evidence and integrate information across modalities for audiovisual reasoning. Existing audio-visual reasoning benchmarks that mainly rely on multiple-choice questions usually score models solely based on answer correctness, making it difficult to assess whether a model truly forms a reasoning chain from evidence to the correct answer. In addition, textual or unimodal shortcuts may allow models to answer correctly without analyzing the necessary audio-visual evidence, further weakening the reliability of evaluation benchmarks. To address these issues, we introduce EvaOmni, an evidence-grounded benchmark containing 392 videos and 1,027 purely human-annotated question-answer pairs, covering 15 capabilities and three levels: retrieval and localization, basic understanding, and complex reasoning. We design a series of rigorous annotation procedures in which experts annotate audio and visual evidence and construct video-based distractors; humans review whether questions meet strict inspection criteria, and finally models filter textual and unimodal shortcuts. Beyond high-quality question-answer pairs, we require models to simultaneously return the answer and supporting evidence intervals in order to evaluate the complete reasoning chain. We propose the Coverage Grounded Score (C.G.) to measure how much annotated evidence a model identifies, and the Bottleneck Grounded Score (B.G.) to penalize poor temporal localization in either modality; both are incorporated into answer correctness. Across 7 open-weight and 17 proprietary Omni LLMs, Gemini3.7-Flash achieved the highest overall accuracy (79.4%), C.G. (70.8%), and B.G. (35.1%). At Level 3, it reached 79.1% accuracy, but its B.G. was only 12.3%. These results show that even the most advanced models still have room for development in grounded audio-visual reasoning.","authors":["Xiaoxing Hu","Wenxi Yao","Chaoyuan Shi","Ziqi Ye","Xiaorong Guo","Peixuan Li","Zhuoran Sun","Ziyang Gong","Peiyuan Zhang","Mingxin Liu","Shuran Ma","Hongbo Liu","Xiaomin Yu","Yu Tian","Wentao Wang","Zhenjie Yang","Jian Hu","Yunhang Shen","Haoyu Cao","Xue Yang"],"published":"2026-10-20T16:03:30Z","updated":"2026-10-20T16:03:30Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28444v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28444v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28444v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","retrieval"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28423v1","title":"WorldSR: Harnessing World Knowledge Search for Grounded Image Super-Resolution","abstract":"Generative-prior-based super-resolution (SR) methods leverage implicit knowledge learned from large-scale models to enhance low-resolution inputs. However, such priors are inherently stochastic and optimized for diverse generation, conflicting with the deterministic, high-fidelity reconstruction required for SR. It is also tied to the base model, costly to update, and prone to hallucinations as real-world data evolves. To address these limitations, we introduce WorldSR, a novel framework that augments SR with explicit, entity-aligned visual world knowledge. WorldSR automatically decomposes the input into semantic entities and performs entity-wise retrieval of structurally and semantically aligned images from a large-scale external corpus. For efficiency and reliability, we incorporate a memory mechanism to reuse previously retrieved results and a multi-step filtering process to refine candidate references. The selected references as world knowledge are then integrated through a novel multi-reference super-resolution model, which provides fine-grained constraints for detail synthesis and structural recovery. Experiments on real-world SR benchmarks show that WorldSR consistently improves structural fidelity and visual quality, outperforming existing generative super-resolution methods. Our results suggest that entity-level grounding in external visual world knowledge provides a simple and effective complement to model-bound generative priors.","authors":["Junyuan Deng","Xinyi Wu","Zhenyao Wu","Song Wang","Yu Wang","Congchao Zhu"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:54:25Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:54:25Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28423v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28423v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28423v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28419v1","title":"DISTRACT: Degradation-Indexed Study of Textual Reference Across Competing Targets","abstract":"Vision-language trackers (VLTs) accept descriptions intended to assist tracking, but accepting text does not establish that its content changes tracking decisions, particularly as visual evidence deteriorates. We introduce DISTRACT, a matched-intervention benchmark that holds video, target, and distractor fixed while crossing four text priors with four image degradations along a controlled severity axis. We evaluate five VLTs on four datasets using behavioral contrasts, layerwise representation probes, and activation patching. Molmo2 and JointNLT exhibit directed text use. Some effects contract under degradation, whereas others show no significant endpoint change after multiplicity correction. UVLTrack, MMTrack, and DUTrack respond only weakly to changes in text semantics, with no directed behavioral effect surviving FDR correction in the tested settings. Model-internal analyses reveal that at decision-near states, correct-versus-conflicting text is decodable in Molmo2 and JointNLT but not in the other three systems. In the responsive models, decodability peaks at late states where replacement produces little behavioral change, whereas replacing selected earlier states redirects conflict-associated trajectories toward the target. Because matched-correct donors do not significantly outperform unrelated donors, these interventions identify earlier intervention-sensitive states without establishing target-specific semantic rescue. DISTRACT thus exposes when and where text affects tracking under degraded visual evidence.","authors":["Dan Zlotnikov","Ohad Ben-Shahar"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:53:23Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:53:23Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28419v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28419v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28419v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28416v1","title":"Code2Video-Compass: A Comprehensive Benchmark for General-Purpose Code-Based Video Generation across T2V, I2V, and V2V","abstract":"Code-based video generation offers fine-grained control, targeted editing, and component reuse compared with direct video generation. This approach requires multimodal large language models (MLLMs) to translate visual and temporal requirements into executable programs that produce complete videos. However, existing code-based benchmarks primarily target specialized tasks, leaving complete video creation across diverse creative categories and input modalities insufficiently evaluated. We introduce Code2Video-Compass, a general-purpose benchmark for code-based video generation across text-to-video (T2V), image-to-video (I2V), and video-to-video (V2V) settings. Code2Video-Compass comprises 2,104 tasks spanning 10 categories, 24 subtypes, and three complexity dimensions covering construction, dynamic animation, and state dependency. It combines tasks curated from textual instructions and visual references with corresponding T2V, I2V, and V2V tasks derived from complete professional and user-created videos. We propose a dual-level evaluation framework combining 12 objective metrics and five subjective metrics. Objective evaluation covers operational performance, video quality, text-video alignment, and reference fidelity, while subjective evaluation uses an MLLM judge to evaluate instruction following and holistic video quality. Extensive evaluation of 15 recent proprietary and open-weight MLLMs from nine families reveals that even leading models face challenges in reliably generating high-quality complete videos aligned with textual and visual requirements, with performance generally declining as construction, dynamic animation, and state-dependency complexity increases. We anticipate that Code2Video-Compass will advance reliable, coherent, and controllable code-based video generation and inspire broader progress in programmatic visual creation.","authors":["Zhenghan Chen","Zekai Shao","Xin Lin","Leting Cheng","Siming Chen"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:51:41Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:51:41Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28416v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28416v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28416v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28411v1","title":"Brain2Vec: A Self-Supervised 3D Foundation Model for Structural Brain MRI","abstract":"Deep learning models have become essential tools in medical imaging, yet training them end-to-end for diverse tasks remains challenging due to limited labeled data and computational constraints. To address this, we introduce Brain2Vec, a foundation model pre-trained on 74,425 T1-weighted Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) scans pooled from multiple studies. Brain2Vec can serve both as a weight initialization backbone for fine-tuning downstream tasks and as a standalone feature extractor. We evaluated Brain2Vec in three tasks: downstream predictive performance, convergence speed, and embedding quality. Using Brain2Vec's pretrained weights as initialization and fine-tuning on labeled data for a brain age prediction task, results demonstrate improved final accuracy and faster convergence compared to random initialization. Additionally, its frozen embeddings outperform anatomically defined volumetric features on four of five downstream classification tasks and perform competitively with a state-of-the-art foundation model pre-trained on substantially more data. Lastly, we show that Brain2Vec encodes information primarily from a spatially localized and neuroanatomically meaningful set of brain regions, and that manipulating a subset of latent features produces monotonic, region-specific effects on the reconstructed anatomy. Brain2Vec code and trained models are available as an open-source package on https://github.com/CBICA/Brain2Vec. Users can apply Brain2Vec locally by installing the package, or directly through the NiChart cloud platform https://cloud.neuroimagingchart.com, where Brain2Vec features can be derived without requiring local installation or specialized infrastructure.","authors":["Spiros Maggioros","Guray Erus","Gareth Harman","George Aidinis","Alexander Getka","Aristeidis Sotiras","Christos Davatzikos"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:50:35Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:50:35Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28411v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28411v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28411v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28410v1","title":"A Multi-Agent Framework for Automated Machine Learning with Evidence-based Planning for Medical Image Analysis","abstract":"Deep learning has become the default tool for medical image analysis, driven by the open-source movement. However, achieving the best performance on a given task remains difficult, with architecture selection and hyperparameter optimization (HPO) still considered \"more of an art than a science\". Automated machine learning (AutoML) automates parts of model training, yet helps little in medical imaging: general-purpose approaches lack medical architectures and preprocessing, and medical-focused ones demand engineering expertise and remain confined to narrow tasks such as 3D segmentation. We introduce MedicalMLOmni, a multi-agent AutoML framework that automates model training for diverse single- and multi-modal medical tasks through a natural language interface. Specifically, MedicalMLOmni performs evidence-based planning by retrieving published results to guide model selection and configuration, and employs parallel HPO where multiple agents explore adjustment strategies concurrently. MedicalMLOmni achieves the best score on 19 of 20 benchmarks; PMC-VQA is the exception, where a vision-language model with broader pre-training leads. Backbone-matched controls attribute the gains to the framework, not the backbone. By removing coding requirements, MedicalMLOmni lowers the barrier for domain experts with rich datasets but limited engineering expertise.","authors":["Yiqing Shen","Mathias Unberath"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:49:38Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:49:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28410v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28410v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28410v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28402v1","title":"StreamEval: Evaluating Interactive Streaming Video Generation Models","abstract":"As streaming video generation advances, enabling real-time interaction with generated video is becoming increasingly important. We introduce StreamEval, a unified benchmark for interactive streaming video generation spanning five tasks: avatar dialogue, avatar performance, world generation, instruction editing, and reference editing. StreamEval comprises 300 scripted test cases lasting 15-300s, each specifying initial assets, incrementally arriving multimodal requests, and explicit requirements for state changes and content preservation. Performance is assessed along four evaluation dimensions: task execution, state consistency, visual fidelity, and audiovisual coordination, instantiated through ten task-specific metrics on a 0-100 scale. Evaluating 37 distinct models across 300 test cases, we find that high visual quality and identity retention do not necessarily coincide with reliable request execution or sustained state consistency. In particular, request execution remains challenging: the highest task-specific scores are 36.90 for Following among seven avatar-performance models, 25.93 for Following in text-conditioned world generation, and 15.00 for Edit Success among three reported reference-editing models. These findings identify reliable request execution under timing and preservation constraints as a central challenge for advancing omni-modal real-time interactive systems. The leaderboard is available at https://streameval.ai","authors":["StreamEval Team"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:47:43Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:47:43Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28402v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28402v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28402v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28382v1","title":"RACE: Learning Multimodal Search through Referential Abstraction and Compositional Evidence","abstract":"Training multimodal search agents requires tasks that make visually grounded search necessary for determining the answer. Textual identity clues and unused target evidence allow shortcuts in synthetic questions, weakening supervision for visual search and evidence integration. We propose RACE, an image-conditioned task synthesis framework that constructs dependencies from images to targets and from target-specific evidence to answers. Referential Abstraction (RA) replaces identity-revealing text with specific visual references or ambiguous relational references resolved by searching among depicted candidates. Compositional Evidence (CE) links retrieved facts about multiple entities in one image or entities from different images into joint questions whose answers require every designated contribution. Both operations also apply to images found during search, allowing new visual observations to determine subsequent search targets or supply answer evidence. Fine-tuning Qwen3.5-27B on only 12K teacher-generated trajectories from RACE raises average accuracy across ten multimodal benchmarks from 53.5 to 56.0, a relative improvement of approximately 4.7% over the matched base agent. Our code, data, and model will be publicly available.","authors":["Eric Tao","Minhao Sun","Liyan Liu","Zhijing Wu","Xiaohan Yu","Lang Mei","Li Huang","Chao Feng","Jinchao Ma","Sichen Kang","Siyu Mo","Chong Chen"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:33:24Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:33:24Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28382v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28382v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28382v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28356v1","title":"CountVideoBench: A Benchmark to Evaluate Prediction Biases on the Object Counting task by Video-Language Models","abstract":"Object counting in videos remains a poorly characterized capability of multimodal large language models. Counting questions are included in many existing VideoQA benchmarks, but the ground-truth count distributions in these benchmarks are heavily skewed toward small values. Across the nine benchmarks we survey, the median ground-truth count is at most five, and the three most frequent values together account for between 45% and 93% of the videos. The resulting mean accuracy is then dominated by the most frequent counts; a value-dependent bias at a rare count contributes negligibly to the average and remains invisible. We introduce CountVideoBench, a benchmark of 2,600 synthetic videos covering 26 cardinalities in the interval [5, 50] (100 videos each), grouped into six clusters of consecutive counts that span the range while enabling near-neighbor comparisons. Each video contains a single class of static objects under a controlled camera motion, removing object motion and reducing recognition as confounds. We evaluate sixteen video-language models from nine architecture families in a zero-shot setting. No model exceeds 27.6% Exact Match. The dependence of accuracy on cardinality varies across models, ranging from monotonic decline to a non-monotonic peak-and-collapse pattern, which rules out increasing difficulty with cardinality as the dominant explanation of counting failure. Every model further concentrates its predictions on its own set of preferred integers, with accuracy peaks coinciding with these values and collapsing at adjacent counts. These findings highlight the need for balanced, diagnostic counting benchmarks to reliably assess this fundamental visual capability.","authors":["Marc Chapus","Carlos Crispim-Junior","Véronique Eglin","Atilla Baskurt"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:20:28Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:20:28Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28356v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28356v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28356v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28354v1","title":"S-Agent 2.0: Spatial Reasoning with a General Coding Agent","abstract":"Spatial reasoning asks a model to work out where objects are in 3D space, how they relate to one another, and how they move. Agentic VLMs have recently brought large improvements on such tasks by letting the model actively gather evidence with 3D perception tools instead of answering in a single pass. Even so, on many questions the tools return the right evidence and the model still does not arrive at the correct answer: the gap between model and tools, where raw readings become usable conclusions, is left unfilled. We present S-Agent 2.0, a spatial reasoning agent that equips an off-the-shelf general coding agent with a command-line perception toolkit, and we design the interface around one principle: help the VLM close the evidence loop: acquire scene evidence, remember it in a persistent scene memory, and interpret each reading in plain spatial language. This design keeps the full flexibility of a coding agent, and every reading it acts on stays grounded in the tools' geometry. On MMSI-Bench and ReVSI, the toolkit improves a strong open-source model, Qwen3.8-27B, by +13.9 and +20.5 points over the same agent without tools, and a strong closed-source model, Gemini-3.8-Flash, by +11.2 and +9.6 points. With the open backbone S-Agent 2.0 reaches 66.5 on MMSI-Bench and 82.3 on ReVSI, ahead of the specialized system SpatialClaw on the same backbone (62.6 and 74.5), and with Gemini-3.8-Flash it reaches 74.1 and 90.5 against its 67.0 and 79.7. Both tool gains shrink as the backbone grows more capable, but SpatialClaw's shrinks far more on ReVSI, from +13.2 to +2.7, while ours stays at +9.6. S-Agent 2.0 also works across different general coding agent harnesses, such as Codex and Claude Code.","authors":["Rui Gong","Ruoyu Wang","Yalun Dai","Hao Li","Sidi Yang","Qihao Zhao","Hongyu Li","Zeyang Guan","Fangzhou Hong","Zhaoxi Chen","Kim-Hui Yap","Ziwei Liu"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:20:00Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:20:00Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28354v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28354v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28354v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28351v1","title":"RayCo4D: Ray-Conditioned Continuous Motion Model for Monocular 4D Dynamic Reconstruction","abstract":"Reconstructing dynamic 3D scenes from monocular video requires inferring motion from noisy and intermittently visible observations. Long-range tracks provide valuable geometric constraints, but integrating their fragmented temporal coverage into shared, continuous motion remains challenging. We introduce \\textbf{RayCo4D}, a ray-conditioned continuous motion model for monocular 4D reconstruction that explicitly separates visual measurements from latent 3D motion states. Each track is associated with a reference-time 3D query constrained to its camera ray, and its image and depth observations constrain shared latent motion nodes through local many-to-many associations. Each node maintains an $\\mathrm{SE}(3)$ pose and body-frame velocity at sparse support times. A white-noise-on-acceleration process prior couples neighboring states and defines continuous-time interpolation, allowing observations across visibility intervals to jointly constrain the same latent state sequence.The inferred shared motion is transferred to Gaussian primitives through dual-quaternion blending, while reference-anchored local $\\mathrm{SE}(3)$ residuals capture fine-scale non-rigid deformation beyond the shared scaffold. Experiments on diverse monocular dynamic scenes show that RayCo4D achieves competitive novel-view synthesis quality, improves structural and temporal consistency under rapid motion and incomplete tracks, and supports continuous-time rendering.","authors":["Yuanxiang Ni","Xianliang Huang","Ruihan Hou","Feng Xie","Hao Zhang"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:17:29Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:17:29Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28351v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28351v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28351v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28346v1","title":"Auto-Director: Leveraging Multi-Agent Collaboration for Autonomous Video Editing","abstract":"Autonomous video editing from open-world resources remains a challenging problem in multimedia content creation. Retrieval-based methods are constrained by fixed source material and lack creative flexibility, while generative models struggle with long-horizon coherence and often diverge from user intent. Both paradigms fail to replicate the structured, multi-stage reasoning that underlies professional post-production. To address these limitations, we present Auto-Director, a tool-augmented multi-agent framework that mirrors professional editorial workflows: a Planning Agent decomposes abstract topics into fact-grounded scripts via retrieval-augmented planning; a Material Agent indexes open-world footage through shot segmentation and visual tagging; an Editor Agent assembles semantically aligned clips with MLLM-driven spatial adaptation; and a Critic Agent governs an iterative Reflect-and-Revise loop that checks scene-level alignment and pacing. To rigorously evaluate this new paradigm, we introduce VEdit-Bench, a benchmark of 100 expert-curated topics with human-annotated Golden Scripts and a five-dimensional metric suite covering Narrative Logic, Factual Accuracy, Semantic Alignment, Compositional Quality, and Stylistic Cohesion. Extensive experiments demonstrate that Auto-Director significantly outperforms baseline methods in both automated metrics and human evaluation, effectively bridging the gap between amateurs and professional-grade video production.","authors":["Yuxia Chen","Wenbo Zhu","Yangguang Ji","Zhenxiang Jiang","Teng Zhong","Yu Zhang"],"published":"2026-10-20T15:11:55Z","updated":"2026-10-20T15:11:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28346v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28346v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28346v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","retrieval","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28323v1","title":"PACE: Plan, Attend, Check, and Emit for Training-Free Response Timing in Streaming Video Understanding","abstract":"Streaming video understanding requires a model to decide not only what to answer but also when to answer. Evidence arrives progressively, and recent benchmarks identify premature responses, issued before the queried event has concluded, as a recurring failure mode. Existing approaches often learn response timing through additional training, while training-free alternatives typically rely on scalar gating signals that do not explicitly represent when an answer becomes final. We organize chunk-wise streaming errors into three patterns: sampling variation can make an answer unstable, a stable answer may lack support in the observed video, or an answer may be supported by the current evidence while the event remains unfinished. We propose PACE (Plan, Attend, Check, and Emit), a training-free framework designed around these three failure modes. Given a question, PACE drafts a plan specifying what to observe and what will signal completion. As the stream unfolds, it invokes an off-the-shelf video LLM at informative moments, evaluates each draft for stability, grounding in its cited frames, and completion, and records verified observations in an event ledger. Experiments on ProactiveVideoQA and OVO-Bench Forward show that PACE improves over the official protocols by up to 5.9 average PAUC points and 6.2 average accuracy points across proprietary and open-source backbones, yielding state-of-the-art performance.","authors":["Siqi Guo","Yuxia Chen","Wenbo Zhu","Yangguang Ji","Zhenxiang Jiang","Teng Zhong","Yu Zhang"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:58:18Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:58:18Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28323v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28323v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28323v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28316v1","title":"ABACUS: A Benchmark for Image Editing Models to Assess Object Category and Counting Under Scene Conservation","abstract":"Instruction-based image editing models can now add, remove a specified number of objects or change their category while preserving the scene. Yet these models often fail to produce the correct number, and these failures grow sharply as scenes become more complex and the requested count increases. Current benchmarks largely miss this problem: they score edits with holistic similarity metrics or with vision-language model (VLM) judges that are themselves poor at counting, so numerical errors go unnoticed or are graded incorrectly. As a result, the true numerical ability of today's editing models has never been accurately measured. We address this issue with ABACUS, a new benchmark, evaluation protocol and dedicated scoring metric for numerical image editing. Our protocol is judge-free: no VLM or MLLM scores an edit or counts objects (a VLM only confirms the category of rare ambiguous crops). Instead, it verifies each edit at the instance level against panoptic ground truth confirmed by detector consensus, checking both that the exact requested count was achieved and that the rest of the scene was preserved, across the add, remove and change operations. By evaluating leading image editors, our method reveals substantial numerical editing failures that worsen with increasing scene complexity and object count; failures that prior benchmarks often overlooked or misjudged.","authors":["Aymen Lassoued","Angelique Loesch","Romaric Audigier"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:53:22Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:53:22Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28316v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28316v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28316v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28313v1","title":"Towards Consistent Geospatial Representations: Aligning One Encoder to Many Foundation Models","abstract":"Geospatial foundation models (GFMs) differ widely in pretraining objective, modality and resolution, and none leads across benchmarks. We ask whether their representations are complementary, and whether a single encoder can inherit that complementarity. We introduce Pangaea-Latents, an extension of the Pangaea benchmark providing frozen embeddings of $5$ GFMs over its $11$ datasets, along with reproduced baselines, and find GFM embeddings to be neither redundant nor disjoint. We then propose GeoMixture, a preliminary framework to align a ViT to several GFMs per dataset through a multi-representation alignment (mREPA) objective. Our encoder ties the best GFM in average rank across nine Pangaea datasets, trading peak performance for cross-task consistency.","authors":["Arthur Ouaknine","David Rolnick"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:52:21Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:52:21Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28313v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28313v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28313v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28284v1","title":"HRoPE: Homography Relative Positional Encoding","abstract":"Geometric Relative Positional Encoding has become the primary mechanism for injecting spatial inductive biases into multi-view transformers. Existing designs condition attention on camera-level frustums or ray-marched depth, associating cross-view tokens through the sensor geometry. Such sensor-centric formulations, however, describe only where a token is observed from, leaving the rich surface geometry of the scene region depicted by the token. In this paper, we advocate a shift toward surface-aware positional encoding that explicitly grounds patch tokens in the geometry of physical surfaces. We introduce HRoPE, which represents the local geometry of each patch as a slanted 3D plane, following the slanted support-window assumption of classical multi-view stereo. We inject token-level, plane-induced homographies into attention as relative transformations. This equips the transformer with a geometric prior for rectifying perspective distortions and aligning patch features across viewpoints. Our encoding requires no ground-truth geometry: the plane parameters can be predicted end-to-end under a view-synthesis objective alone, or derived from foundation models. Extensive experiments on RealEstate10K, CO3Dv2, and DL3DV show that our best variant of HRoPE consistently outperforms the evaluated geometric positional encodings for novel view synthesis. Extensive ablations and analysis show the effectiveness of the proposed encoding. Please refer to our project page at: https://gaohchen.github.io/HRoPE","authors":["Huachen Gao","Yang Yang","Xiaoqian Liang","Xingyu Chen","Youjia Zhang","Qi Zhang","Baorui Ma","Anpei Chen","Wei Yang"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:36:51Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:36:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28284v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28284v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28284v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28282v1","title":"ChartEvo: Self-Evolving Agent Harnesses for Long-Tail Chart Reasoning","abstract":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have substantially advanced chart understanding and reasoning, yet their performance remains highly uneven across chart types. While modern MLLMs perform well on common charts, their accuracy drops sharply on long-tail charts with diverse visual structures and reasoning requirements. Tool-augmented agents can improve complex chart reasoning by introducing specialized visual capabilities, but manually engineering strategies and tools for the heterogeneous and evolving long tail is difficult to scale. We introduce \\textbf{ChartEvo}, a recursive self-improvement framework that enables chart agents to adapt to underperforming chart types through experience-driven harness-level self-improvement. ChartEvo jointly evolves reusable chart-specific Skills and executable Tools, while learning reusable local tool-call chains to compose evolved capabilities and control the inference overhead of tool-augmented reasoning. On long-tail charts from ChartBench and ChartX, ChartEvo improves accuracy over its base MLLM, Qwen3.7-Plus, by \\textbf{45.6\\%} and \\textbf{32.5\\%}, respectively, demonstrating the effectiveness of recursive self-improvement for long-tail chart adaptation.","authors":["Qi Wang","Peijie Wang","Jun Cao","Haoyang Tong","Xiao-Hui Li","Fei Yin","Cheng-Lin Liu"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:34:54Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:34:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28282v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28282v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28282v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28280v1","title":"GALA: Geometry-Aware Language Model for Controllable Object Arrangement","abstract":"Reasoning about fine-grained placement constraints, such as support and containment, is essential for realistic 3D scene creation. It remains a significant challenge for existing methods that rely on simplified object representations like oriented bounding box (OBB), which are blind to the detailed geometry. To address this limitation, we introduce GALA, a geometry-aware language model with explicit point cloud perception for controllable object placement. Our model performs autoregressively by predicting the 6D pose of one target object at a time conditioned on the scene context and placement instruction. We utilize a two-stage training strategy: the first stage aligns geometric and textual modalities through pretraining tasks, and the second stage fine-tunes the model for placement. To facilitate this task, we also curate a multi-source scene dataset covering diverse room types and fine-grained furniture arrangements, which will be open-sourced for community benefit. Experiments on our benchmark show our model significantly outperforms baselines without explicit geometric reasoning. We further develop a multi-stage plan-place-verify agent pipeline that uses the model for text-driven scene generation. Project Page: https://xccelephant.github.io/paper/gala/","authors":["Chucheng Xiang","Runze Wang","Zhi Deng","Ruchao Bao","Yuanwei Zhang","Yuxuan Xie","Hanliu Wang","Liangzhen Fei","Wenzheng Wu","Cheng Wan","Peifeng Li","Zhongyuan Liu","Ligang Liu"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:32:52Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:32:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28280v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28280v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28280v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28266v1","title":"VGL-stGen: Learning a shared multimodal model for lung histology and spatial transcriptomics","abstract":"VGL-stGen is a multimodal large language model adapted from a pretrained medical vision-language model using paired lung H&E and spatial transcriptomics with single-cell references. Training integrates image-expression and expression-cell-type pairs, and gene-conditioned pseudo-labels transfer transcriptomic cell-type supervision to histology images. One deployed checkpoint supports thirteen evaluated tasks spanning tissue and cell-type classification, gene prediction and conditional expression completion from combinations of histology, cell identity and observed gene expression. Task-specific decoding produces categorical labels, gene lists and structured expression levels. For conditional completion, fixing queried gene identities and predicting their levels autoregressively improves structured prediction. Controlled adaptation shows that restricting supervision to requested expression-level tokens can further improve image-conditioned completion. Image-permutation experiments indicate that correctly paired histology adds information beyond observed expression. External classification relies partly on computational references with shared annotation lineage, and some H&E sources were exposed during cancer training or model development. Within these conditions, VGL-stGen demonstrates how a medical vision-language backbone can learn from spatial transcriptomics and histology to connect tissue morphology and molecular states through shared generative modeling.","authors":["Haenara Shin","Dongjoo Lee","Sungwoo Bae","Kwon Joong Na","Daeseung Lee","Hongyoon Choi"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:22:47Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:22:47Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28266v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28266v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28266v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28264v1","title":"PI-MLLM: Psychophysics-Inspired Multimodal Large Language Model for Oddly Satisfying Video Emotion Analysis","abstract":"Oddly Satisfying Videos (OSVs) can evoke strong viewer responses through repetitive motion, visual tactility, and ASMR-like sounds despite containing few explicit semantic emotion cues. This makes OSVs a challenging setting for Video Emotion Analysis (VEA), since most existing methods focus on semantic video content and under-model the physical sensory dynamics that shape viewer responses. To support Oddly Satisfying Video Emotion Analysis (OSVEA), we construct the Oddly Satisfying Video Dataset (OSVD), comprising 12,000 annotated 5-second clips. Each clip provides viewer-emotion labels under video-only, audio-only, and audio-visual conditions, together with explanations grounded in visual and acoustic sensory evidence. Building on OSVD, we propose PI-MLLM, a psychophysics-inspired multimodal large language model that integrates Modality-Specific Dynamics Alignment (MSDA) and Coalition-Based Audio-Visual Attribution (CAVA). MSDA uses auxiliary prediction objectives to align modality representations with visual motion and acoustic energy-onset dynamics. CAVA evaluates emotion scores across modality coalitions and applies Shapley attribution to identify the dominant modality and determine whether visual and auditory evidence is synergistic or conflicting. Experiments show that PI-MLLM improves both viewer-side emotion prediction and sensory-grounded explanation quality, suggesting the value of physical sensory cues to video emotion analysis.","authors":["Yuheng Zhang","Jie Zhang","Yuecong Min","Yunwei Zhao","Meichen Liu","Qiankun Li","Shiguang Shan"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:20:39Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:20:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28264v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28264v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28264v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28257v1","title":"SplatFlux: Feed-Forward 4D Gaussian Reconstruction and Generation from Video Latents","abstract":"This work presents SplatFlux, a feed-forward latent-to-4DGS framework for both video-based 4D reconstruction and conditional 4D generation. Our key observation is that observed videos and conditionally generated content can be represented in the same pretrained video latent space, allowing both tasks to be formulated as latent-to-4DGS decoding. To construct compact and temporally coherent 4D scenes, SplatFlux introduces a static-dynamic decoupled 4D Gaussian representation that explicitly predicts dynamic probabilities for Gaussian primitives. This representation separates static scene structures from moving regions, enabling complete 4D scene construction in a feed-forward manner through static aggregation and dynamic motion interpolation. To learn static-dynamic decomposition and dynamic Gaussian motion without costly annotations, we further develop an auxiliary distillation strategy that provides pseudo dynamic masks and optical-flow cues. Extensive experiments across 3D and 4D reconstruction and generation demonstrate that SplatFlux achieves state-of-the-art performance.","authors":["Runmin Zhang","Binglin Ren","Zhu Yu","Fudong Wang","Jiangke Lin","Yichun Shentu","Si-Yuan Cao","Yi Yuan","Hui-Liang Shen"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:12:53Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:12:53Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28257v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28257v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28257v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28255v1","title":"Convergent Performance, Divergent Mechanisms in LoRA-Adapted Vision Foundation Models for Brain MRI","abstract":"Adapting Vision Foundation Models (VFMs) to medical imaging via LoRA has become standard practice, yet model selection is guided almost entirely by downstream task metrics, treating backbones with similar performance as interchangeable. We investigate whether equal performance implies equal internal representational shift. We analyse four ViT-B/16 backbones, DINOv3, SigLIP2, MerMED-FM, and BiomedCLIP, pre-trained with different objectives (self-distillation vs. contrastive vision-language) and on different data domains (natural vs. medical images). We adapt each to brain tumour segmentation and evaluate on six datasets covering both segmentation and Alzheimer's disease classification. Using attention divergence, weight-change analysis, and activation patching, we find that the four models converge to similar segmentation performance (mean Dice 78-81%) through markedly different internal dynamics, yet diverge in cross-task transfer: segmentation adaptation improves Alzheimer's disease classification for DINOv3 but degrades it for the other three backbones. We reveal a dissociation between modification magnitude and causal importance: the most heavily modified layers are not the causally relevant ones, with early-to-mid layers identified as sufficient for segmentation recovery. Restricting LoRA to these layers matches or approaches full-layer segmentation while reducing classification degradation in the three affected backbones, providing a mechanistically grounded strategy that is more parameter-efficient and more robust than standard full-layer adaptation.","authors":["Cecilia Diana-Albelda","Fiona Kekwick","Raghav Mehta","Aleksandra Kulbaka","Paul M. Matthews","Jesus Bescos","Álvaro García-Martín","Wenjia Bai","Bernhard Kainz"],"published":"2026-10-20T14:12:44Z","updated":"2026-10-20T14:12:44Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28255v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28255v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28255v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28210v1","title":"Learn to Modify: Self-evolving Multi-agent Refinement Framework for Conversational Text-to-Motion Generation","abstract":"Despite rapid progress in text-to-motion generation, existing methods are commonly designed for single-shot generation. When users are dissatisfied with specific motion details, they are often forced to regenerate the entire sequence from scratch, which is computationally inefficient and potentially destroys previously established kinematic context. A practical generation system should instead support iterative user feedback and refine only the affected spatio-temporal regions while preserving the remaining motion. Therefore, we introduce a conversational motion generation paradigm that requires the system to coordinate instruction understanding, motion generation, localized editing, and quality assessment. Recent advances in LLM-driven multi-agent systems provide a promising foundation for such role-specialized coordination. To tackle these challenges, a Self-evolving Multi-Agent RefinemenT Framework (SMART) is proposed for conversational motion generation. Within SMART, to preserve semantic consistency, we design a semantic-guided masked motion editing mechanism, which precisely grounds natural language feedback into spatiotemporal motion regions to enable localized refinements without altering unedited regions. To reduce redundant exploration, we propose a memory-based self-evolving strategy that dynamically accumulates motion editing knowledge from interaction history and reuses past successful experience. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SMART effectively supports multi-turn conversational motion generation and enables efficient motion refinement.","authors":["Jianan Li","Jiawei Zhao","Yangtao Zhou","Zhifu Zhao","Shaoxin Wu","Yutao Wang","Dezhen Wang","Hua Chu","Fu Li"],"published":"2026-10-20T13:47:11Z","updated":"2026-10-20T13:47:11Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28210v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28210v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28210v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28192v1","title":"Offline Makes Online Better: Calibrated Cache Compression for Video Generation","abstract":"Token merging is an effective approach to reducing KV-cache memory consumption in autoregressive video generation by clustering temporally redundant tokens and retaining only a small set of representatives. Existing methods, however, typically apply a uniform merging ratio across all attention layers and heads. We observe that different layers and heads exhibit substantially different yet jointly correlated sensitivities to token merging, and that these sensitivity patterns remain consistent across prompts. Based on this observation, we introduce CALM, a Calibrated Allocation for Layer-Head Merging that uses offline calibration to improve online cache compression. Exhaustively evaluating all possible layer-head allocations is prohibitively expensive. To enable efficient calibration, we show that attention-output error strongly correlates with end-to-end video quality and can therefore serve as a lightweight proxy for allocation risk assessment. We evaluate different allocation strategies using this proxy and construct a fixed layer-head risk map offline. Given a cache budget at inference time, CALM uses dynamic programming to determine the best allocation of merging ratios. Experiments across three different autoregressive video models demonstrate that CALM consistently outperforms uniform token merging under the same memory budget, by reducing the LPIPS to original videos by around 21% with higher video-quality scores.","authors":["Zhen Wang","Ning Lai","Qingtao Yu"],"published":"2026-10-20T13:33:04Z","updated":"2026-10-20T13:33:04Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28192v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28192v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28192v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28190v1","title":"CrossFit-GS: Out-of-Fold Pose Estimation for COLMAP-Free 3D Gaussian Splatting","abstract":"Pose-free 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) aims to bypass cumbersome Structure-from-Motion (SfM) by directly reconstructing scenes from unposed image sequences. In this work, we present CrossFit-GS, a deferred-commitment progressive framework that mitigates cumulative trajectory drift and breaks the self-reinforcing loop of distorted-map error propagation. Specifically, we partition the input sequence into interleaved subsets and maintain two complementary submaps. Each candidate pose is cross-refined against a frozen opposite submap that has never been optimized using the target frame, thereby providing an independent geometric reference for pose estimation. Checkerboard validation and spatial coverage safeguards further reject unreliable updates, and only verified poses are committed to their corresponding owner submap. Once the trajectory has been refined, the intermediate submaps are discarded and the final global representation is trained from scratch using the fixed refined poses. Extensive evaluations across multiple benchmarks demonstrate that CrossFit-GS substantially reduces cumulative trajectory drift and consistently improves both camera pose estimation and novel-view synthesis over existing methods.","authors":["Zijian Wu","Jinliang Wang","Mingfeng Jiang","Zidian Lin","Ying Song","Ziqian Lu"],"published":"2026-10-20T13:29:26Z","updated":"2026-10-20T13:29:26Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28190v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28190v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28190v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28131v1","title":"OV-Foam: How 3D Representations Shape Semantic Feature Lifting","abstract":"Existing feature lifting approaches for open-vocabulary segmentation rely on simple backprojection to assign features to primitives. In the case of 3D Gaussian splatting reconstruction, the primitives overlap heavily, leading to suboptimal reconstructions, governed by many primitives per ray. Backprojection, however, reduces to a least-squares solvable problem, exactly when no two primitives share a ray. Motivated by this insight, we propose OV-Foam, which uses PowerFoam as the underlying representation. This representation uses a power-diagram to partition the scene, resulting in zero overlap between primitives and a substantial reduction in primitives per ray, which ultimately simplifies the lifting problem and allows for closer-to-optimal feature assignment. We show that the space partitioning primitives of OV-Foam reach near-optimal assignment, and our experiments demonstrate improved open-vocabulary segmentation over Gaussian baselines.","authors":["Yousef Rajeh","Alejandra Esparza Pelcastre","Ivan Viola","Dominik Engel"],"published":"2026-10-20T12:38:14Z","updated":"2026-10-20T12:38:14Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28131v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28131v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28131v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28129v1","title":"GAMF-Net: Grouped Availability-Aware Multimodal Fusion for Smartphone Skin Lesion Classification","abstract":"Smartphone clinical images provide a low-cost data source for computer-aided skin lesion classification in resource-constrained settings, but images alone cannot fully capture patients' medical histories and lesion symptoms. To address the lack of structured organization among heterogeneous clinical variables, the ambiguity between observed and imputed values, and the risk that clinical information may interfere with visual evidence, we propose GAMF-Net, a grouped availability-aware multimodal fusion network. Ten clinical variables are divided into three semantic groups---demographics, history-related risk, and lesion presentation---and each group jointly encodes field values and field-level availability masks. The image branch produces the base classification logits, while each clinical group generates image-conditioned class-level residual evidence. Only available groups participate in availability-normalized equal-weight fusion; when all clinical information is unavailable, the model reduces exactly to the image-only pathway. We evaluate the method on PAD-UFES-20 under a patient-disjoint five-fold protocol with separate training, validation, and test partitions. GAMF-Net achieves an Accuracy of $74.17\\pm2.85\\%$, a balanced accuracy (BACC) of $70.11\\pm2.11\\%$, a Macro-F1 of $68.85\\pm0.85\\%$, and a Macro-AUROC of $93.42\\pm1.20\\%$, outperforming all image-only and multimodal baselines reimplemented under a unified protocol. Compared with the strongest baseline, MetaBlock, the corresponding improvements are 2.37, 2.59, 4.14, and 1.02 percentage points, respectively. Ablation results show that clinical information substantially improves class-balanced performance, constrained equal-weight fusion is more effective than dynamic fusion, and the lesion-presentation group provides the most informative clinical complement.","authors":["Yue Zhuo","Jingjia Yan","Fangyu Liu","Lingjun Liu","Zongjun Chen","Zhisheng Luo","Zhonghua Xie"],"published":"2026-10-20T12:35:39Z","updated":"2026-10-20T12:35:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28129v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28129v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28129v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28120v1","title":"PointForward V2: Point-Aligned Interaction for Feedforward Driving Reconstruction","abstract":"Feedforward reconstruction of dynamic driving scenes requires integrating observations across multiple views and time steps into a coherent 3D representation. PointForward established state-of-the-art feedforward driving reconstruction by lifting image observations into sparse 3D queries, demonstrating the effectiveness of point-aligned scene representations. However, these queries are largely processed independently, limiting information exchange across space and time, while direct global attention among tens of thousands of point queries is computationally prohibitive. In this work, we present \\textbf{PointForward V2}, which advances point-aligned representation to point-aligned interaction. Our key idea is to mediate query interaction through compact, scene-structured registers. For the static background, frame-wise Static Scene Registers compress point information, communicate across frames, and propagate the aggregated context back to the queries. For each dynamic instance, queries from different frames are aligned in an object-centric canonical space and interact through instance-specific Dynamic Instance Registers. We further employ 4D positional encoding that combines aligned spatial coordinates with original timestamps, enabling spatially aligned yet temporally distinguishable interactions. In this way, PointForward V2 enables scalable spatio-temporal reasoning directly among lifted 3D queries without dense global attention. Experiments on Waymo, nuScenes, and Argoverse 2 demonstrate state-of-the-art performance across large-scale driving benchmarks.","authors":["Xianqi Wang","Cheng Chi","Yuechuan Pu","Hongcheng Luo","Junda Cheng","Guang Chen","Hangjun Ye","Bing Wang","Xin Yang"],"published":"2026-10-20T12:30:21Z","updated":"2026-10-20T12:30:21Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28120v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28120v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28120v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation","instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28118v1","title":"Reliability-Guided Vision-Language Decoding with Text-Aware Skip Refinement for Medical Image Segmentation","abstract":"Medical image segmentation remains challenging when targets exhibit limited visual distinction, irregular structure, or substantial variation across imaging modalities. Medical text can reduce this ambiguity, but indiscriminate fusion may introduce unreliable guidance. We present RELiF, a vision--language framework that integrates text through reliability-guided decoding. Its Reliability-guided Fusion Gate (RFG) adaptively controls cross-modal updates, while the Text-guided Skip Refinement Adapter (TSRA) refines high-resolution features using text guidance. RELiF is evaluated on radiographic, CT, and endoscopic segmentation tasks, where it shows competitive performance across diverse imaging characteristics and target appearances. Additional analyses examine efficiency and stability. The complementary uncertainty representation derived from the reliability response also shows an association with prediction difficulty, with higher uncertainty values occurring more frequently in pixels associated with segmentation errors. Code: https://github.com/SeivanTalaie/RELiF.","authors":["Sivan Talaei","Fatemeh Daneshfar","Mustaqeem Khan","Abdulhady Abas Abdullah","Mourad Oussalah"],"published":"2026-10-20T12:29:44Z","updated":"2026-10-20T12:29:44Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28118v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28118v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28118v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28091v1","title":"LatticeRank: Spacing-Ranked Consistency for Native-Grid 3D Medical Image Segmentation","abstract":"Variable voxel spacing changes more than image resolution: it alters the discrete sampling lattice, tensor geometry, and anatomical evidence available to a 3D segmentation network. While conventional pipelines restore images to a canonical grid, direct-grid deployment requires the model itself to tolerate these changes. We observe that candidate grids are not exchangeable views -- a coarse grid removes more structural information than a mildly resampled one -- yet standard consistency objectives assign them equal influence. We therefore propose LatticeRank, a training-only framework that aligns candidate posteriors in physical space and allocates consistency supervision by voxel-spacing order: among endpoint-matched candidates, the auxiliary loss concentrates on the coarsest requested grid, while inference retains a single network forward with no candidate branch or ensemble. On MSD Task04, LatticeRank improves coarse-grid Extra Dice over mean consistency by 0.03436 across five seeds while meeting the prespecified reference-grid noninferiority criterion. A controlled ladder shows that grid exposure provides the largest recovery, posterior consistency adds a second gain, and spacing-ranked allocation yields the strongest direct-grid result; requested spacing thus provides a simple, model-independent ordering signal for direct-grid consistency learning.","authors":["Jingjia Yan","Yue Zhuo","Zhonghua Xie"],"published":"2026-10-20T12:08:51Z","updated":"2026-10-20T12:08:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28091v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28091v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28091v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28087v1","title":"WildAIGC-Bench: Can AI Image Detectors Handle the Wild? A Benchmark of Generated and Edited Images in Online Circulation","abstract":"AI-generated images linked to news events and online claims can mislead audiences, enable fraud, or cause public controversy. We introduce WildAIGC-Bench, built through recurring collection across news websites, fact-checking sources, and related social media content. The pipeline combines broad coverage, timely updates, and human-verified labels through scheduled discovery, automatic screening and preliminary annotation, and human review of image-specific evidence. The benchmark contains 407 verified AI-generated or edited images, each accompanied by a source webpage link and an evidence summary. Images retain the conditions encountered online, including compression, screenshots, and recapture. Evaluation of dedicated visual detectors, multimodal large language models, and a simple Perception Encoder baseline reveals substantial recall gaps relative to existing benchmarks. WildAIGC-Bench provides both a practical detection challenge and a basis for continued data expansion and source-based verification. The dataset is released at https://github.com/YuZijian/WildAIGC-Bench.","authors":["Zijian Yu","Hangwei Zhang","Jun Lan","Huijia Zhu","Weiqiang Wang","Jianfu Zhang"],"published":"2026-10-20T12:07:16Z","updated":"2026-10-20T12:07:16Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28087v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28087v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28087v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28086v1","title":"MoDDiT: Simple Token Routing in Diffusion Transformers","abstract":"Real-time video generation, interactive world models, and on-device image synthesis are almost a reality. Diffusion Transformers (DiT) achieve excellent sample quality, shifting the bottleneck to the throughput of the models themselves. Natural images are inherently sparse, as large portions of an image are often background, contribute little to the scene content, and are easy to denoise. DiTs fail to exploit this sparsity, as they process image tokens statically and uniformly. We propose MoDDiT, a simple architecture modification that learns to route tokens through both MLP and self-attention operations. By routing tokens through both blocks independently, MoDDiT allows for dynamic compute allocation in MLPs that varies across tokens, images, and denoising timesteps. Using MoDDiT to train image generation models from scratch significantly reduces computational costs of training and inference, processing as few as 12.5% of the tokens. By increasing sparsity and model depth, MoDDiT reduces costs and improves generation performance over unrouted baselines. The learned allocation is interpretable, revealing how computation varies with visual content and noise level. We also use MoDDiT to train video generation models, where allocation correlates with motion, and to distill a dense bidirectional video model into a sparse autoregressive student.","authors":["Jarek Liesen","Matthew T. Jackson","Benjamin Ellis","Shimon Whiteson","Jakob N. Foerster"],"published":"2026-10-20T12:05:49Z","updated":"2026-10-20T12:05:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28086v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28086v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28086v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28074v1","title":"Slot-JEPA: Object-Structured Prediction Interfaces for Latent World Models","abstract":"As a prototypical latent world model, Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPAs) provide strong semantic representations. However, dense patch representations expose a redundant interface to downstream predictors, which must implicitly infer spatial organization and temporal correspondence, presenting three challenges. First, object information is distributed across patches, forcing each downstream predictor to redundantly recover object structure that could otherwise be reused across tasks before modeling interactions. Second, the lack of explicit entity correspondence across time makes historical transitions expensive, restricting the predictor's flexibility in utilizing context. Third, without explicit transport structure, existing JEPA models typically use pointwise l1/l2 distances, which are insensitive to spatial displacement when predicted and goal supports are largely disjoint. We propose the Slot-based Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture (Slot-JEPA), which decomposes dense representations into stable semantic slots and time-varying spatial masks. Pretrained once and frozen downstream, this decomposition exposes a reusable object-structured interface. Building on it, our Transition Memory Predictor (TMP) stores visual transitions as compact masks and retrieves useful context using statistics of corresponding slots. Its two branches separate slot-specific dynamics from shared cross-slot dynamics. We further use Unbalanced Optimal Transport (UOT) to supervise mask prediction and evaluate candidate plans by transporting mass between aligned predicted and goal masks, providing a signal better aligned with spatial progress. Across seven visual-understanding benchmarks, the slot-reconstructed features remain transferable. Slot-JEPA also improves goal-conditioned planning across six planar environments and four robotic arm planning tasks on the REALM-JEPA benchmark.","authors":["Peisong Wen","Qianqian Xu","Minye Lei","Siran Dai","Yang Liu","Qingming Huang"],"published":"2026-10-20T11:53:42Z","updated":"2026-10-20T11:53:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28074v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28074v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28074v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28059v1","title":"Auditing Where Concept Bottleneck Accuracy Comes From on CUB","abstract":"Concept Bottleneck Models (CBMs) promise predictions that humans can inspect and correct through concepts such as \"curved beak\". Yet on CUB-200-2011, their standard benchmark, progress is measured like that of ordinary classifiers: by task and concept accuracy. Neither reveals whether the label is predicted from the exposed concept scores, or whether a concept is supported by visual evidence of that concept rather than by co-occurring features such as cues to the species as a whole. We audit where the accuracy of CBMs and Concept Embedding Models (CEMs) on CUB comes from. The CUB annotations are too noisy to explain it: class-level training labels agree with per-image annotations on only ~82% of image--concept pairs, and classifiers trained on per-image annotations reach only ~37% species accuracy. Leakage probes show that CEM predictions cannot be reconstructed from their concept scores; the missing class information sits in internal embeddings. We identify four design axes through which class information can enter concept scores---receptive field, shared versus concept-specific representations, norm-dependent scoring, and task supervision. An ablation shows that most of it enters through non-local features: restricting a ResNet50 backbone to local BagNet patches lowers the accuracy recoverable from concept scores from 72% to 43%, close to what ground-truth annotations support, while joint training raises it again at the cost of concept accuracy. Localization and occlusion show that locality makes grounding measurable but does not guarantee it: under class-level labels, local concept scores use the head as a proxy for body, wing, and tail concepts, and CEM concept scores barely respond to occluding their supporting part. Task and concept accuracy should thus support, not drive, the evaluation of CBMs, and given the noisy annotations, CUB accuracy gaps should not be overinterpreted.","authors":["Sascha Saralajew","Eleonora Poeta","Eliana Pastor","Ammar Shaker"],"published":"2026-10-20T11:47:49Z","updated":"2026-10-20T11:47:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28059v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28059v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28059v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28051v1","title":"Assessing Representation Semantics Under Language Pretraining in Fine-Grained Classification","abstract":"Understanding how supervision signals shape semantic visual representations remains challenging, particularly in fine-grained, low-data settings. Standard evaluations of vision models rely on downstream accuracy, which often fails to capture structural differences in learned representations. We propose a compact explainability-driven framework for comparing encoders trained with classification-only and caption-generation supervision on CUB-200. Using a controlled two-stage protocol in which both encoders are ultimately optimized for the same classification task, we enable a direct comparison of the resulting representations. Saliency maps are generated using attention rollout and gradient-weighted rollout and evaluated with five metrics covering faithfulness (positive and negative perturbation) and localization (keypoint emphasis, background attribution, and Earth Mover's Distance). Across random seeds and explanation methods, caption supervision yields representations that are more sensitive to the removal of salient regions, better aligned with annotated object parts, and less reliant on background regions, whereas classification supervision produces representations that are slightly more robust to the removal of low-saliency regions. An ablation study on the Oxford-IIIT Pet dataset shows consistent trends across key metrics. These results demonstrate that the proposed framework makes supervision-induced representational differences observable and quantifiable beyond downstream accuracy alone. More broadly, it provides a systematic basis for assessing learned representations and comparing alternative training and transfer-learning strategies.","authors":["Md Raisul Kibria","Sébastien Lafond"],"published":"2026-10-20T11:39:21Z","updated":"2026-10-20T11:39:21Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28051v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28051v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28051v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28018v1","title":"P$^3$-VLM: A Point-based Alternative for Grounded 3D Vision-Language Models","abstract":"Examining existing benchmarks for grounding in 3D VLMs reveals a pervasive \"semantic leakage\" in the evaluation and prompting protocols: a heavy reliance on bounding boxes as spatial prompts or representation primitives. Because bounding boxes are highly informative of object categories, they introduce a shortcut for the models to neglect visual information during 3D VLM training. We demonstrate this with a \"blind\" LLM that, prompted only with the box coordinates, can perform surprisingly well. To further analyze this shortcut, we propose point-based grounding prompting - querying models with a single 3D point instead of a box. Under this protocol, the accuracy of the blind LLM drops from 36.4% to 18.0% on ScanNet's Nr3D, and similarly degrades for 3D VLMs (e.g. LL3DA). Building on this insight, we introduce P$^3$-VLM, a novel tri-pathway 3D VLM that supports point-based prompts and achieves 52.1% state-of-the-art accuracy on location-grounded captioning under this setting. To achieve this, we redesign the architecture while keeping it detector-free, scene-centric, and suitable for 3D GS inputs. P$^3$-VLM utilizes three complementary pathways (location-, task-, and context-aware), enabling strong reasoning about scenes without any bounding box dependencies. The key benefit of point-based learning is a consistent improvement across settings: P$^3$-VLM achieves state-of-the-art performance under point-based grounding while also improving robustness, OOD generalization, and performance on ungrounded scene-centric 3D VQA benchmarks, reflecting its reduced reliance on bounding-box shortcuts and resulting stronger visual-spatial reasoning.","authors":["Anna-Maria Halacheva","Jan-Nico Zaech","Sombit Dey","Luc Van Gool","Danda Pani Paudel"],"published":"2026-10-20T11:08:07Z","updated":"2026-10-20T11:08:07Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28018v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28018v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28018v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28007v1","title":"AdaptMIL: Domain Adaptation for Multiple Instance Learning in Digital Pathology","abstract":"Pathology foundation models (PFMs) support a wide range of histopathology tasks on whole-slide images through multiple instance learning (MIL). Despite recent progress, these models remain sensitive to cross-hospital distribution shifts: performance often degrades when a task head (e.g., classification) is trained on data from one hospital and then used on data from another hospital. To address this challenge, we propose AdaptMIL, a training strategy for integrating domain adaptation into MIL training. AdaptMIL is motivated by our finding that directly applying domain adaptation objectives during MIL training can interfere with MIL aggregation learning, often resulting in performance degradation over standard training. AdaptMIL resolves this by decoupling representation-level domain alignment learning from MIL aggregation learning through a two-stage training procedure. We show that AdaptMIL improves cross-hospital performance and outperforms alternative adaptation methods across experiments spanning multiple WSI datasets, tasks, PFMs, MIL architectures, and domain adaptation objectives. AdaptMIL's code will be released upon publication.","authors":["Ben Vardi","Dana Schonberger","Yuval Friedmann","Zohar Yakhini","Iris Barshack","Alexander Loebel","Ariel Shamir"],"published":"2026-10-20T10:52:55Z","updated":"2026-10-20T10:52:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28007v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28007v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28007v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.28004v1","title":"Differentiable Task-Driven Coded Aperture Camera Design with 3D Gaussian Scene Representations","abstract":"Camera design heavily influences perception task performance, yet cameras are typically designed by domain experts through costly experimentation, in isolation from the tasks they serve. Existing joint-design methods either rely on differentiable simulation limited to pre-captured 2D images, restricting them to optics or image-processing pipeline design, or use non-differentiable camera simulators optimised with derivative-free or reinforcement-learning methods that scale poorly with the number of design parameters. We present a differentiable framework for jointly designing complex camera systems and downstream perception tasks. We use it to design two coded-aperture depth-from-defocus cameras, a monocular and a stereo configuration, by jointly optimising the coded aperture mask, camera parameters, and a depth estimation network end-to-end. Our framework represents scenes with 3D Gaussian Splatting reconstructed from real-world captures, providing a differentiable image formation process for gradient-based optimisation over design spaces intractable for prior differentiable approaches. Results demonstrate that our differentiable method improves over existing coded aperture design and non-differentiable camera design baselines, offering a step forward in automated and principled camera design for autonomous systems.","authors":["Chengyang Yan","Donald G. Dansereau"],"published":"2026-10-20T10:48:08Z","updated":"2026-10-20T10:48:08Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28004v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.28004v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28004v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27999v1","title":"Matrix-Game 4.0: Harnessing Video Models for Real-Time Playable Game Worlds","abstract":"Moving from controllable videos to genuinely playable worlds requires persistent world state, executable game rules, and low-latency interaction. We outline a roadmap for the next generation of Matrix-Game that separates game execution from visual generation through an executable game harness, and introduce Matrix-Game 4.0 as the first system built on this design. A lightweight game engine maintains world state and executes interaction rules, a multimodal coding agent translates player ideas into game code and dynamic scene captions, and a renderer based on autoregressive video model transforms engine-rendered low-poly proxies into detailed visuals, conditioned on dynamic captions and memory references. This framework provides continuous guidance for visual generation and enables natural-language creation and modification of scenes and gameplay. Our renderer is trained on aligned low-poly and high-fidelity video pairs and distilled into a causal generator that generates in four-frame chunks with one or two denoising steps. Together with targeted inference optimizations, Matrix-Game 4.0 enables 480p, 60 FPS gameplay on a single NVIDIA RTX5090 with a key-press-to-display latency of 112ms. Single-step generation achieves 83FPS steady-state throughput with 18.2GiB of peak GPU memory usage. With fixed prompts, steady-state throughput also reaches 67FPS on an RTX5080 and 58FPS on an RTX5070Ti. We demonstrate customizable shooter and driving games with engine-enforced mechanics, and release code, model weights, and low-poly game assets to support research and creation of playable game worlds.","authors":["Jihai Zhang","Runjia Qian","Kaiqin Kong","Biao Jiang","Puyi Wang","Siru Chen","Tianle Li","Guanjie Chen","Junyi Wu","Chubin Chen","Yuanhang Li","Kaichen Huang","Haobo Zhang","Yangguang Li","Wei Yu","Guibin Chen","Yu Cheng","Yahui Zhou"],"published":"2026-10-20T10:41:38Z","updated":"2026-10-20T10:41:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27999v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27999v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27999v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27986v1","title":"Recast the Past: Stable Long Video Generation with Full Context Memory","abstract":"Long-horizon video generation requires retaining an ever-expanding history, but conditioning on its growing token sequence is costly. Existing methods restrict or compress historical context, potentially losing information needed for future generation. This work proposes ReCam, which groups local spatiotemporal blocks of historical latents into vectors and projects them directly into the high-dimensional hidden space of a Diffusion Transformer. The model learns to use the resulting memory tokens through native self-attention, reducing token count while retaining block-level access to the full history. To mitigate temporal drift during autoregressive rollout, we further introduce dual-pointer denoising with separate noise schedules for recent context and target frames. Among the evaluated methods, ReCam achieves the highest VBench-Long overall score of 80.1 and competitive static-scene revisitation performance on R2M-Bench. It also leads on all four consistency metrics for off-screen subject reappearance, including clothing consistency of 4.09 versus 3.03 for the best baseline.","authors":["Yukuo Ma","Cong Liu","Junke Wang","Junqi Liu","Haibin Huang","Zuxuan Wu","Xuelong Li"],"published":"2026-10-20T10:27:07Z","updated":"2026-10-20T10:27:07Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27986v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27986v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27986v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27982v1","title":"Beyond the Frame: Redundancy, Not Trajectory, Carries the Privacy Leak in Video Unlearning","abstract":"Class unlearning for video segmentation aims to ensure that a removed category cannot be reconstructed from a deployed model's outputs. Current leakage evaluation algorithms inspect one frame at a time, and we show that this understates leakage by a wide margin: an attacker that averages a model's output field over a two-second clip recovers the forgotten class from every method we evaluate on VSPW and most on Virtual KITTI~2, including models whose per-frame predictions are visually indistinguishable from a retrain oracle (a model trained from scratch without the forgotten class). We attribute the recoverable signal to cross-frame redundancy rather than object trajectory, establish this with a time-shuffled control that leaves leakage intact, and introduce PACE, an unlearning method that suppresses redundancy directly using an exactly permutation-invariant pooled adversarial discriminator and a two-sided temporal consistency objective. Across VSPW and Virtual KITTI~2, PACE lowers pooled attacker AP to $18.79$ (\\texttt{person}) and $12.10$ (\\texttt{car}) on VSPW and $9.11$ (\\texttt{car}) on Virtual KITTI~2, achieving on the latter the strongest suppression among verdict-safe methods retaining usable utility, while retaining $19.8$ mIoU on VSPW \\texttt{car} against at most $8.3$ for any baseline achieving comparable erasure.","authors":["Kavyansh Tyagi","Paolo Russo"],"published":"2026-10-20T10:24:33Z","updated":"2026-10-20T10:24:33Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27982v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27982v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27982v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27974v1","title":"Media Intelligence Platform: Multimodal Detection of Synthetic and Manipulated Content","abstract":"The rapid progress of generative artificial intelligence has led to a significant increase in manipulated multimedia content, including synthetic faces, forged videos, AI-generated images, fake audio, and locally edited images. Existing detection approaches are typically designed for a single manipulation type, limiting their applicability in real-world scenarios involving heterogeneous media sources. In this work, we present the Media Intelligence Platform (MIP), a unified web-based framework for multimodal media forensics integrating specialized modules for fake video detection, face morphing detection, AI-generated image detection, audio deepfake detection, localized image manipulation detection, and text analysis. Its modular architecture enables flexible integration of heterogeneous models and supports future extensions for emerging manipulation techniques. Experimental results on representative benchmark datasets demonstrate reliable detection performance across multiple media modalities while providing a practical and user-friendly interface for forensic investigations. The platform highlights the potential of unified multimodal systems for comprehensive deepfake detection in real-world environments, and is accompanied by a public demonstrator released with this workshop submission to support reproducibility and community evaluation.","authors":["David Fischinger","Marcel Hasenbalg","Silvia Poletti","Davide Antonutti","Mina Schütz","Lam Pham","Justin Ilyes","Péter Rjabcsenkó","Simon Freitter","Martin Boyer"],"published":"2026-10-20T10:18:49Z","updated":"2026-10-20T10:18:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27974v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27974v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27974v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27960v1","title":"Efficient SAM3 Adaptation for Multi-Class Semantic Segmentation via Dense Competitive Representations","abstract":"The Segment Anything Model 3 (SAM3) has achieved significant progress in Promptable Concept Segmentation (PCS) by processing short noun phrases to generate segmentation masks with unique instance identifiers. While SAM3 excels at single-concept segmentation, it falls short in complex, real-world environments where multiple concepts must be segmented simultaneously. In particular, SAM3 typically fails to simultaneously process concepts with semantic overlap, leading to semantic misclassifications and redundant mask predictions. Moreover, as the number of concepts increases, the independent inference paradigm introduces prohibitive inference latency. To address these limitations, we propose the Dense Competitive Representations SAM (DCR-SAM) framework. By decoupling cross-modal interactions, the proposed method alleviates the inference latency. To resolve semantic misclassifications, DCR-SAM dynamically aligns textual concepts with visual representations and incorporates a dense competition head to enforce explicit inter-class competition. Furthermore, a learnable background token is applied to absorb non-target objects dynamically. These mechanisms effectively suppress overlapping predictions and generate high-fidelity masks. Extensive experimental evaluations across multiple benchmarks demonstrate that the proposed framework: (1) achieves a 2.3% $\\sim$ 10.7% performance improvement over state-of-the-art methods, (2) delivers a 33$\\times$ inference speedup compared to the native SAM3, and (3) exhibits strong robustness across diverse scenarios.","authors":["Wenbin Liao","Hao Zhu","Yike Ma","Hao Jiang","Feng Dai"],"published":"2026-10-20T10:06:19Z","updated":"2026-10-20T10:06:19Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27960v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27960v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27960v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27958v1","title":"From Proper Scoring Rules and Edge Biases to Mumford-Shah Models: Robust Unsupervised Hyperspectral Image Segmentation","abstract":"Hyperspectral images provide a rich representation of the underlying spectrum for each pixel, allowing for a pixel-wise classification/segmentation into different classes. As the availability of labeled hyperspectral images is limited and the acquisition of labeled training data is time-consuming, unsupervised segmentation methods are important in hyperspectral image segmentation. Spectral variability, mixed pixels, noise as well as high correlation between neighboring spectral bands make this task challenging. We present a feature-agnostic, unsupervised hyperspectral segmentation framework based on the piecewise-constant Mumford-Shah functional. Our framework turns a strictly proper scoring rule and a family of parametric probability distributions into an indicator function for the Mumford-Shah functional, and we naturally generalize some previous Mumford-Shah based models for image segmentation this way. Our focus will be on heavy-tailed elliptical distributions, the negative log-likelihood score and the score based on the beta-divergence to deal with the heavy outliers in hyperspectral imaging. Using the interplay between the calculus of variations and statistics, we also propose a robustness step to estimate the statistical parameters of these heavy-tailed elliptical distributions. In addition, an edge bias, which contains prior boundary information, is incorporated into both the data fidelity term and the perimeter term of the Mumford-Shah functional. Numerical experiments show that, to the best of our knowledge, our method achieves the highest overall accuracy among fully unsupervised methods on three of the four standard benchmarks (Indian Pines, Kennedy Space Center, Pavia University and Salinas) evaluated on the full scenes. It also improves upon our previous Mumford-Shah based model.","authors":["Fabian Conrad","Benjamin Berkels"],"published":"2026-10-20T10:04:28Z","updated":"2026-10-20T10:04:28Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27958v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27958v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27958v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27947v1","title":"Edit in Sync: Unified End-to-End Audio-Visual Editing for Human-Centric Videos","abstract":"Human-centric audio-visual editing requires precise alignment of lip movements, gestures, and expressions with speech. Existing methods process inputs through independent video and audio pipelines, preventing cross-modal interaction between visual editing and audio dubbing. Many of these pipelines further rely on mask-based inpainting. As a result, they suffer from poor lip-audio synchronization, cumulative pipeline errors, and an inherent mask-size dilemma. We present AVE, the first unified end-to-end framework that jointly performs reference-guided person swapping, insertion, and deletion across both audio and video modalities without requiring spatial masks. To overcome data scarcity, we design a synthesis pipeline and introduce AVE-Dataset, the first audio-visual editing dataset to provide 100K pairs with appearance and voice references. By combining Synthetic-to-Real reconstruction with preliminary audio warm-up, our framework transcends synthetic pipeline limits and mitigates audio-video token imbalance. AVE outperforms modality-separate audio-visual editing pipelines in editing realism, temporal consistency, and audio-visual alignment.","authors":["Ziye Yuan","Minghong Cai","Xu He","Ruchang Yao","Xiao Fu","Kaixin Ding","Ming Zhang","Xiangyu Du","Tianpeng Lan","Da Xie","Xiangyu Yue"],"published":"2026-10-20T09:53:32Z","updated":"2026-10-20T09:53:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27947v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27947v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27947v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27946v1","title":"Decoupling Action from Egocentric Observation for World Simulation","abstract":"Egocentric action transfer aims to decouple the semantic action from egocentric observations and reproduce them in new visual contexts, enabling scalable world simulation, embodied policy learning, and robotic data generation. Existing approaches to transferring actions rely on condition-guided video generation, which converts the observation video into explicit geometric controls (e.g., hand poses or meshes) to drive synthesis. However, these methods introduce geometric estimation errors, making it difficult to preserve physically plausible interactions when transferred to new scenes. Alternatively, motion transfer methods directly extract motion patterns from the observation video, yet motion-level features alone cannot encode rich contact dynamics, frequently leading to severe hand structural collapse and implausible contact layout. To address both limitations, we present EgoACT, a test-time framework for egocentric action transfer that operates directly on the denoising process of video diffusion models without requiring additional geometric estimators. EgoACT uses Velocity-guided Structure Anchoring to stabilize reference-consistent hand-object structure in the early denoising stage, and Sparse Correspondence Calibration to refine reliable local correspondences in the mid-to-late denoising stages. Together, these two components preserve transferable action semantics while improving temporal coherence and interaction realism. We further establish EgoActionBench, a benchmark for evaluating action preservation, visual quality, and hand-object plausibility across diverse egocentric manipulation scenarios. Experiments show that EgoACT generates more coherent and physically plausible interaction videos than strong baselines.","authors":["Yue Ma","Pengjie Song","Xinyu Wang","Yi He","Zeqian Long","Fangneng Zhan","Kaichen Zhou","Hongyu Liu","Hongfa Wang","Peihao Li","Haoyang Huang","Nan Duan","Qifeng Chen"],"published":"2026-10-20T09:52:46Z","updated":"2026-10-20T09:52:46Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27946v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27946v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27946v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27944v1","title":"In PRAISE of Attributes: Grounding Retrieval Geometry with Query-Conditioned Subspaces for Person Re-Identification","abstract":"Person re-identification (ReID) ranks an image gallery using either an image query or a textual query. In image-to-image (I2I) ReID, viewpoint, illumination, occlusion, and background changes can make distractors appear more similar than the true match. In text-to-image (T2I) ReID, incomplete or ambiguous descriptions may omit key visual evidence. We study this problem for vision-language model (VLM) based ReID, where attributes such as clothing color and carried objects provide useful fine-grained matching cues. However, directly using attribute-prompt similarities as retrieval scores is unreliable because templated prompts can be biased, correlated, and poorly calibrated. We therefore propose \\textbf{PR}ompt-guided \\textbf{A}ttribute-based \\textbf{I}nference-time \\textbf{S}imilarity \\textbf{E}nhancement (\\texttt{PRAISE}), a query-side test-time adaptation framework for VLM-based ReID. Instead of treating prompts as scores, \\texttt{PRAISE} uses a transferable attribute-prompt bank to construct an \\emph{Attribute-Conditioned Subspace} (ACS): a query-dependent low-rank subspace induced by the most compatible attribute prompts. ACS provides a controlled attribute-conditioned geometry for reshaping query--gallery affinities and guiding lightweight inference-time updates without identity or attribute labels. Experiments across multiple I2I- and T2I-ReID benchmarks show that \\texttt{PRAISE} consistently improves VLM retrieval backbones and outperforms both generic and ReID-specific TTA baselines.","authors":["Rajarshi Bhattacharya","Aryan Shukla","Shakeeb Murtaza","Christian Desrosiers","Jose Dolz","Maguelonne Heritier","Eric Granger"],"published":"2026-10-20T09:52:06Z","updated":"2026-10-20T09:52:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27944v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27944v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27944v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27914v1","title":"World Models with Configurable Perception","abstract":"Real-world observations are noisy and often contain excessive task-irrelevant information. Effective world modeling and planning need to abstract observations into world state representations while filtering out irrelevant information. However, encoders in existing world models are typically non-adaptive, leading to state representations that are easily distracted by task-irrelevant information. As a result, the model may not selectively filter observations to cater to different task demands. To address this problem, we introduce ConfigWM, which realizes configurable perception through task-conditioned state extraction. Given a language description of the task at hand, ConfigWM uses it as a configuration signal to steer how visual embeddings are mapped into a task-specific state space, selectively preserving information relevant to the current task while suppressing task-irrelevant variations. Action-conditioned dynamics are then learned in this task-specific state space for planning. We evaluate ConfigWM in environments with increasing levels of distraction. While existing methods exhibit substantial declines in planning success rate as the environments become increasingly noisy, ConfigWM remains comparatively stable and outperforms the strongest competing method by 22.3% on average across diverse environments. Consistent gains in physical-quantity probing (+0.365 Pearson r) and distracted-to-clean retrieval (+75.5%) further indicate that ConfigWM learns states that preserve task-relevant information while remaining robust to distractors, supporting reliable world modeling and planning in noisy worlds. Project Page: https://configwm.github.io","authors":["Yijun Shen","Delong Chen","Xianming Hu","Kai Zhang","Pascale Fung"],"published":"2026-10-20T09:26:32Z","updated":"2026-10-20T09:26:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27914v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27914v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27914v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27904v1","title":"Staged Continual Adaptation of Multimodal Foundation Models for Japanese Financial Documents","abstract":"Staged post-training of multimodal foundation models chains vision-language alignment, reasoning distillation, and domain tuning, yet how each phase trades capabilities against the others remains under-characterised. Tracking an 8.4B-parameter model from an un-fine-tuned baseline through three training phases on Japanese financial documents (evaluated on five text-input benchmarks), we find the phases interact non-additively: math-only distillation overwrites the financial knowledge acquired earlier even as procedural math skill survives, and the domain phase repairs more than it installs. A skip-phase ablation is best for domain knowledge but forfeits numerical-reasoning gains, so per-phase contributions are not decomposable and the best checkpoint is task-dependent, with implications for checkpoint retention and scheduling rather than added stages.","authors":["Genshin Kakimoto","Atsushi Yanagisawa"],"published":"2026-10-20T09:19:36Z","updated":"2026-10-20T09:19:36Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27904v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27904v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27904v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27894v1","title":"Testing Associative Creativity in Humans and Multimodal LLMs with Difficult-to-Name Visual Stimuli","abstract":"We investigate associative creativity in descriptions of abstract visual stimuli by humans and multimodal large language models (MLLMs). While conceptual association is central to human communication, it remains unclear how humans and models adapt when visual stimuli become increasingly difficult to map onto familiar semantic concepts. We evaluate several state-of-the-art MLLMs and human participants along two dimensions of creativity, originality and effectiveness, using two datasets with controlled manipulations of difficulty. SilCC varies naming difficulty through progressively more complex combinations of abstract shapes, and PixCC varies difficulty through increasing levels of random visual noise. Results show that humans produce more diverse associative descriptions than MLLMs, including greater semantic distance, higher entropy, and more novel expressions relative to reference lexica. As difficulty increases, effectiveness declines in SilCC, and humans increasingly shift from holistic associations to descriptions of low-level visual properties in PixCC.","authors":["Manar Ali","Lisa Gottschalk","Simeon Junker","Lisa Hudalla","Alon Fishman","Joana Cholin","Stefan Kopp","Hendrik Buschmeier","Sina Zarrieß"],"published":"2026-10-20T09:13:16Z","updated":"2026-10-20T09:13:16Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27894v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27894v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27894v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27890v1","title":"EndoWorld-JEPA: Motion-Guided Global-Local Latent World Modeling for Endoscopic Video Representation Learning","abstract":"Endoscopic video contains state transitions induced by camera displacement, deformable tissue, and instrument interaction, yet existing pre-training methods treat dynamics implicitly. Inspired by action-conditioned joint-embedding predictive world models and by the observation that endoscopic optical flow contains both shared image-plane displacement and localized residual motion, we reformulate passive endoscopic video pre-training as causal motion-guided latent-state prediction. We present EndoWorld-JEPA, to our knowledge the first latent world-model pre-training framework designed for general-purpose endoscopic video representation learning. In place of recorded actions, calibrated poses, or robot kinematics, the model conditions prediction of the next latent state on motion observed up to the current state. Its Optical Motion-guided module separates dominant displacement from residual motion and converts the resulting evidence into an ordered temporal motion condition and spatial local-motion weights. Complementary global and motion-local predictors use these outputs: the temporal condition modulates both latent transitions, whereas the local weights define an auxiliary motion-local state that extends prediction from scene-level evolution to spatially selective dynamics. Patch-wise motion weighting emphasizes localized change while retaining anatomical context, and past-motion-window dropout randomly withholds the complete motion condition to reduce dependence on unreliable motion. This global--local formulation learns predictable visual--motion dynamics in representation space without reconstructing stochastic appearance artifacts. Evaluation shows that a common pre-trained checkpoint achieves strong transfer across four downstream benchmarks, supporting motion-guided global--local latent prediction as an effective pre-training paradigm for endoscopic video.","authors":["Jie Wang","Shilong Yang","Tao Zhou","Juan Yu","Rong Qu","Jianfeng Ren","Zhicheng Zhang","Linlin Shen"],"published":"2026-10-20T09:11:49Z","updated":"2026-10-20T09:11:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27890v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27890v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27890v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27882v1","title":"S$^3$E-Det: A Strict Spatial-Spectral Equivariant Detector for Multispectral Object Detection","abstract":"Multispectral object detection requires detectors to exploit material-dependent spectral responses while maintaining consistent localization under geometric variations. Existing MOD detectors learn discriminative spectral representations, yet lack a unified mechanism that preserves spectral order, adapts spectral-context fusion across pyramid levels, and regulates geometric responses under rotation. To address these limitations, we propose S$^3$E-Det, a strict spatial-spectral equivariant detector that couples ordered spectral modeling with transformation-consistent prediction. Spectral Order-aware Sampling Calibration (SOSC) imposes local order constraints on input-derived spectral features through first-order variation, while Common-Differential Equivariant Fusion (CDEF) adapts spectral-context aggregation across pyramid levels. A compact Geometric Alignment Head (GAH) reconciles invariant and equivariant output properties within a unified prediction stage. Across MODA, HOD3K, and DroneVehicle, S$^3$E-Det combines high detection accuracy with a compact parameter footprint and reduced rotation-induced variation. Code is available at https://github.com/Riiluo/S3E-Det.","authors":["Peng Zhang","Tingfa Xu","Jinrui Sui","Yuyang Tian","Jianan Li"],"published":"2026-10-20T09:06:01Z","updated":"2026-10-20T09:06:01Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27882v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27882v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27882v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27877v1","title":"Gaze2HOI: Gaze-Aware Intention Disambiguation for Egocentric Hand-Object Interaction Forecasting","abstract":"Anticipating how users will interact with their surroundings is essential for proactive systems that provide timely assistance in AR, VR, and embodied applications. Prior work typically forecasts hand-object interactions directly from historical observations, leaving intention and contact geometry implicit. However, similar observation histories can precede semantically or geometrically distinct futures. Inspired by behavioral evidence that gaze can provide early cues for underlying intention, we propose Gaze2HOI, a gaze-aware framework that formulates hand-object interaction forecasting as a hierarchical problem comprising intention inference, contact grounding, and coupled hand-object motion generation. Specifically, Gaze2HOI first weights gaze cues by fixation strength and recency, and uses them to modulate the visual representations of a vision-language model (VLM) through Temporal Gaze Saliency Modulation (TGSM), thereby reducing semantic ambiguity in intention inference. Subsequently, an Anticipatory Contact Grounding (ACG) module maps these structured intentions into explicit contact, support, and placement fields, effectively mitigating geometric ambiguity. Driven by these explicit conditions, a diffusion-based model jointly generates the 6-DoF object trajectories and future articulated motion of both hands. To evaluate Gaze2HOI, we further introduce EgoAIM, a phase-aware benchmark that organizes egocentric hand-object interactions into four phases defined by evolving hand-object relations and provides fine-grained intention annotations. Extensive experiments demonstrate that Gaze2HOI achieves state-of-the-art performance in both intention inference and motion forecasting, even approaching oracle-level end-to-end performance in specific interaction phases. Project page: https://gaze2hoi.github.io/.","authors":["Chuanyang Ma","Chenye Wang","Yixuan Wang","Huiling Zhou","Xingqun Qi","Muyi Sun","Man Zhang","Caifeng Shan","Zhenan Sun"],"published":"2026-10-20T08:59:54Z","updated":"2026-10-20T08:59:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27877v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27877v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27877v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"},{"id":"2610.27851v1","title":"LaLa-World: Scaling Latent World Models with Natural Language Actions","abstract":"This paper shows that JEPA-style latent world models (LWMs) can learn across diverse domains and data sources by adopting the language-conditioned interface of video generation models. We establish this approach as an effective and efficient basis for planning with imagination and downstream policy fine-tuning. To this end, we introduce LaLa-World, a Language-conditioned Latent World model that learns from simulation and robot trajectories together with broader image and video data. Two designs make this work. First, we describe both numerical actions and visual changes in language, enabling joint training of a single future predictor across heterogeneous datasets. Second, we build the predictor on a language model, allowing it to interpret language conditions while predicting future latent states. The resulting predictor achieves higher average MPC planning performance than existing LWMs trained separately for each domain or task. Adding image and video data further improves average planning performance and robustness to action phrasing. In a matched comparison with an RAEv2-based video generation model baseline, LaLa-World reaches comparable planning performance with $6\\times$ less training cost and $13.9\\times$ faster planning. Beyond planning, the pretrained LaLa-World serves as an initialization for LaLa-Policy, which jointly predicts actions and future visual representations. LaLa-Policy performs competitively with Cosmos Policy on LIBERO and RoboCasa, with up to $5.5\\times$ faster inference.","authors":["Junha Song","Geonmo Gu","Jeongeun Park","Heejeong Nam","Lucas Maes","Byeongho Heo","Dongyoon Han","Sangdoo Yun"],"published":"2026-10-20T08:32:14Z","updated":"2026-10-20T08:32:14Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27851v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.27851v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.27851v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-10-21T05:39:21+00:00"}],"tracked_paper_count":75,"storage_mode":"latest_overwrite"}};
